const modules = [{"id":"math-arith-real-numbers","subject":"math","category":"Arithmetic & Number Sense","title":"The Real Number System","competency":"Natural, whole, integer, rational, and irrational numbers — classifying numbers","estLesson":8,"estPractice":10,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Classifying numbers"},{"type":"p","text":"In this module, natural numbers are the counting numbers {1, 2, 3, …}. Some conventions include 0. The whole numbers include 0. The integers also include negative whole numbers. Rational numbers can be written as a/b with integers a and b and b ≠ 0. Irrational numbers, such as π and √2, cannot. Together the rational and irrational numbers form the real numbers."},{"type":"list","items":["<b>Natural:</b> 1, 2, 3, 47, 1000","<b>Whole:</b> 0, 1, 2, 3, 47","<b>Integer:</b> −12, −1, 0, 6, 47","<b>Rational:</b> −1/2, 0.75, 5, 0.333…","<b>Irrational:</b> √2, π, √7"]},{"type":"example","prompt":"Classify each number by every set it belongs to: −5, 0.75, √9, √2","work":"−5 → integer, rational, real (not natural or whole — it's negative)\n0.75 → rational, real (it's a terminating decimal, i.e. 3/4)\n√9 = 3 → natural, whole, integer, rational, real\n√2 ≈ 1.4142… → irrational, real only (non-terminating, non-repeating)","answer":"see work above"},{"type":"tip","text":"For a nonnegative integer n, √n is an integer if n is a perfect square and irrational otherwise. A square root of a fraction can be rational without being an integer. For example, √(1/4) = 1/2."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which set does the number −7 NOT belong to?","options":["Integers","Rational numbers","Whole numbers","Real numbers"],"answer":2},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which of the following is irrational?","options":["√16","0.333…","√7","−3/4"],"answer":2},{"id":"q3","type":"short","prompt":"Under the convention used in this lesson (natural numbers start at 1), is 0 a natural number? Answer yes or no.","accepted":["no"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Every integer is also a:","options":["Natural number","Whole number","Rational number","Irrational number"],"answer":2},{"id":"q5","type":"fillblank","prompt":"√25 simplifies to a whole number, so √25 is classified as a {blank} number (rational or irrational).","accepted":["rational"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which best describes π?","options":["A terminating decimal","A repeating decimal","A non-terminating, non-repeating decimal","A whole number"],"answer":2},{"id":"q7","type":"reflect","prompt":"Name one number that is rational but NOT an integer, and explain why it qualifies as rational."}]},{"id":"math-arith-operations","subject":"math","category":"Arithmetic & Number Sense","title":"Operations on Integers, Fractions & Decimals","competency":"Signed-number rules · adding, subtracting, multiplying, dividing fractions and decimals","estLesson":9,"estPractice":12,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Signed number rules"},{"type":"list","items":["Same signs multiply or divide to a <b>positive</b> result: (−) × (−) = +, (+) × (+) = +","Different signs multiply or divide to a <b>negative</b> result: (−) × (+) = −","Adding same signs: add the numbers, keep the sign","Adding different signs: subtract the smaller absolute value from the larger, keep the sign of the number with the larger absolute value"]},{"type":"example","prompt":"Evaluate: −8 + 5","work":"Different signs → subtract: 8 − 5 = 3\nKeep the sign of the larger absolute value (−8)","answer":"−3"},{"type":"example","prompt":"Evaluate: (−6) × (−3)","work":"Same signs → positive result\n6 × 3 = 18","answer":"18"},{"type":"h","text":"Fraction operations"},{"type":"p","text":"To add or subtract fractions, first convert to a common denominator. To multiply fractions, multiply straight across (numerator × numerator, denominator × denominator). To divide fractions, multiply by the reciprocal of the second fraction."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Evaluate: 2/3 + 1/4","work":"LCD of 3 and 4 is 12\n2/3 = 8/12, 1/4 = 3/12\n8/12 + 3/12 = 11/12","answer":"11/12"},{"type":"example","prompt":"Evaluate: 3/5 ÷ 2/7","work":"Multiply by the reciprocal of 2/7, which is 7/2\n3/5 × 7/2 = 21/10","answer":"21/10"},{"type":"h","text":"Decimal operations"},{"type":"p","text":"Align decimal points when adding or subtracting. When multiplying, count the total decimal places in both factors and place the decimal point that many places from the right in the answer. When dividing, move the decimal point in the divisor to make it a whole number, and move the decimal in the dividend the same number of places."},{"type":"tip","text":"Convert a fraction to a decimal (divide numerator by denominator) whenever it makes comparing or estimating faster under time pressure."},{"type":"tip","text":"Division requires a nonzero divisor. For addition with opposite signs and equal absolute values, the sum is zero."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"−12 + (−7) = ?","options":["−19","−5","5","19"],"answer":0},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"(−4) × 6 = ?","options":["−24","−2","2","24"],"answer":0},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"2/3 + 1/4 = {blank} (in lowest terms)","accepted":["11/12"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"3/5 ÷ 2/7 = ?","options":["6/35","21/10","10/21","35/6"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Compute: 4.75 − 1.6","accepted":["3.15"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"(−15) ÷ (−3) = ?","options":["−5","5","−45","45"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"fillblank","prompt":"0.6 × 0.3 = {blank}","accepted":["0.18"]},{"id":"q8","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which is greatest: −3, −8, 0, −1?","options":["−3","−8","0","−1"],"answer":2}]},{"id":"math-arith-gemdas","subject":"math","category":"Arithmetic & Number Sense","title":"Order of Operations (GEMDAS)","competency":"Grouping, exponents, multiplication/division, addition/subtraction — evaluating expressions correctly","estLesson":7,"estPractice":10,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"The GEMDAS rule"},{"type":"p","text":"GEMDAS tells you the order to evaluate an expression: Grouping symbols first, then Exponents, then Multiplication and Division (working left to right, since they share equal priority), then Addition and Subtraction (also left to right, equal priority)."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Evaluate: 6 + 2 × (5 − 3)²","work":"Grouping: (5 − 3) = 2\nExponent: 2² = 4\nMultiply: 2 × 4 = 8\nAdd: 6 + 8","answer":"14"},{"type":"tip","text":"A common trap: 8 ÷ 2 × 4 is NOT equal to 1. Multiplication and division have equal priority, so you work strictly left to right."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Evaluate: 8 ÷ 2 × 4","work":"Left to right: 8 ÷ 2 = 4\n4 × 4 = 16","answer":"16"}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Evaluate: 6 + 2 × (5 − 3)²","options":["14","22","64","28"],"answer":0},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Evaluate: 8 ÷ 2 × 4","options":["1","4","16","32"],"answer":2},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Evaluate: 3 + 4 × 2 = {blank}","accepted":["11"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Evaluate: (3 + 5)² ÷ 4","options":["16","14","32","4"],"answer":0},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Evaluate: 20 − (2 + 3) × 2","accepted":["10"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which operation should be performed FIRST in 5 × (2 + 3)² − 1?","options":["Multiplication","Exponent","Addition inside the parentheses","Subtraction"],"answer":2},{"id":"q7","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Evaluate: 10 − 2 × 3 + 4 = {blank}","accepted":["8"]}]},{"id":"math-arith-factors-multiples","subject":"math","category":"Arithmetic & Number Sense","title":"Factors, Multiples & Divisibility Rules","competency":"Divisibility rules for 2–10 · identifying factors and multiples","estLesson":8,"estPractice":10,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Divisibility rules"},{"type":"list","items":["<b>2</b> — last digit is even","<b>3</b> — digit sum is divisible by 3","<b>4</b> — last two digits form a number divisible by 4","<b>5</b> — ends in 0 or 5","<b>6</b> — divisible by both 2 and 3","<b>9</b> — digit sum is divisible by 9","<b>10</b> — ends in 0"]},{"type":"example","prompt":"Is 4,536 divisible by 9?","work":"Digit sum: 4 + 5 + 3 + 6 = 18\n18 is divisible by 9","answer":"Yes"},{"type":"h","text":"Factors vs. multiples"},{"type":"p","text":"A factor of a number divides it exactly, with no remainder. A multiple of a number is that number multiplied by an integer. Every number is both a factor and a multiple of itself."},{"type":"example","prompt":"List all factors of 24","work":"Pair up numbers that multiply to 24:\n1×24, 2×12, 3×8, 4×6","answer":"1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 12, 24"},{"type":"tip","text":"For divisibility by 4, you only ever need to check the last two digits — ignore the rest of the number entirely."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which number is divisible by 9?","options":["452","522","548","563"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which of the following is a factor of 36?","options":["8","9","10","14"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"The first four positive multiples of 7 are 7, 14, 21, and {blank}.","accepted":["28"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Is 3,150 divisible by 6?","options":["Yes, because it's divisible by both 2 and 3","No, because it's odd","Yes, because it ends in 0","No, because its digit sum is not divisible by 3"],"answer":0},{"id":"q5","type":"reflect","prompt":"List all the positive factors of 18."},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which number is divisible by 4?","options":["1,234","5,316","7,221","8,002"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which number is a multiple of 12?","options":["30","42","54","60"],"answer":3}]},{"id":"math-arith-gcf-lcm","subject":"math","category":"Arithmetic & Number Sense","title":"Prime Factorization, GCF & LCM","competency":"Prime factorization with factor trees · finding the GCF and LCM of two numbers","estLesson":9,"estPractice":10,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Prime factorization"},{"type":"p","text":"The prime factorization of a number writes it as a product of prime numbers only, usually found with a factor tree."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Find the prime factorization of 60","work":"60 = 2 × 30\n= 2 × 2 × 15\n= 2 × 2 × 3 × 5","answer":"2² × 3 × 5"},{"type":"h","text":"Greatest Common Factor (GCF)"},{"type":"p","text":"The GCF of two numbers is the product of the lowest power of each prime factor they have in common."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Find the GCF of 24 and 36","work":"24 = 2³ × 3\n36 = 2² × 3²\nCommon primes at lowest power: 2² × 3 = 4 × 3","answer":"12"},{"type":"h","text":"Least Common Multiple (LCM)"},{"type":"p","text":"The LCM is the product of the highest power of every prime factor that appears in either number. A useful shortcut once you know the GCF: LCM(a, b) = (a × b) ÷ GCF(a, b)."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Find the LCM of 24 and 36","work":"Using the shortcut: LCM = (24 × 36) ÷ GCF(24, 36)\n= 864 ÷ 12","answer":"72"},{"type":"tip","text":"If you already found the GCF, get the LCM instantly with LCM = (a × b) ÷ GCF instead of building a whole new factor tree."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the prime factorization of 60?","options":["2 × 3 × 10","2² × 3 × 5","2 × 3² × 5","2² × 15"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"fillblank","prompt":"The GCF of 24 and 36 is {blank}.","accepted":["12"]},{"id":"q3","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the LCM of 24 and 36?","options":["36","72","144","864"],"answer":1},{"id":"q4","type":"short","prompt":"Find the GCF of 18 and 30.","accepted":["6"]},{"id":"q5","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which pair of numbers has a GCF of 1 (they are “relatively prime”)?","options":["8 and 12","9 and 16","10 and 15","14 and 21"],"answer":1},{"id":"q6","type":"fillblank","prompt":"The LCM of 8 and 12 is {blank}.","accepted":["24"]},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"If GCF(a, b) = 6 and a × b = 216, what is LCM(a, b)?","options":["6","36","42","216"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"math-arith-ratio-proportion","subject":"math","category":"Arithmetic & Number Sense","title":"Ratio, Proportion & Rate","competency":"Writing and simplifying ratios · solving proportions · rate and unit-rate problems","estLesson":9,"estPractice":12,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Ratios and simplifying them"},{"type":"p","text":"A ratio compares two quantities. Simplify a ratio the same way you'd simplify a fraction — divide both terms by their GCF."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Simplify the ratio 18:24","work":"GCF(18, 24) = 6\n18 ÷ 6 = 3, 24 ÷ 6 = 4","answer":"3:4"},{"type":"h","text":"Solving proportions"},{"type":"p","text":"A proportion states that two ratios are equal. Solve for an unknown by cross-multiplying: a/b = c/d means a × d = b × c."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Solve for x: 4/9 = x/45","work":"4 × 45 = 9 × x\n180 = 9x\nx = 180 ÷ 9","answer":"x = 20"},{"type":"h","text":"Rate and unit rate"},{"type":"p","text":"A rate compares two quantities with different units, like km per hour. A unit rate has a denominator of 1 — it tells you “how much per one.”"},{"type":"example","prompt":"A car travels 180 km in 3 hours. Find its unit rate (speed).","work":"180 ÷ 3","answer":"60 km/h"},{"type":"tip","text":"When a ratio problem gives a total and asks for one part, split the total into (sum of the ratio terms) equal shares first, then multiply by the number of shares you need."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"fillblank","prompt":"The ratio 18:24 simplified to lowest terms is {blank}.","accepted":["3:4"]},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Solve for x: 4/9 = x/45","options":["15","18","20","25"],"answer":2},{"id":"q3","type":"short","prompt":"A car travels 180 km in 3 hours at a constant speed. What is its speed in km/h?","accepted":["60","60 km/h"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"A recipe uses flour and sugar in the ratio 5:2. If 20 cups of flour are used, how many cups of sugar are needed?","options":["6","8","10","40"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"fillblank","prompt":"₱2,500 is divided between two siblings in the ratio 3:2. The sibling with the larger share gets ₱{blank}.","accepted":["1,500","1500"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which ratio is equivalent to 6:9?","options":["2:3","4:9","9:6","3:9"],"answer":0},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"A printer produces 45 pages in 5 minutes. At this rate, how many pages can it print in 12 minutes?","options":["81","90","108","135"],"answer":2}]},{"id":"math-arith-percent","subject":"math","category":"Arithmetic & Number Sense","title":"Percentage: Change, Discount, Interest & Mixture","competency":"Percent change · discount and markup · simple interest · mixture problems","estLesson":10,"estPractice":12,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Percent change"},{"type":"p","text":"Percent change = (New − Original) ÷ Original × 100%. A positive result is an increase; a negative result is a decrease. Always divide by the ORIGINAL value, never the new one."},{"type":"example","prompt":"A population grows from 4,000 to 4,600. Find the percent increase.","work":"(4,600 − 4,000) ÷ 4,000\n= 600 ÷ 4,000 = 0.15","answer":"15% increase"},{"type":"h","text":"Discount and simple interest"},{"type":"p","text":"Sale price = Original × (1 − discount rate). Simple interest uses I = P × r × t, where P is the principal, r is the annual rate (as a decimal), and t is the time in years."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Find the simple interest on ₱10,000 at 5% per year for 3 years.","work":"I = 10,000 × 0.05 × 3","answer":"₱1,500"},{"type":"h","text":"Mixture problems"},{"type":"p","text":"Set up mixture problems as: (amount₁ × concentration₁) + (amount₂ × concentration₂) = (total amount × final concentration)."},{"type":"example","prompt":"How many liters of a 40% acid solution must be mixed with a 10% acid solution to make 30 liters of a 20% solution?","work":"Let x = liters of the 40% solution\n0.4x + 0.1(30 − x) = 0.2(30)\n0.4x + 3 − 0.1x = 6\n0.3x = 3 → x = 10","answer":"10 liters of the 40% solution (and 20 liters of the 10% solution)"},{"type":"tip","text":"For percent-change questions, always divide by the ORIGINAL (starting) value, never the new value."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"A shirt's price drops from ₱800 to ₱680. What is the percent decrease?","options":["10%","15%","20%","25%"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"fillblank","prompt":"The simple interest on ₱15,000 at 4% per year for 2 years is ₱{blank}.","accepted":["1,200","1200"]},{"id":"q3","type":"mcq","prompt":"An item originally priced at ₱950 is on sale for 30% off. What is the sale price?","options":["₱285","₱620","₱665","₱950"],"answer":2},{"id":"q4","type":"short","prompt":"A population grows from 4,000 to 4,600. What is the percent increase?","accepted":["15%","15"]},{"id":"q5","type":"mcq","prompt":"₱20,000 is invested at 6% simple interest per year. How much total interest is earned after 5 years?","options":["₱1,200","₱6,000","₱12,000","₱20,000"],"answer":1},{"id":"q6","type":"fillblank","prompt":"A price increases from ₱500 to ₱575. This is a {blank}% increase.","accepted":["15"]},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"How much water by mass must be added to 10 kg of a 30% salt solution by mass to make a 20% solution?","options":["2 kg","3 kg","5 kg","10 kg"],"answer":2}]},{"id":"math-arith-parity-primes","subject":"math","category":"Arithmetic & Number Sense","title":"Odd/Even & Prime/Composite Numbers","competency":"Parity rules for operations · identifying prime and composite numbers","estLesson":7,"estPractice":9,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Odd and even rules"},{"type":"list","items":["even ± even = even, odd ± odd = even, even ± odd = odd","even × anything = even","odd × odd = odd"]},{"type":"example","prompt":"Is the sum of three odd numbers odd or even?","work":"odd + odd = even\neven + odd = odd","answer":"Odd"},{"type":"h","text":"Prime and composite numbers"},{"type":"p","text":"A prime number has exactly two factors: 1 and itself. A composite number has more than two factors. The number 1 is neither prime nor composite, and 2 is the only even prime number."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Is 51 prime or composite?","work":"51 = 3 × 17\nIt has factors beyond 1 and itself","answer":"Composite"},{"type":"tip","text":"To check if a number under 100 is prime, you only need to test divisibility by 2, 3, 5, and 7 — if none divide it evenly, it's prime."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which of the following is a prime number?","options":["21","27","29","33"],"answer":2},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"The sum of two odd numbers is always:","options":["Odd","Even","Prime","Negative"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"51 = 3 × 17, so 51 is classified as a {blank} number (prime or composite).","accepted":["composite"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which number is neither prime nor composite?","options":["0","1","2","3"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Name the only even prime number.","accepted":["2"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"The product of an even number and an odd number is always:","options":["Even","Odd","Prime","Zero"],"answer":0},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which of the following is composite?","options":["41","43","47","51"],"answer":3}]},{"id":"math-arith-scientific-notation","subject":"math","category":"Arithmetic & Number Sense","title":"Scientific Notation","competency":"Writing numbers in scientific notation · multiplying and dividing in scientific notation","estLesson":9,"estPractice":10,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Writing numbers in scientific notation"},{"type":"p","text":"A number in scientific notation is written as a × 10ⁿ, where 1 ≤ a < 10. For large numbers, move the decimal point left and count the moves as a positive exponent. For small numbers, move it right and use a negative exponent."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Write 45,000 in scientific notation","work":"Move the decimal 4 places left: 4.5","answer":"4.5 × 10⁴"},{"type":"example","prompt":"Write 0.00032 in scientific notation","work":"Move the decimal 4 places right: 3.2","answer":"3.2 × 10⁻⁴"},{"type":"h","text":"Multiplying and dividing"},{"type":"p","text":"Multiply or divide the leading numbers normally, then add the exponents (for multiplication) or subtract them (for division)."},{"type":"example","prompt":"(3 × 10⁴) × (2 × 10³)","work":"3 × 2 = 6\nExponents: 4 + 3 = 7","answer":"6 × 10⁷"},{"type":"tip","text":"If multiplying the leading numbers gives 10 or more (like 4 × 4 = 16), shift one more power of ten: 16 × 10ⁿ = 1.6 × 10ⁿ⁺¹."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Write 6,200,000 in scientific notation.","options":["6.2 × 10⁵","6.2 × 10⁶","62 × 10⁵","6.2 × 10⁷"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Write 0.0007 in scientific notation.","options":["7 × 10³","7 × 10⁻³","7 × 10⁻⁴","7 × 10⁴"],"answer":2},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"3.4 × 10³ written in standard form is {blank}.","accepted":["3400","3,400"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"(2 × 10⁵) × (3 × 10²) = ?","options":["5 × 10⁷","6 × 10⁷","6 × 10¹⁰","5 × 10¹⁰"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Compute (8 × 10⁶) ÷ (2 × 10²) and write the answer in scientific notation.","accepted":["4 × 10⁴","4×10^4","4x10^4","4 x 10^4"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is correctly written in scientific notation?","options":["45 × 10³","4.5 × 10⁴","0.45 × 10⁵","450 × 10²"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"fillblank","prompt":"(4 × 10³) × (5 × 10²) = {blank} × 10⁶ (leading coefficient only).","accepted":["2"]}]},{"id":"math-arith-estimation","subject":"math","category":"Arithmetic & Number Sense","title":"Estimation & Rounding","competency":"Rounding to a given place value · front-end and compatible-number estimation","estLesson":6,"estPractice":9,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Rounding to a place value"},{"type":"p","text":"Look at the digit right after the place you're rounding to: 5 or more rounds up, 4 or less stays the same (rounds down)."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Round 4,872 to the nearest hundred.","work":"The digit after the hundreds place is the tens digit: 7\n7 ≥ 5, so round up","answer":"4,900"},{"type":"h","text":"Estimating with compatible numbers"},{"type":"p","text":"Replace numbers with nearby values that are easy to compute mentally. This is useful for checking whether an exact answer is reasonable — or for quickly eliminating wrong multiple-choice options."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Estimate 812 × 19","work":"Round to compatible numbers: 800 × 20","answer":"≈ 16,000"},{"type":"tip","text":"On multiple-choice exams, estimating first often eliminates two or three answer choices immediately — before you do the exact calculation."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Round 4,872 to the nearest hundred.","options":["4,800","4,870","4,900","5,000"],"answer":2},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Round 0.4562 to the nearest hundredth.","options":["0.45","0.46","0.456","0.5"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Round 38,450 to the nearest thousand: {blank}","accepted":["38,000","38000"]},{"id":"q4","type":"short","prompt":"Estimate 812 × 19 using compatible numbers (round to the nearest hundred and ten).","accepted":["16,000","16000"]},{"id":"q5","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which is the best estimate of 5,987 + 3,102?","options":["8,000","9,000","10,000","11,000"],"answer":1},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Round 2.5 to the nearest whole number using standard rounding rules.","options":["2","3","2.5","0"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Round 149,900 to the nearest ten-thousand: {blank}","accepted":["150,000","150000"]}]},{"id":"math-alg-expressions","subject":"math","category":"Algebra","title":"Algebraic Expressions & Terms","competency":"Terms, coefficients, like terms — combining, simplifying, and evaluating expressions","estLesson":8,"estPractice":10,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Parts of an algebraic expression"},{"type":"p","text":"An expression is made of terms separated by + or − signs. Each term has a coefficient (its numerical factor) and, usually, a variable part. A term with no variable is a constant. Like terms have identical variable parts — the same variables raised to the same powers — and only like terms can be combined."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Identify the terms, coefficients, and constant in 5x² − 3x + 7","work":"Terms: 5x², −3x, 7\nCoefficients: 5 and −3\nConstant: 7","answer":"see work above"},{"type":"h","text":"Combining like terms"},{"type":"example","prompt":"Simplify: 4x + 7y − 2x + 3y","work":"Group like terms: (4x − 2x) + (7y + 3y)","answer":"2x + 10y"},{"type":"h","text":"Evaluating expressions"},{"type":"p","text":"Substitute the given values for each variable, then follow the order of operations."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Evaluate 3a² − 2b when a = 2, b = 5","work":"3(2)² − 2(5)\n= 3(4) − 10\n= 12 − 10","answer":"2"},{"type":"tip","text":"Distribute a negative sign to every term inside parentheses before combining like terms: −(2x − 5) = −2x + 5, not −2x − 5."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"In the expression 6x² − 4x + 9, what is the coefficient of x?","options":["6","−4","9","x"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which pair are like terms?","options":["3x² and 3x","5xy and −2xy","4a and 4b","7 and 7x"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Simplify: 6x + 5y − x + 2y = {blank}","accepted":["5x+7y","7y+5x"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Evaluate 4a² − 3b when a = 2, b = 1.","options":["9","13","16","19"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Simplify: 5(2x − 3) − 4x","accepted":["6x-15","6x - 15"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which is the constant term in −7y² + 3y − 8?","options":["−7","3","−8","y"],"answer":2},{"id":"q7","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Simplify: −(3x − 7) = {blank}","accepted":["-3x+7","-3x + 7","7-3x","7 - 3x"]}]},{"id":"math-alg-exponents","subject":"math","category":"Algebra","title":"Laws of Exponents","competency":"Product, quotient, power, zero, and negative exponent rules","estLesson":8,"estPractice":10,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Core exponent rules"},{"type":"list","items":["<b>Product rule:</b> aᵐ × aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ","<b>Quotient rule:</b> aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = aᵐ⁻ⁿ (a ≠ 0)","<b>Power rule:</b> (aᵐ)ⁿ = aᵐⁿ","<b>Power of a product:</b> (ab)ⁿ = aⁿbⁿ","<b>Zero exponent:</b> a⁰ = 1 (a ≠ 0)","<b>Negative exponent:</b> a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ"]},{"type":"example","prompt":"Simplify: x⁵ × x³","work":"Add the exponents: 5 + 3","answer":"x⁸"},{"type":"example","prompt":"Simplify: (2x³)⁴","work":"Apply the exponent to each factor:\n2⁴ × x^(3×4) = 16x¹²","answer":"16x¹²"},{"type":"example","prompt":"Simplify: x⁻³","work":"Negative exponent → reciprocal","answer":"1/x³"},{"type":"tip","text":"When dividing powers with the same base, subtract exponents — even if the result is negative or zero: x² ÷ x⁵ = x⁻³ = 1/x³."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"x⁴ × x⁶ = ?","options":["x¹⁰","x²⁴","x²","2x¹⁰"],"answer":0},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"(3x²)³ = ?","options":["9x⁵","27x⁶","9x⁶","27x⁵"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"x⁻⁵ written with a positive exponent is {blank}","accepted":["1/x^5","1/x⁵"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"(x⁴)² ÷ x³ = ?","options":["x⁵","x¹¹","x²","x"],"answer":0},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Simplify: y⁰ (y ≠ 0)","accepted":["1"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"(3a²b)³ = ?","options":["9a⁶b³","27a⁵b³","27a⁶b³","9a⁵b³"],"answer":2},{"id":"q7","type":"fillblank","prompt":"For x ≠ 0, x⁷ ÷ x⁷ = {blank}.","accepted":["1"]}]},{"id":"math-alg-factoring","subject":"math","category":"Algebra","title":"Special Products & Factoring","competency":"Difference of squares, perfect square trinomials, factoring trinomials and the GCF","estLesson":10,"estPractice":12,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Special products (multiplying patterns)"},{"type":"list","items":["<b>Difference of squares:</b> (a + b)(a − b) = a² − b²","<b>Perfect square trinomial:</b> (a + b)² = a² + 2ab + b², and (a − b)² = a² − 2ab + b²"]},{"type":"example","prompt":"Expand (2x + 3)²","work":"a = 2x, b = 3\n(2x)² + 2(2x)(3) + 3²","answer":"4x² + 12x + 9"},{"type":"h","text":"Factoring out the GCF"},{"type":"p","text":"Always check for a common factor in every term first, before trying anything else."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Factor 6x² + 9x","work":"GCF of 6x² and 9x is 3x","answer":"3x(2x + 3)"},{"type":"h","text":"Factoring trinomials (x² + bx + c)"},{"type":"p","text":"Find two numbers that multiply to c and add to b."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Factor x² + 7x + 12","work":"Need two numbers that multiply to 12 and add to 7: 3 and 4","answer":"(x + 3)(x + 4)"},{"type":"h","text":"Factoring a difference of squares"},{"type":"example","prompt":"Factor 25x² − 16","work":"25x² = (5x)², 16 = 4²","answer":"(5x + 4)(5x − 4)"},{"type":"tip","text":"Before trying any factoring technique, always pull out the GCF first — it makes every later step smaller and easier."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Expand (3x + 2)²","options":["9x² + 4","9x² + 12x + 4","9x² + 6x + 4","6x² + 12x + 4"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Factor 10x² + 15x","options":["5(2x² + 3x)","5x(2x + 3)","x(10x + 15)","5x(2x + 15)"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Factor x² + 9x + 20 = {blank}","accepted":["(x+4)(x+5)","(x+5)(x+4)"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Factor 49x² − 4","options":["(7x + 2)(7x − 2)","(7x − 2)²","(49x + 4)(x − 1)","(7x + 4)(7x − 1)"],"answer":0},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Expand (x − 5)²","accepted":["x^2-10x+25","x²-10x+25","x^2 - 10x + 25"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the first step in factoring 8x² + 20x?","options":["Apply difference of squares","Factor out the GCF (4x)","Use the quadratic formula","Complete the square"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Factor x² − 9","options":["(x − 3)(x − 3)","(x + 3)(x + 3)","(x + 3)(x − 3)","(x − 9)(x + 1)"],"answer":2}]},{"id":"math-alg-linear-eq-ineq","subject":"math","category":"Algebra","title":"Linear Equations & Inequalities","competency":"Solving one-variable linear equations and inequalities; graphing on a number line","estLesson":8,"estPractice":10,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Solving linear equations"},{"type":"example","prompt":"Solve: 2(x − 3) + 5 = 11","work":"Distribute: 2x − 6 + 5 = 11\n2x − 1 = 11\n2x = 12","answer":"x = 6"},{"type":"h","text":"Solving linear inequalities"},{"type":"p","text":"Solve exactly like an equation, but flip the inequality sign whenever you multiply or divide both sides by a negative number."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Solve: −3x + 4 > 13","work":"−3x > 9\nDivide by −3, flip the sign","answer":"x < −3"},{"type":"h","text":"Graphing on a number line"},{"type":"p","text":"Use an open circle for < or >, a closed circle for ≤ or ≥, and shade in the direction of the solution."},{"type":"tip","text":"Whenever you multiply or divide an inequality by a negative number, flip the inequality symbol — this is the single most common mistake on this topic."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Solve: 3(x − 2) + 4 = 13","options":["x=3","x=5","x=7","x=9"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Solve: −2x + 5 > 11","options":["x>-3","x<-3","x>3","x<3"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Solve for x: 5x − 8 = 27 → x = {blank}","accepted":["7"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which graph description matches x ≥ 4?","options":["Open circle at 4, shading right","Closed circle at 4, shading right","Closed circle at 4, shading left","Open circle at 4, shading left"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Solve: 4x + 9 ≤ 25","accepted":["x≤4","x<=4","x ≤ 4"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Solve: x/(−2) < 5","options":["x<-10","x>-10","x<10","x>10"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Solve: 7 − 2x = 1 → x = {blank}","accepted":["3"]}]},{"id":"math-alg-systems","subject":"math","category":"Algebra","title":"Systems of Linear Equations","competency":"Solving 2×2 systems by substitution and elimination; intro to 3-variable systems","estLesson":10,"estPractice":12,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Solving by substitution"},{"type":"p","text":"Solve one equation for one variable, then substitute that expression into the other equation."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Solve: y = 2x + 1 and 3x + y = 11","work":"Substitute: 3x + (2x + 1) = 11\n5x + 1 = 11\n5x = 10 → x = 2\ny = 2(2) + 1","answer":"(2, 5)"},{"type":"h","text":"Solving by elimination"},{"type":"p","text":"Add or subtract the equations (after scaling one if needed) so that one variable cancels out."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Solve: 2x + 3y = 12 and 2x − y = 4","work":"Subtract: (2x + 3y) − (2x − y) = 12 − 4\n4y = 8 → y = 2\nThen: 2x − 2 = 4 → x = 3","answer":"(3, 2)"},{"type":"h","text":"Three-variable systems (brief)"},{"type":"p","text":"Use one pair of equations to eliminate a variable, then a different pair to eliminate the same variable — this leaves a 2-variable system you already know how to solve. Back-substitute to find the third variable."},{"type":"tip","text":"Check your solution by plugging both values back into BOTH original equations — a valid solution must satisfy every equation in the system."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Solve the system: y = x + 4, 2x + y = 13. What is x?","options":["1","3","5","7"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Using the same system (y = x + 4, 2x + y = 13), what is y?","options":["3","5","7","9"],"answer":2},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Solve: 2x + 5y = 16 and 2x − y = 4. y = {blank}","accepted":["2"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the first useful step to solve a system by elimination when the x-coefficients are already opposites (e.g. 3x and −3x)?","options":["Multiply one equation by -1","Add the two equations together","Divide both equations by 3","Graph both lines"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Solve for x: 2x + 5y = 16 and 2x − y = 4.","accepted":["3"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"A system of two linear equations has no solution when the lines are:","options":["Intersecting","Parallel and distinct","The same line","Perpendicular"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"A system of two linear equations has infinitely many solutions when the two equations represent:","options":["Parallel distinct lines","Perpendicular lines","The same line","Intersecting lines at one point"],"answer":2}]},{"id":"math-alg-quadratics","subject":"math","category":"Algebra","title":"Quadratic Equations","competency":"Factoring, completing the square, the quadratic formula, and the discriminant","estLesson":12,"estPractice":14,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Solving by factoring"},{"type":"example","prompt":"Solve: x² − 5x + 6 = 0","work":"Factor: (x − 2)(x − 3) = 0\nZero-product property: x − 2 = 0 or x − 3 = 0","answer":"x = 2 or x = 3"},{"type":"h","text":"The quadratic formula"},{"type":"p","text":"For ax² + bx + c = 0: x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac)) ÷ 2a. This works for any quadratic, even ones that don't factor nicely."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Solve: 2x² + 3x − 2 = 0","work":"a=2, b=3, c=−2\nDiscriminant: 3² − 4(2)(−2) = 9 + 16 = 25\nx = (−3 ± 5) ÷ 4\nx = 2/4 = 0.5, or x = −8/4 = −2","answer":"x = 1/2 or x = −2"},{"type":"h","text":"The discriminant"},{"type":"p","text":"b² − 4ac tells you how many real solutions exist: positive → two real solutions; zero → one repeated real solution; negative → no real solutions."},{"type":"h","text":"Completing the square (brief)"},{"type":"p","text":"Rewrite ax² + bx + c = 0 by turning the x² and x terms into a perfect square trinomial. It's how the quadratic formula itself is derived, and it's the basis of vertex form."},{"type":"tip","text":"Compute the discriminant first if you just need to know how many real solutions exist — you don't always need to solve the whole equation."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Solve: x² − 7x + 12 = 0","options":["x=3 or x=4","x=-3 or x=-4","x=1 or x=12","x=3 or x=-4"],"answer":0},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"For 3x² − 2x − 1 = 0, what is the discriminant?","options":["4","16","-8","20"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Solve: 3x² − 2x − 1 = 0. The positive solution is x = {blank}","accepted":["1"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"A quadratic equation has a discriminant of −7. How many real solutions does it have?","options":["Two real solutions","One real solution","No real solutions","Infinitely many"],"answer":2},{"id":"q5","type":"reflect","prompt":"Solve by completing the square: x² + 8x + 7 = 0. Show your steps and give both solutions."},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which value of the discriminant indicates exactly one repeated real solution?","options":["Positive","Zero","Negative","Cannot be determined"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Using the quadratic formula on ax² + bx + c = 0, x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac)) ÷ {blank}","accepted":["2a"]}]},{"id":"math-alg-rational","subject":"math","category":"Algebra","title":"Rational Expressions & Equations","competency":"Simplifying, multiplying/dividing rational expressions; solving rational equations","estLesson":10,"estPractice":12,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Simplifying rational expressions"},{"type":"p","text":"Factor the numerator and denominator completely, then cancel common factors. Any value that makes the denominator zero must be excluded from the domain."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Simplify: (x² − 9)/(x + 3)","work":"Factor the numerator: (x + 3)(x − 3) / (x + 3)\nCancel (x + 3)","answer":"x − 3, x ≠ −3"},{"type":"h","text":"Multiplying and dividing rational expressions"},{"type":"p","text":"Multiply straight across after factoring and canceling. To divide, multiply by the reciprocal of the second expression."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Simplify: (x/(x + 2)) ÷ (x²/(3x + 6))","work":"Multiply by the reciprocal:\nx/(x + 2) × (3x + 6)/x²\n= x × 3(x + 2) / [(x + 2) × x²]","answer":"3/x"},{"type":"h","text":"Solving rational equations"},{"type":"p","text":"Multiply every term by the least common denominator (LCD) to clear the fractions, solve the resulting equation, then check for extraneous solutions — values that make any original denominator zero."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Solve: 1/x + 1/2 = 3/4","work":"LCD = 4x\n4 + 2x = 3x\n4 = x","answer":"x = 4"},{"type":"tip","text":"Always check your solution against the original denominators — a value that makes any denominator zero must be rejected, even if it solves the cleared equation."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Simplify (x² − 16)/(x − 4), where x ≠ 4.","options":["x+4","x-4","x²+4","x+4, valid for all x"],"answer":0},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"For what value of x is (x + 1)/(x − 4) undefined?","options":["-1","0","4","-4"],"answer":2},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Solve: 1/x + 1/3 = 5/6. x = {blank}","accepted":["2"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Simplify (x/(x + 3)) ÷ (x²/(4x + 12)), where x ≠ −3 and x ≠ 0.","options":["4/x","x/4","4x","x²/4"],"answer":0},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Simplify: (4x)/(2x²) for x ≠ 0","accepted":["2/x"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"When solving a rational equation, why must you check for extraneous solutions?","options":["To simplify the final answer","Because a value that makes a denominator zero is not a valid solution","To find additional solutions","Because rational equations always have two answers"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"fillblank","prompt":"The LCD of 1/x and 1/2 is {blank}","accepted":["2x"]}]},{"id":"math-alg-radicals","subject":"math","category":"Algebra","title":"Radical Expressions & Equations","competency":"Simplifying radicals, rationalizing denominators, solving radical equations","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Simplifying radicals"},{"type":"p","text":"Pull out any perfect-square factors from under the root."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Simplify √72","work":"72 = 36 × 2\n√36 × √2 = 6√2","answer":"6√2"},{"type":"h","text":"Rationalizing the denominator"},{"type":"p","text":"Multiply the numerator and denominator by the radical in the denominator to remove it."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Rationalize: 5/√3","work":"Multiply by √3/√3:\n5√3 / 3","answer":"5√3/3"},{"type":"h","text":"Solving radical equations"},{"type":"p","text":"Isolate the radical, then raise both sides to the matching power (square, for a square root). Always check for extraneous solutions afterward."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Solve: √(x + 3) = 5","work":"Square both sides: x + 3 = 25\nx = 22\nCheck: √(22+3) = √25 = 5 ✓","answer":"x = 22"},{"type":"tip","text":"Squaring both sides of an equation can introduce solutions that don't actually work in the original equation — always substitute back to check."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Simplify √48","options":["4√3","2√12","16√3","48"],"answer":0},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Rationalize: 7/√5","options":["7√5/5","7/5","7√5","35/√5"],"answer":0},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Solve: √(x − 2) = 4 → x = {blank}","accepted":["18"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why does √x = −4 have no solution?","options":["Because -4 is negative","Because a principal square root is never negative","Because x must be a whole number","Because you can't square a negative"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Simplify √50","accepted":["5√2","5*sqrt(2)","5sqrt2"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"After squaring both sides to solve a radical equation, what must you always do?","options":["Simplify the coefficient","Check the solution in the original equation","Divide by 2","Nothing further is needed"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Simplify: √(x²) for x ≥ 0 → {blank}","accepted":["x"]}]},{"id":"math-alg-functions","subject":"math","category":"Algebra","title":"Functions: Domain, Range & Operations","competency":"Function notation, domain/range, evaluation, composition, and inverse functions","estLesson":11,"estPractice":12,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"What is a function?"},{"type":"p","text":"A function assigns exactly one output to each input. The notation f(x) reads “f of x” — the output when x is the input."},{"type":"h","text":"Domain and range"},{"type":"p","text":"The domain is the set of all allowed inputs; the range is the set of all possible outputs. Watch for restrictions: a denominator can't be zero, and a value under an even root can't be negative."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Find the domain of f(x) = 1/(x − 3)","work":"The denominator can't equal 0:\nx − 3 ≠ 0","answer":"All real numbers except x = 3"},{"type":"h","text":"Evaluating and composing functions"},{"type":"p","text":"To evaluate f(a), substitute a for x. To compose (f∘g)(x) = f(g(x)), evaluate the inner function first, then plug that result into the outer function."},{"type":"example","prompt":"If f(x) = 2x + 1 and g(x) = x², find (f∘g)(3)","work":"g(3) = 3² = 9\nf(9) = 2(9) + 1","answer":"19"},{"type":"h","text":"Inverse functions"},{"type":"p","text":"To find f⁻¹(x), swap x and y in y = f(x), then solve for the new y."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Find the inverse of f(x) = 2x − 4","work":"y = 2x − 4\nSwap: x = 2y − 4\nx + 4 = 2y","answer":"f⁻¹(x) = (x + 4) / 2"},{"type":"tip","text":"A function and its inverse are reflections of each other across the line y = x — and composing them, f(f⁻¹(x)), always gives back x."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Find the domain of f(x) = 1/(x + 5)","options":["All real numbers","All real numbers except x=-5","All real numbers except x=5","x≥-5"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"If f(x) = 2x + 1 and g(x) = x², find (f∘g)(4).","options":["16","17","33","32"],"answer":2},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"If f(x) = 3x − 2, then f(5) = {blank}","accepted":["13"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Find the inverse of f(x) = 3x + 6","options":["f⁻¹(x)=(x-6)/3","f⁻¹(x)=(x+6)/3","f⁻¹(x)=3x-6","f⁻¹(x)=x/3+6"],"answer":0},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"If g(x) = x² − 1, find g(−3).","accepted":["8"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which best describes the range of a function?","options":["The set of all allowed inputs","The set of all possible outputs","The set of x-intercepts","The domain restrictions"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"For an invertible function f and any x in its range, f(f⁻¹(x)) equals:","options":["0","1","x","f(x)²"],"answer":2}]},{"id":"math-alg-linear-functions","subject":"math","category":"Algebra","title":"Linear Functions & Graphs","competency":"Slope, slope-intercept form, x/y-intercepts, and graphing lines","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Slope"},{"type":"p","text":"Slope m = (y₂ − y₁) / (x₂ − x₁), measuring a line's steepness and direction."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Find the slope through (2, 3) and (6, 11)","work":"m = (11 − 3) / (6 − 2) = 8/4","answer":"m = 2"},{"type":"h","text":"Slope-intercept form"},{"type":"p","text":"y = mx + b, where m is the slope and b is the y-intercept — where the line crosses the y-axis (at x = 0)."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Write the equation of a line with slope 3 passing through (0, −2)","work":"The point (0, −2) is on the y-axis, so b = −2 directly","answer":"y = 3x − 2"},{"type":"h","text":"Finding intercepts"},{"type":"p","text":"For the x-intercept, set y = 0 and solve for x. For the y-intercept, set x = 0 and solve for y."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Find the x- and y-intercepts of 2x + 3y = 12","work":"y=0: 2x=12 → x=6 → (6, 0)\nx=0: 3y=12 → y=4 → (0, 4)","answer":"(6, 0) and (0, 4)"},{"type":"tip","text":"A positive slope rises left to right; a negative slope falls left to right; a slope of 0 is a horizontal line; an undefined slope is a vertical line."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Find the slope of the line through (1, 4) and (5, 16).","options":["2","3","4","12"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the y-intercept of y = 3x − 2?","options":["3","-2","2","0"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Find the x-intercept of 3x + 4y = 24: ({blank}, 0)","accepted":["8"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"A line with a slope of 0 is:","options":["Vertical","Horizontal","Diagonal upward","Diagonal downward"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Write, in slope-intercept form, the equation of a line with slope 3 that passes through (0, −2).","accepted":["y=3x-2","y = 3x - 2"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is true about a line with an undefined slope?","options":["It is horizontal","It passes through the origin","It is vertical","It has a slope of 0"],"answer":2},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Find the slope of the line passing through (−1, 4) and (3, 4).","options":["0","1","4","Undefined"],"answer":0}]},{"id":"math-alg-quadratic-functions","subject":"math","category":"Algebra","title":"Quadratic Functions & Graphs","competency":"Vertex, axis of symmetry, and the shape of a parabola","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"The graph of a quadratic function"},{"type":"p","text":"f(x) = ax² + bx + c graphs as a parabola. If a > 0, it opens upward and has a minimum point; if a < 0, it opens downward and has a maximum point."},{"type":"h","text":"Finding the vertex and axis of symmetry"},{"type":"p","text":"The axis of symmetry is x = −b/(2a). Substitute that x back into the function to get the vertex's y-coordinate."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Find the vertex of f(x) = x² − 4x + 1","work":"Axis: x = −(−4)/(2×1) = 2\nf(2) = 4 − 8 + 1","answer":"Vertex: (2, −3)"},{"type":"h","text":"Vertex form"},{"type":"p","text":"f(x) = a(x − h)² + k gives the vertex (h, k) directly, without any calculation."},{"type":"example","prompt":"State the vertex of f(x) = 2(x − 3)² + 5","work":"Compare to a(x − h)² + k: h=3, k=5","answer":"(3, 5)"},{"type":"tip","text":"The axis of symmetry is a vertical line, x = [value] — it splits the parabola into two mirror-image halves."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Find the axis of symmetry of f(x) = x² − 6x + 5","options":["x=3","x=-3","x=6","x=-6"],"answer":0},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Find the vertex of f(x) = x² − 6x + 5","options":["(3,-4)","(3,5)","(-3,32)","(6,5)"],"answer":0},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"The vertex of f(x) = 2(x − 3)² + 5 is ({blank}, 5)","accepted":["3"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"If a < 0 in f(x) = ax² + bx + c, the parabola:","options":["Opens upward with a minimum","Opens downward with a maximum","Is a straight line","Has no vertex"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Find the axis of symmetry of f(x) = 2x² + 8x − 3.","accepted":["x=-2","x = -2","-2"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"A parabola opening upward has vertex (0, 0). What is its minimum output value?","options":["-1","0","1","Undefined"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which form of a quadratic directly shows the vertex?","options":["Standard form ax²+bx+c","Vertex form a(x-h)²+k","Factored form","Slope-intercept form"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"math-alg-explog","subject":"math","category":"Algebra","title":"Exponential & Logarithmic Equations","competency":"Solving exponential equations, converting between exponential/log form, log properties","estLesson":10,"estPractice":12,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Solving exponential equations with matching bases"},{"type":"p","text":"If bᵐ = bⁿ (same base), then m = n. Rewrite both sides with the same base whenever possible."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Solve: 2^(x+1) = 32","work":"32 = 2⁵\nx + 1 = 5","answer":"x = 4"},{"type":"h","text":"Converting between exponential and logarithmic form"},{"type":"p","text":"bʸ = x is equivalent to log_b(x) = y. log(x) without a written base means base 10; ln(x) means the natural log, base e."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Rewrite 5³ = 125 in logarithmic form","work":"Base 5, exponent 3, result 125","answer":"log₅(125) = 3"},{"type":"h","text":"Log properties"},{"type":"list","items":["log(ab) = log(a) + log(b)","log(a/b) = log(a) − log(b)","log(aⁿ) = n · log(a)"]},{"type":"example","prompt":"Solve: log₂(x) = 5","work":"Rewrite exponentially: x = 2⁵","answer":"x = 32"},{"type":"tip","text":"When an exponential equation's bases don't obviously match (like 2^x = 30), take the log of both sides and use log(aⁿ) = n·log(a) to bring the exponent down."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Solve: 3^(x+1) = 81","options":["x=2","x=3","x=4","x=27"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Rewrite 4² = 16 in logarithmic form.","options":["log₄(16)=2","log₁₆(4)=2","log₂(16)=4","log₄(2)=16"],"answer":0},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Solve: log₃(x) = 4 → x = {blank}","accepted":["81"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"For a, b > 0 and logarithms of the same valid base, log(a) + log(b) equals:","options":["log(a+b)","log(a-b)","log(ab)","log(a/b)"],"answer":2},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Solve: 3^x = 81","accepted":["4"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"For a > 0 and real n, log(aⁿ) is equivalent to:","options":["n + log(a)","n · log(a)","log(a)ⁿ","log(na)"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"fillblank","prompt":"log₃(9) = {blank}","accepted":["2"]}]},{"id":"math-alg-sequences","subject":"math","category":"Algebra","title":"Arithmetic & Geometric Sequences","competency":"nth-term formulas, common difference/ratio, sum of a finite series","estLesson":10,"estPractice":12,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Arithmetic sequences"},{"type":"p","text":"Each term is found by adding a constant common difference d to the previous term. The nth term: aₙ = a₁ + (n − 1)d."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Find the 10th term of 3, 7, 11, 15, …","work":"a₁ = 3, d = 4\na₁₀ = 3 + (10 − 1)(4) = 3 + 36","answer":"39"},{"type":"h","text":"Sum of an arithmetic series"},{"type":"p","text":"Sₙ = n/2 × (a₁ + aₙ)"},{"type":"example","prompt":"Find the sum of the first 10 terms of 3, 7, 11, 15, …","work":"S₁₀ = 10/2 × (3 + 39) = 5 × 42","answer":"210"},{"type":"h","text":"Geometric sequences"},{"type":"p","text":"Each term is found by multiplying the previous term by a constant common ratio r. The nth term: aₙ = a₁ × r^(n−1)."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Find the 6th term of 2, 6, 18, 54, …","work":"a₁ = 2, r = 3\na₆ = 2 × 3⁵ = 2 × 243","answer":"486"},{"type":"h","text":"Sum of a finite geometric series"},{"type":"p","text":"Sₙ = a₁(1 − rⁿ) / (1 − r), for r ≠ 1."},{"type":"tip","text":"To tell them apart quickly: if consecutive terms have a constant DIFFERENCE, it's arithmetic; if they have a constant RATIO, it's geometric."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Find the 10th term of the arithmetic sequence 5, 9, 13, 17, …","options":["37","41","45","49"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Find the sum of the first 10 terms of 5, 9, 13, 17, …","options":["215","230","245","260"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Find the 6th term of the geometric sequence 3, 6, 12, 24, … {blank}","accepted":["96"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"The sequence 5, 5, 5, 5, … is a geometric sequence with common ratio:","options":["0","1","5","Undefined"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"What is the common difference of the arithmetic sequence 20, 14, 8, 2, …?","accepted":["-6"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which sequence is geometric?","options":["2, 4, 6, 8, …","3, 9, 27, 81, …","1, 3, 5, 7, …","10, 8, 6, 4, …"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"fillblank","prompt":"For an arithmetic sequence with a₁ = 5 and d = 3, the 8th term is {blank}","accepted":["26"]}]},{"id":"math-alg-variation","subject":"math","category":"Algebra","title":"Variation: Direct, Inverse & Joint","competency":"Setting up and solving direct, inverse, and joint variation problems","estLesson":8,"estPractice":10,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Direct variation"},{"type":"p","text":"“y varies directly with x” means y = kx for some constant k — as x increases, y increases proportionally."},{"type":"example","prompt":"y varies directly with x. If y = 15 when x = 3, find y when x = 8.","work":"Find k: 15 = k(3) → k = 5\ny = 5(8)","answer":"40"},{"type":"h","text":"Inverse variation"},{"type":"p","text":"“y varies inversely with x” means y = k/x — as x increases, y decreases."},{"type":"example","prompt":"y varies inversely with x. If y = 4 when x = 6, find y when x = 8.","work":"k = xy = 4 × 6 = 24\ny = 24 ÷ 8","answer":"3"},{"type":"h","text":"Joint variation"},{"type":"p","text":"“z varies jointly with x and y” means z = kxy — z depends on the product of two (or more) variables."},{"type":"example","prompt":"z varies jointly with x and y. If z = 48 when x = 4 and y = 3, find z when x = 6 and y = 2.","work":"k = 48 ÷ (4 × 3) = 4\nz = 4(6)(2)","answer":"48"},{"type":"tip","text":"In every variation problem, the first step is always the same: use the given values to solve for the constant k, then use that k to answer the actual question."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"y varies directly with x. If y = 20 when x = 4, find y when x = 9.","options":["36","40","45","50"],"answer":2},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"y varies inversely with x. If y = 10 when x = 4, find y when x = 5.","options":["6","8","10","12"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"If z varies jointly with x and y, and z = 60 when x = 5, y = 4, then k = {blank}","accepted":["3"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which equation represents “y varies inversely with x”?","options":["y=kx","y=k/x","y=kx²","y=k+x"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"z varies jointly with x and y. If z = 60 when x = 5 and y = 4, find z when x = 7 and y = 3.","accepted":["63"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"In direct variation y = kx, if x doubles, y:","options":["Doubles","Halves","Stays the same","Quadruples"],"answer":0},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"In inverse variation y = k/x, if x doubles, y:","options":["Doubles","Halves","Stays the same","Quadruples"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"math-alg-word-modeling","subject":"math","category":"Algebra","title":"Word Problems & Mathematical Modeling","competency":"Translating age, work, and motion word problems into solvable equations","estLesson":11,"estPractice":13,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Age problems"},{"type":"p","text":"Represent unknown ages with variables. “In n years” means +n, and “n years ago” means −n, applied to every person's age."},{"type":"example","prompt":"A father is 3 times as old as his son. In 15 years, he will be twice as old as his son. Find their current ages.","work":"Let son = x and father = 3x.\n3x + 15 = 2(x + 15)\n3x + 15 = 2x + 30, so x = 15.","answer":"Son: 15. Father: 45."},{"type":"h","text":"Work problems"},{"type":"p","text":"Represent each worker's rate as (1 job) ÷ (time to finish alone). The combined rate is the sum of the individual rates, and the time working together is 1 ÷ combined rate."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Pipe A fills a tank in 6 hours; pipe B fills it in 3 hours. How long will both take together?","work":"Rate A = 1/6, Rate B = 1/3 = 2/6\nCombined = 3/6 = 1/2\nTime = 1 ÷ (1/2)","answer":"2 hours"},{"type":"h","text":"Motion problems"},{"type":"p","text":"Distance = rate × time (d = rt). For objects moving in opposite directions, add their rates; for the same direction, compare their rates or positions directly."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Two buses leave the same terminal at the same time, traveling in opposite directions, at 60 km/h and 80 km/h. After how many hours are they 350 km apart?","work":"Combined rate = 140 km/h\nTime = 350 ÷ 140","answer":"2.5 hours"},{"type":"tip","text":"For every word problem, write what each unknown represents in words FIRST, before writing any equation — it prevents you from mixing up whose age or whose rate you're solving for."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"A mother is 5 times as old as her son. In 16 years, she will be 3 times as old as him. How old is the son now?","options":["4","8","16","20"],"answer":2},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Pipe A fills a pool in 8 hours; pipe B fills it in 8 hours. How long will both take together?","options":["2 hours","4 hours","8 hours","16 hours"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Two trains leave the same station at the same time in opposite directions, at 70 km/h and 90 km/h. After {blank} hours they are 480 km apart.","accepted":["3"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"In a work-rate problem, if a worker can finish a job alone in 5 hours, their rate is:","options":["5 jobs/hour","1/5 job/hour","5 hours/job","1 job/5 workers"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"A mother is 4 times as old as her daughter. In 6 years, she will be 3 times as old as her daughter. How old is the daughter now?","accepted":["12"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Two cars start from the same point and travel in the same direction, one at 50 km/h and the other at 70 km/h. How far apart are they after 3 hours?","options":["20 km","60 km","150 km","210 km"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"In d = rt, if a car travels 240 km in 4 hours, its rate is:","options":["60 km/h","80 km/h","120 km/h","960 km/h"],"answer":0}]},{"id":"math-geo-angles","subject":"math","category":"Geometry","title":"Basic Terms & Angle Relationships","competency":"Points, lines, planes, angles — complementary, supplementary, and vertical angles","estLesson":8,"estPractice":10,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Basic terms"},{"type":"p","text":"A point has no size, just location. A line extends infinitely in both directions with no thickness. A plane is a flat surface extending infinitely in all directions. An angle is formed by two rays sharing a common endpoint (the vertex), measured in degrees."},{"type":"h","text":"Angle relationships"},{"type":"list","items":["<b>Complementary angles</b> sum to 90°","<b>Supplementary angles</b> sum to 180°","<b>Vertical angles</b> (formed by two intersecting lines, opposite each other) are always equal"]},{"type":"example","prompt":"Two angles are complementary. One measures 37°. Find the other.","work":"90 − 37","answer":"53°"},{"type":"example","prompt":"Two angles are supplementary. One measures 112°. Find the other.","work":"180 − 112","answer":"68°"},{"type":"example","prompt":"Two lines intersect, forming a 65° angle. Find the measure of its vertical angle.","work":"Vertical angles are always equal","answer":"65°"},{"type":"tip","text":"Angles that form a straight line (a linear pair) are always supplementary — this is different from vertical angles, which are equal, not necessarily summing to anything specific."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Two angles are complementary. One measures 52°. Find the other.","options":["28°","38°","48°","58°"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Two angles are supplementary. One measures 75°. Find the other.","options":["95°","105°","115°","125°"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Two intersecting lines form a 48° angle. Its vertical angle measures {blank}°.","accepted":["48"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which pair of angles always sums to 180°?","options":["Complementary","Supplementary","Vertical","Adjacent"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Two angles are complementary. One is three times the other. Find the smaller angle.","accepted":["22.5","22.5°"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Vertical angles are always:","options":["Complementary","Supplementary","Equal","Right angles"],"answer":2},{"id":"q7","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Two supplementary angles differ by 40°. The larger angle measures {blank}°.","accepted":["110"]}]},{"id":"math-geo-triangle-properties","subject":"math","category":"Geometry","title":"Triangle Properties","competency":"Angle sum, the triangle inequality theorem, and the exterior angle theorem","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Angle sum of a triangle"},{"type":"p","text":"The interior angles of any triangle always sum to 180°."},{"type":"example","prompt":"A triangle has angles 55° and 65°. Find the third angle.","work":"180 − 55 − 65","answer":"60°"},{"type":"h","text":"Triangle inequality theorem"},{"type":"p","text":"The sum of the lengths of any two sides of a triangle must be greater than the length of the third side. Use this to check whether three given lengths can actually form a triangle."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Can sides of length 4, 7, and 12 form a triangle?","work":"4 + 7 = 11, which is NOT greater than 12","answer":"No"},{"type":"h","text":"Exterior angle theorem"},{"type":"p","text":"An exterior angle of a triangle equals the sum of the two non-adjacent (remote) interior angles."},{"type":"example","prompt":"A triangle's interior angles are 40° and 75°. Find the exterior angle at the third vertex.","work":"40 + 75","answer":"115°"},{"type":"tip","text":"To quickly check the triangle inequality, you only need to test the two SHORTEST sides — if their sum beats the longest side, all three combinations automatically work."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"A triangle has angles 48° and 82°. Find the third angle.","options":["40°","50°","60°","70°"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Can sides of length 3, 5, and 9 form a triangle?","options":["Yes, because 3+5>9","No, because 3+5<9","Yes, because all are positive","No, because 9 is too small"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"A triangle's interior angles are 35° and 90°. The exterior angle at the third vertex measures {blank}°.","accepted":["125"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which set of side lengths CAN form a triangle?","options":["2, 3, 6","4, 4, 9","5, 6, 10","1, 2, 3"],"answer":2},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"A triangle has angles in the ratio 2:3:4. Find the smallest angle.","accepted":["40","40°"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"The exterior angle theorem states that an exterior angle of a triangle equals:","options":["The sum of all three interior angles","The sum of the two remote (non-adjacent) interior angles","Twice the smallest interior angle","Half the sum of the interior angles"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"A triangle has sides 6 and 10. Which could NOT be the length of the third side?","options":["5","8","16","12"],"answer":2}]},{"id":"math-geo-special-triangles","subject":"math","category":"Geometry","title":"Special Right Triangles","competency":"Side ratios for 30-60-90 and 45-45-90 triangles","estLesson":8,"estPractice":10,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"45-45-90 triangles"},{"type":"p","text":"The two legs are equal in length, and the hypotenuse = leg × √2."},{"type":"example","prompt":"A 45-45-90 triangle has legs of length 7. Find the hypotenuse.","work":"hypotenuse = 7 × √2","answer":"7√2"},{"type":"h","text":"30-60-90 triangles"},{"type":"p","text":"The sides are always in the ratio 1 : √3 : 2 (short leg : long leg : hypotenuse). If the short leg = x, the long leg = x√3 and the hypotenuse = 2x."},{"type":"example","prompt":"A 30-60-90 triangle has a short leg of 5. Find the long leg and hypotenuse.","work":"Long leg = 5√3\nHypotenuse = 2 × 5","answer":"long leg = 5√3, hypotenuse = 10"},{"type":"tip","text":"Recognize these triangles immediately when you see a right angle plus one other given angle of 30°, 45°, or 60° — you can skip the Pythagorean theorem entirely and use the ratio directly."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"A 45-45-90 triangle has legs of length 9. Find the hypotenuse.","options":["9","9√2","9√3","18"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"A 30-60-90 triangle has a short leg of 6. Find the hypotenuse.","options":["6","6√3","12","12√3"],"answer":2},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"A 30-60-90 triangle has a short leg of 6. The long leg is {blank}.","accepted":["6√3","6*sqrt(3)","6sqrt3"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"A 45-45-90 triangle has a hypotenuse of 10√2. Find the length of each leg.","options":["5","10","10√2","20"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"A 30-60-90 triangle has a hypotenuse of 14. Find the short leg.","accepted":["7"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"In a 30-60-90 triangle, the ratio of short leg : long leg : hypotenuse is:","options":["1:2:3","1:√2:2","1:√3:2","√2:√3:2"],"answer":2},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"A right triangle has two legs of equal length. This must be a:","options":["30-60-90 triangle","45-45-90 triangle","Scalene right triangle","Obtuse triangle"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"math-geo-pythagorean","subject":"math","category":"Geometry","title":"Pythagorean Theorem","competency":"a² + b² = c² — finding a missing side and identifying right triangles","estLesson":9,"estPractice":12,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"The theorem"},{"type":"p","text":"In a right triangle, a² + b² = c², where c is the hypotenuse — the side opposite the right angle, and always the longest side."},{"type":"example","prompt":"A right triangle has legs 6 and 8. Find the hypotenuse.","work":"6² + 8² = 36 + 64 = 100\n√100","answer":"10"},{"type":"h","text":"Finding a missing leg"},{"type":"example","prompt":"A right triangle has a hypotenuse of 13 and one leg of 5. Find the other leg.","work":"5² + b² = 13²\n25 + b² = 169\nb² = 144","answer":"12"},{"type":"h","text":"The converse: identifying right triangles"},{"type":"p","text":"If a² + b² = c² holds true for three given side lengths (c being the longest), the triangle is a right triangle."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Is a triangle with sides 9, 12, 15 a right triangle?","work":"9² + 12² = 81 + 144 = 225\n15² = 225 — equal","answer":"Yes, it's a right triangle"},{"type":"tip","text":"Memorize common Pythagorean triples — 3-4-5, 5-12-13, 8-15-17 — and their multiples (like 6-8-10) to save time recognizing right triangles instantly."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"A right triangle has legs 8 and 15. Find the hypotenuse.","options":["17","20","23","161"],"answer":0},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"A right triangle has a hypotenuse of 25 and one leg of 7. Find the other leg.","options":["18","20","22","24"],"answer":3},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"A right triangle has legs 20 and 21. The hypotenuse is {blank}.","accepted":["29"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Is a triangle with sides 10, 24, 26 a right triangle?","options":["Yes, 10²+24²=26²","No, the sides don't satisfy a²+b²=c²","Yes, because 10+24>26","Cannot be determined"],"answer":0},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"A 15 ft ladder leans against a vertical wall, with its base 9 ft from the wall on level ground. How high up the wall does it reach?","accepted":["12","12 ft","12 feet"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"In a right triangle, the hypotenuse is:","options":["Always the shortest side","Always opposite the right angle and the longest side","Always equal to one of the legs","Only present in 45-45-90 triangles"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which set of numbers is a Pythagorean triple?","options":["2, 3, 4","6, 8, 10","5, 6, 7","9, 10, 11"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"math-geo-congruence-similarity","subject":"math","category":"Geometry","title":"Congruent & Similar Triangles","competency":"Congruence criteria (SSS, SAS, ASA) and similarity with proportional sides","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Congruent triangles"},{"type":"p","text":"Congruent triangles are identical in size and shape — all corresponding sides and angles are equal. Common criteria used to prove congruence: SSS (three sides), SAS (two sides and the included angle), and ASA (two angles and the included side). Note that AAA (matching all three angles) only proves triangles are similar, not congruent — congruence additionally requires matching size."},{"type":"h","text":"Similar triangles"},{"type":"p","text":"Similar triangles have the same shape but not necessarily the same size — corresponding angles are equal, and corresponding sides are in proportion (the same ratio, called the scale factor)."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Triangle ABC is similar to triangle DEF. AB=6, DE=9, and BC=8. Find EF.","work":"Scale factor = 9/6 = 1.5\nEF = 8 × 1.5","answer":"12"},{"type":"tip","text":"Match up corresponding vertices carefully by the order of the letters in the similarity statement (△ABC ~ △DEF means A↔D, B↔E, C↔F) — mismatching them is the most common error."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which criterion proves two triangles congruent using two sides and the included angle?","options":["SSS","SAS","ASA","AAA"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Triangle ABC ~ Triangle DEF. AB=4, DE=10, BC=6. Find EF.","options":["12","15","18","20"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Similar triangles have corresponding angles that are {blank} and corresponding sides that are proportional.","accepted":["equal","congruent"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which is NOT a valid triangle congruence criterion?","options":["SSS","SAS","ASA","AAA"],"answer":3},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Triangle PQR ~ triangle XYZ. The scale factor from PQR to XYZ is 2. If PQ = 5, find XY.","accepted":["10"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"If two triangles are congruent, which of the following is true?","options":["Their angles are equal but sides may differ","Their corresponding sides and angles are all equal","They must be similar but not congruent","Their perimeters may differ"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"In similar figures, the ratio of any two corresponding sides is called the:","options":["Congruence ratio","Scale factor","Angle sum","Perimeter ratio"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"math-geo-polygons","subject":"math","category":"Geometry","title":"Polygons: Angle Sums & Quadrilaterals","competency":"Interior/exterior angle sum formulas; properties of common quadrilaterals","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Interior angle sum"},{"type":"p","text":"The sum of the interior angles of a polygon with n sides = (n − 2) × 180°."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Find the sum of the interior angles of a hexagon (6 sides).","work":"(6 − 2) × 180 = 4 × 180","answer":"720°"},{"type":"h","text":"Exterior angle sum"},{"type":"p","text":"The sum of the exterior angles of any convex polygon (one at each vertex) is always 360°, regardless of the number of sides."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Find each exterior angle of a regular pentagon (5 sides).","work":"360 ÷ 5","answer":"72°"},{"type":"h","text":"Quadrilateral properties"},{"type":"list","items":["<b>Parallelogram</b> — opposite sides parallel and equal, opposite angles equal","<b>Rectangle</b> — a parallelogram with four right angles","<b>Rhombus</b> — a parallelogram with four equal sides","<b>Square</b> — a rectangle and a rhombus (all sides equal, all angles 90°)","<b>Trapezoid</b> — exactly one pair of parallel sides"]},{"type":"tip","text":"For a REGULAR polygon (all sides and angles equal), divide the total interior angle sum by n to get the measure of just one interior angle."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Find the sum of the interior angles of an octagon (8 sides).","options":["900°","1080°","1260°","1440°"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Find each interior angle of a regular pentagon.","options":["100°","108°","120°","135°"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Find each exterior angle of a regular decagon (10 sides): {blank}°","accepted":["36"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which quadrilateral has four equal sides but not necessarily four right angles?","options":["Rectangle","Rhombus","Trapezoid","Square"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"A polygon has an interior angle sum of 1,440°. How many sides does it have?","accepted":["10"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"The sum of the exterior angles (one per vertex) of any convex polygon is always:","options":["180°","270°","360°","Depends on the number of sides"],"answer":2},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which shape is defined as a parallelogram with four right angles?","options":["Rhombus","Trapezoid","Rectangle","Kite"],"answer":2}]},{"id":"math-geo-circles","subject":"math","category":"Geometry","title":"Circles: Angles, Arcs, Chords & Tangents","competency":"Central and inscribed angles, arcs, chords, tangents, and secants","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Central and inscribed angles"},{"type":"p","text":"A central angle has its vertex at the circle's center; its measure equals the measure of the arc it intercepts. An inscribed angle has its vertex ON the circle; its measure is HALF the measure of its intercepted arc."},{"type":"example","prompt":"A central angle intercepts an arc of 80°. Find the central angle's measure.","work":"Equal to the arc","answer":"80°"},{"type":"example","prompt":"An inscribed angle intercepts the same 80° arc. Find the inscribed angle's measure.","work":"Half of 80","answer":"40°"},{"type":"h","text":"Chords and tangents"},{"type":"p","text":"A chord is a segment connecting two points on the circle. A tangent line touches the circle at exactly one point and is always perpendicular to the radius drawn to that point. A secant line intersects the circle at two points."},{"type":"tip","text":"Whenever you see a tangent line and a radius meeting at the point of tangency, that angle is always 90° — mark it immediately, it often unlocks the rest of the problem."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"A central angle intercepts an arc of 100°. Find the central angle's measure.","options":["50°","100°","200°","180°"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"An inscribed angle intercepts an arc of 100°. Find the inscribed angle's measure.","options":["25°","50°","100°","200°"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"A tangent line meets a circle's radius at the point of tangency. The angle between them is always {blank}°.","accepted":["90"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which segment connects two points on a circle without necessarily passing through the center?","options":["Radius","Diameter","Chord","Tangent"],"answer":2},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"An inscribed angle measures 35°. Find the measure of its intercepted arc.","accepted":["70","70°"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"A line that intersects a circle at exactly one point is called a:","options":["Chord","Secant","Tangent","Diameter"],"answer":2},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"A line that intersects a circle at two points is called a:","options":["Tangent","Secant","Radius","Central line"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"math-geo-area-perimeter-volume","subject":"math","category":"Geometry","title":"Perimeter, Area & Volume Formulas","competency":"Formulas for common 2D shapes and basic 3D solids","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"2D perimeter and area"},{"type":"list","items":["<b>Rectangle:</b> P = 2(l + w), A = lw","<b>Triangle:</b> A = ½bh","<b>Circle:</b> circumference = 2πr, A = πr²","<b>Trapezoid:</b> A = ½(b₁ + b₂)h"]},{"type":"example","prompt":"Find the area of a triangle with base 10 cm and height 6 cm.","work":"A = ½(10)(6)","answer":"30 cm²"},{"type":"example","prompt":"Find the circumference and area of a circle with radius 7 cm (use π ≈ 22/7).","work":"C = 2 × 22/7 × 7 = 44 cm\nA = 22/7 × 7² = 22/7 × 49 = 154 cm²","answer":"C = 44 cm, A = 154 cm²"},{"type":"h","text":"3D volume basics"},{"type":"list","items":["<b>Rectangular prism:</b> V = lwh","<b>Cylinder:</b> V = πr²h","<b>Cube:</b> V = s³"]},{"type":"example","prompt":"Find the volume of a rectangular prism 5 cm × 4 cm × 3 cm.","work":"5 × 4 × 3","answer":"60 cm³"},{"type":"tip","text":"Always double-check your units: perimeter and circumference are measured in linear units (cm), area in square units (cm²), and volume in cubic units (cm³)."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Find the area of a triangle with base 14 cm and height 5 cm.","options":["19 cm²","35 cm²","70 cm²","140 cm²"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Find the area of a circle with radius 14 cm (use π ≈ 22/7).","options":["44 cm²","88 cm²","308 cm²","616 cm²"],"answer":3},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Find the perimeter of a rectangle 8 cm by 5 cm: {blank} cm","accepted":["26"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Find the volume of a rectangular prism 6 cm × 4 cm × 2 cm.","options":["12 cm³","24 cm³","48 cm³","96 cm³"],"answer":2},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Find the circumference of a circle with radius 21 cm (use π ≈ 22/7).","accepted":["132","132 cm"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which formula gives the area of a trapezoid?","options":["A=lw","A=½bh","A=½(b₁+b₂)h","A=πr²"],"answer":2},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"What are the correct units for volume, if length is measured in meters?","options":["m","m²","m³","No units"],"answer":2}]},{"id":"math-geo-solids","subject":"math","category":"Geometry","title":"Solid Geometry: Prisms, Cylinders, Cones, Spheres & Pyramids","competency":"Volume and surface area formulas for common solids","estLesson":10,"estPractice":12,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Volume formulas"},{"type":"list","items":["<b>Prism:</b> V = Bh (B = area of the base)","<b>Cylinder:</b> V = πr²h","<b>Pyramid:</b> V = ⅓Bh","<b>Cone:</b> V = ⅓πr²h","<b>Sphere:</b> V = 4/3πr³"]},{"type":"example","prompt":"Find the volume of a cone with radius 3 cm and height 8 cm (use π ≈ 3.14).","work":"V = ⅓ × 3.14 × 9 × 8\n= ⅓ × 226.08","answer":"≈ 75.36 cm³"},{"type":"h","text":"Surface area formulas"},{"type":"list","items":["<b>Cylinder:</b> SA = 2πr² + 2πrh (two circles + the rectangle that wraps around)","<b>Sphere:</b> SA = 4πr²"]},{"type":"example","prompt":"Find the surface area of a sphere with radius 5 cm (use π ≈ 3.14).","work":"SA = 4 × 3.14 × 25\n= 4 × 78.5","answer":"314 cm²"},{"type":"tip","text":"Notice the pattern: a pyramid's volume is exactly ⅓ of a prism with the same base and height, and a cone's volume is exactly ⅓ of a cylinder with the same base and height."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Find the volume of a cylinder with radius 4 cm and height 10 cm (use π ≈ 3.14).","options":["125.6 cm³","251.2 cm³","502.4 cm³","1004.8 cm³"],"answer":2},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Find the volume of a pyramid with a base area of 24 cm² and height 9 cm.","options":["36 cm³","72 cm³","108 cm³","216 cm³"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Find the volume of a cone with radius 5 cm and height 6 cm (use π ≈ 3.14): {blank} cm³","accepted":["157"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which formula gives the volume of a sphere?","options":["V=4πr²","V=4/3πr³","V=πr²h","V=⅓πr²h"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Find the surface area of a sphere with radius 3 cm (use π ≈ 3.14).","accepted":["113.04","113.04 cm²"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"A cone and a cylinder have the same base radius and height. The cone's volume is:","options":["Equal to the cylinder's","Half the cylinder's","One-third the cylinder's","Twice the cylinder's"],"answer":2},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which formula gives the volume of any prism, using the area of its base B?","options":["V=Bh","V=⅓Bh","V=B²h","V=2Bh"],"answer":0}]},{"id":"math-geo-coordinate","subject":"math","category":"Geometry","title":"Coordinate Geometry","competency":"Distance formula, midpoint formula, and slope in the coordinate plane","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Distance formula"},{"type":"p","text":"Derived from the Pythagorean theorem: distance = √[(x₂ − x₁)² + (y₂ − y₁)²]."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Find the distance between (1, 2) and (4, 6).","work":"√[(4−1)² + (6−2)²] = √[9 + 16]","answer":"5"},{"type":"h","text":"Midpoint formula"},{"type":"p","text":"Midpoint = ((x₁+x₂)/2, (y₁+y₂)/2) — literally the average of the x-coordinates and the average of the y-coordinates."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Find the midpoint between (2, 3) and (8, 11).","work":"((2+8)/2, (3+11)/2)","answer":"(5, 7)"},{"type":"h","text":"Using slope to classify lines"},{"type":"p","text":"Parallel lines have equal slopes. Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other (their product is −1)."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Line A has a slope of 2/3. What slope would a line perpendicular to it have?","work":"Negative reciprocal of 2/3","answer":"−3/2"},{"type":"tip","text":"The distance formula is just the Pythagorean theorem in disguise — the horizontal and vertical differences between two points are the two legs of a right triangle, and the distance is the hypotenuse."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Find the distance between (0, 0) and (6, 8).","options":["8","10","12","14"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Find the midpoint between (4, 1) and (10, 9).","options":["(7,5)","(6,5)","(7,4)","(6,4)"],"answer":0},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Find the distance between (0, 0) and (5, 12): {blank}","accepted":["13"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Line A has a slope of −4/5. What slope would a line perpendicular to it have?","options":["4/5","-4/5","5/4","-5/4"],"answer":2},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Find the midpoint between (−2, 6) and (4, −2).","accepted":["(1,2)","(1, 2)"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Two lines are parallel. Line A has a slope of 3/4. What is the slope of Line B?","options":["3/4","-3/4","4/3","-4/3"],"answer":0},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which formula is used to find the midpoint of a segment?","options":["√[(x₂−x₁)²+(y₂−y₁)²]","((x₁+x₂)/2, (y₁+y₂)/2)","(y₂−y₁)/(x₂−x₁)","(x₂−x₁)(y₂−y₁)"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"math-trig-functions","subject":"math","category":"Trigonometry","title":"The Six Trigonometric Functions","competency":"Sine, cosine, tangent and their reciprocals — cosecant, secant, cotangent","estLesson":8,"estPractice":10,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"The three primary functions"},{"type":"p","text":"For a right triangle with reference angle θ: sin θ = opposite/hypotenuse, cos θ = adjacent/hypotenuse, tan θ = opposite/adjacent."},{"type":"h","text":"The three reciprocal functions"},{"type":"list","items":["csc θ = 1/sin θ = hypotenuse/opposite","sec θ = 1/cos θ = hypotenuse/adjacent","cot θ = 1/tan θ = adjacent/opposite"]},{"type":"example","prompt":"A right triangle has opposite=3, adjacent=4, hypotenuse=5 relative to angle θ. Find sin θ, cos θ, and tan θ.","work":"sin θ = 3/5\ncos θ = 4/5\ntan θ = 3/4","answer":"sin θ=3/5, cos θ=4/5, tan θ=3/4"},{"type":"example","prompt":"Using the same triangle, find csc θ and sec θ.","work":"csc θ = 1/sin θ = 5/3\nsec θ = 1/cos θ = 5/4","answer":"csc θ=5/3, sec θ=5/4"},{"type":"tip","text":"sin and csc are reciprocals, cos and sec are reciprocals, tan and cot are reciprocals — notice each pair shares no letters in its abbreviation, which helps you avoid mixing them up (sec is NOT the reciprocal of sin, even though the names look similar)."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"A right triangle has opposite=5, adjacent=12, hypotenuse=13 relative to angle θ. Find sin θ.","options":["5/12","5/13","12/13","13/5"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Using the same triangle, find cos θ.","options":["5/12","5/13","12/13","13/12"],"answer":2},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Using the same triangle (opposite=5, adjacent=12, hyp=13), tan θ = {blank}","accepted":["5/12"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the reciprocal of cos θ?","options":["sin θ","csc θ","sec θ","cot θ"],"answer":2},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"If sin θ = 3/5, what is csc θ?","accepted":["5/3"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which function is the reciprocal of tangent?","options":["Sine","Cosine","Cosecant","Cotangent"],"answer":3},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"If cos θ = 4/5, what is sec θ?","options":["4/5","5/4","3/5","3/4"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"math-trig-right-triangle","subject":"math","category":"Trigonometry","title":"Right-Triangle Trigonometry (SOH-CAH-TOA)","competency":"Using sine, cosine, and tangent to find missing sides and angles","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"The SOH-CAH-TOA mnemonic"},{"type":"list","items":["<b>SOH:</b> sin θ = Opposite / Hypotenuse","<b>CAH:</b> cos θ = Adjacent / Hypotenuse","<b>TOA:</b> tan θ = Opposite / Adjacent"]},{"type":"h","text":"Finding a missing side"},{"type":"example","prompt":"A right triangle has a 30° angle and a hypotenuse of 10. Find the side opposite the 30° angle.","work":"sin 30° = opposite / 10\nopposite = 10 × sin 30° = 10 × 0.5","answer":"5"},{"type":"h","text":"Finding a missing angle"},{"type":"p","text":"Use inverse trig functions (sin⁻¹, cos⁻¹, tan⁻¹) when you know two sides and need the angle."},{"type":"example","prompt":"A right triangle has an opposite side of 7 and a hypotenuse of 25. Find the angle θ.","work":"sin θ = 7/25 = 0.28\nθ = sin⁻¹(0.28)","answer":"θ ≈ 16.3°"},{"type":"tip","text":"Before applying any ratio, label the triangle's sides relative to the angle you're using — 'opposite,' 'adjacent,' and 'hypotenuse' change depending on which angle is your reference angle."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"A right triangle has a 30° angle and a hypotenuse of 16. Find the side opposite the 30° angle.","options":["4","8","8√3","16"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"A right triangle has a 60° angle and an adjacent side of 10. Find the hypotenuse. (cos 60° = 0.5)","options":["5","10","20","10√3"],"answer":2},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"A right triangle has an opposite side of 9 and an adjacent side of 12 relative to angle θ. tan θ = {blank}","accepted":["3/4","0.75"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"A right triangle has a side opposite θ of length 9 and a hypotenuse of length 41. Find θ to the nearest degree.","options":["9°","13°","22°","77°"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"A right triangle has a 45° angle and a hypotenuse of 8√2. Find the length of each leg.","accepted":["8"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which ratio should you use to find a missing hypotenuse if you know the angle and the ADJACENT side?","options":["sin θ=opp/hyp","cos θ=adj/hyp","tan θ=opp/adj","None of these"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"To find a missing ANGLE when two sides are known, you should use:","options":["sin, cos, or tan directly","The inverse trig functions (sin⁻¹, cos⁻¹, tan⁻¹)","The Pythagorean theorem only","The law of cosines only"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"math-trig-unit-circle","subject":"math","category":"Trigonometry","title":"The Unit Circle & Reference Angles","competency":"Coordinates on the unit circle, reference angles, and common angle values","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"The unit circle"},{"type":"p","text":"A circle of radius 1 centered at the origin. For any angle θ measured from the positive x-axis, the point where the terminal side crosses the circle is (cos θ, sin θ)."},{"type":"h","text":"Common angle values"},{"type":"list","items":["0°: (1, 0)","30°: (√3/2, 1/2)","45°: (√2/2, √2/2)","60°: (1/2, √3/2)","90°: (0, 1)"]},{"type":"h","text":"Reference angles"},{"type":"p","text":"A reference angle is the acute angle between the terminal side and the x-axis — it gives the trig value's magnitude, while the quadrant gives the sign."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Find the reference angle for 150°.","work":"150° is in Quadrant II\nReference angle = 180° − 150°","answer":"30°"},{"type":"example","prompt":"Find sin(150°) using its reference angle.","work":"Reference angle = 30°, sin 30° = 1/2\nSine is positive in Quadrant II","answer":"1/2"},{"type":"tip","text":"Memorize the sign pattern by quadrant: All positive in QI, Sine positive in QII, Tangent positive in QIII, Cosine positive in QIV — remembered by 'All Students Take Calculus.'"}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Find the reference angle for 210°.","options":["30°","60°","150°","210°"],"answer":0},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Find the reference angle for 300°.","options":["30°","60°","120°","300°"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"The coordinates of the point at 60° on the unit circle are (1/2, {blank})","accepted":["√3/2","sqrt(3)/2"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"In which quadrant(s) is tangent positive?","options":["QI only","QIII only","QI and QIII","QII and QIV"],"answer":2},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Find cos(120°) using its reference angle. (Reference angle = 60°, and cosine is negative in QII.)","accepted":["-1/2","-0.5"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the reference angle for an angle of 315°?","options":["45°","90°","135°","315°"],"answer":0},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which quadrant has ALL trig functions positive?","options":["QI","QII","QIII","QIV"],"answer":0}]},{"id":"math-trig-identities","subject":"math","category":"Trigonometry","title":"Trigonometric Identities","competency":"Pythagorean, reciprocal, and co-function identities","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Pythagorean identity"},{"type":"p","text":"sin²θ + cos²θ = 1, derived directly from the Pythagorean theorem on the unit circle. Two useful variations: 1 + tan²θ = sec²θ, and 1 + cot²θ = csc²θ."},{"type":"example","prompt":"If sin θ = 3/5 and θ is in Quadrant I, find cos θ.","work":"(3/5)² + cos²θ = 1\n9/25 + cos²θ = 1\ncos²θ = 16/25\ncos θ = 4/5 (positive in QI)","answer":"4/5"},{"type":"h","text":"Reciprocal identities"},{"type":"list","items":["sin θ = 1/csc θ","cos θ = 1/sec θ","tan θ = 1/cot θ"]},{"type":"h","text":"Co-function identities"},{"type":"p","text":"sin θ = cos(90° − θ), and cos θ = sin(90° − θ) — sine and cosine of complementary angles are equal."},{"type":"example","prompt":"If sin 40° = 0.643, find cos 50°.","work":"cos 50° = sin(90° − 50°) = sin 40°","answer":"0.643"},{"type":"tip","text":"The Pythagorean identity sin²θ + cos²θ = 1 is the single most useful identity — most other identities can be derived from it when you forget them."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"If sin θ = 5/13 and θ is in Quadrant I, find cos θ.","options":["8/13","12/13","5/12","13/12"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which is the Pythagorean identity?","options":["sinθ+cosθ=1","sin²θ+cos²θ=1","sin²θ-cos²θ=1","sinθ×cosθ=1"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"1 + tan²θ = {blank}","accepted":["sec^2(theta)","sec²θ","sec^2θ","sec^2 theta"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"If cos 25° = 0.906, what is sin 65°?","options":["0.906","0.423","0.5","1"],"answer":0},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"If cos θ = 7/25 and θ is in Quadrant I, find sin θ.","accepted":["24/25"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which is a correct reciprocal identity?","options":["sinθ=1/cosθ","tanθ=1/cotθ","cosθ=1/sinθ","secθ=1/sinθ"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"sin θ = cos(90° − θ) is known as a:","options":["Pythagorean identity","Reciprocal identity","Co-function identity","Double angle identity"],"answer":2}]},{"id":"math-trig-laws","subject":"math","category":"Trigonometry","title":"Law of Sines & Law of Cosines","competency":"Solving non-right (oblique) triangles","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Law of sines"},{"type":"p","text":"For any triangle, a/sin A = b/sin B = c/sin C. Use it when you know an angle and its opposite side, plus one more piece of information (AAS or ASA)."},{"type":"example","prompt":"In triangle ABC, angle A=40°, angle B=60°, and side a=10. Find side b.","work":"10/sin 40° = b/sin 60°\nb = 10 × sin 60° / sin 40° ≈ 10 × 0.866 / 0.643","answer":"≈ 13.5"},{"type":"h","text":"Law of cosines"},{"type":"p","text":"c² = a² + b² − 2ab·cos C. Use it when you know all three sides (to find an angle) or two sides and the included angle (to find the third side)."},{"type":"example","prompt":"A triangle has sides a=7, b=9, and included angle C=60°. Find side c.","work":"c² = 7² + 9² − 2(7)(9)cos 60°\n= 49 + 81 − 126(0.5)\n= 130 − 63","answer":"c = √67 ≈ 8.19"},{"type":"tip","text":"Use the law of sines when you have a matching angle-side pair (AAS or ASA); use the law of cosines when you have SSS or SAS — no matching angle-side pair available."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"In triangle ABC, angle A=50°, angle B=70°, and side a=12. Which law should you use to find side b?","options":["Law of sines","Law of cosines","Pythagorean theorem","SOH-CAH-TOA"],"answer":0},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"A triangle has sides a=8, b=10, and included angle C=45°. Which law finds side c?","options":["Law of sines","Law of cosines","Pythagorean theorem only","Cannot be found"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"The law of sines states: a/sin A = b/sin B = {blank}","accepted":["c/sin C","c/sinC"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"A triangle has sides a=5, b=6, c=7 (all three sides known, no angles). Which law would you use to find an angle?","options":["Law of sines","Law of cosines","Either works equally well","Neither can be used"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"In triangle ABC, angle A=30°, angle B=90°, and side a=6. Find side b (the hypotenuse) using the law of sines. (sin30°=0.5, sin90°=1)","accepted":["12"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"The law of cosines formula c² = a² + b² − 2ab·cos C becomes the Pythagorean theorem when:","options":["Angle C = 90°","Angle C = 0°","a = b","The triangle is equilateral"],"answer":0},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which combination of known parts is best solved with the law of sines?","options":["SSS","SAS","AAS","None of these"],"answer":2}]},{"id":"math-trig-elevation-depression","subject":"math","category":"Trigonometry","title":"Angles of Elevation & Depression","competency":"Setting up and solving real-world height/distance problems using right-triangle trig","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Definitions"},{"type":"p","text":"The angle of elevation is measured UPWARD from the horizontal to a line of sight. The angle of depression is measured DOWNWARD from the horizontal. When two observers at different heights look at each other, these two angles are equal (alternate interior angles on parallel horizontal lines)."},{"type":"example","prompt":"From a point 50 m from the base of a tower, the angle of elevation to the top is 35°. Find the tower's height.","work":"tan 35° = height / 50\nheight = 50 × tan 35° ≈ 50 × 0.700","answer":"≈ 35 m"},{"type":"example","prompt":"A lighthouse keeper 60 m above sea level sees a boat at an angle of depression of 20°. Find the horizontal distance to the boat.","work":"tan 20° = 60 / distance\ndistance = 60 / tan 20° ≈ 60 / 0.364","answer":"≈ 164.8 m"},{"type":"tip","text":"Draw the horizontal line FIRST in every elevation/depression problem — the angle is always measured from that horizontal, never from the ground or a wall directly, unless the horizontal happens to coincide with the ground."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"From a point 40 m from the base of a building, the angle of elevation to the top is 50°. Which ratio finds the height?","options":["height=40×sin50°","height=40×cos50°","height=40×tan50°","height=40/tan50°"],"answer":2},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"A person standing on a cliff 80 m high looks down at a boat with an angle of depression of 25°. Which ratio finds the horizontal distance to the boat?","options":["distance=80×tan25°","distance=80/tan25°","distance=80×sin25°","distance=80/sin25°"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"On level ground, the horizontal distance to the base of a vertical 30 m flagpole is 30 m. Measured from ground level, the angle of elevation to its top is {blank}°.","accepted":["45"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"If two observers at different heights look directly at each other, the angle of elevation from the lower one equals the angle of depression from the higher one, because:","options":["They are complementary angles","They are alternate interior angles on parallel horizontal lines","They are both always 45°","They are vertical angles"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"From a point 100 m from the base of a tower, the angle of elevation to the top is 30°. Find the tower's height. (tan30° ≈ 0.577)","accepted":["57.7","≈57.7","57.7 m"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"The angle of elevation is always measured:","options":["From the vertical line downward","From the horizontal line upward","From the ground to the observer","Between two objects directly"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"A ship’s crew member looks up at a lighthouse observer at an angle of elevation of 15°. What angle does the observer measure looking back at that crew member?","options":["Elevation of 15°","Depression of 15°","Elevation of 75°","Depression of 75°"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"math-trig-radians","subject":"math","category":"Trigonometry","title":"Degree–Radian Conversion","competency":"Converting between degrees and radians; common angle equivalents","estLesson":7,"estPractice":9,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"The conversion relationship"},{"type":"p","text":"180° = π radians. To convert degrees to radians, multiply by π/180. To convert radians to degrees, multiply by 180/π."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Convert 90° to radians.","work":"90 × (π/180)","answer":"π/2 radians"},{"type":"example","prompt":"Convert 3π/4 radians to degrees.","work":"(3π/4) × (180/π) = 3 × 180 / 4 = 540/4","answer":"135°"},{"type":"h","text":"Common equivalents to memorize"},{"type":"list","items":["30° = π/6","45° = π/4","60° = π/3","90° = π/2","180° = π","360° = 2π"]},{"type":"tip","text":"A radian measure is usually written as a fraction times π — if you see a plain decimal number without π attached, like '1.57,' it's already a numeric radian value, not degrees."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Convert 60° to radians.","options":["π/6","π/4","π/3","π/2"],"answer":2},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Convert 5π/6 radians to degrees.","options":["100°","120°","150°","300°"],"answer":2},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Convert 270° to radians (as a fraction times π): {blank}","accepted":["3π/2","3pi/2","3π/2 radians"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Convert 2π/3 radians to degrees.","options":["60°","90°","120°","240°"],"answer":2},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Convert 45° to radians.","accepted":["π/4","pi/4"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"How many radians are in a full circle (360°)?","options":["π","2π","π/2","4π"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"fillblank","prompt":"180° is equivalent to {blank} radians.","accepted":["π","pi"]}]},{"id":"math-stat-central-tendency","subject":"math","category":"Statistics & Probability","title":"Measures of Central Tendency","competency":"Mean, median, and mode — calculating and choosing the right measure","estLesson":8,"estPractice":10,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Mean"},{"type":"p","text":"The mean (average) = sum of all values ÷ number of values."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Find the mean of 4, 8, 6, 10, 12.","work":"Sum = 40\n40 ÷ 5","answer":"8"},{"type":"h","text":"Median"},{"type":"p","text":"The middle value when data is arranged in order. For an even number of values, average the two middle numbers."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Find the median of 3, 7, 9, 15, 20.","work":"Already ordered; the middle (3rd) value","answer":"9"},{"type":"example","prompt":"Find the median of 2, 4, 6, 8.","work":"Even count — average the middle two: (4 + 6) / 2","answer":"5"},{"type":"h","text":"Mode"},{"type":"p","text":"The value(s) that appear most often. A data set can have no mode, one mode, or multiple modes."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Find the mode of 2, 3, 3, 5, 7, 7, 7.","work":"7 appears most often (3 times)","answer":"7"},{"type":"tip","text":"The mean is sensitive to outliers (extreme values); the median is not. If a data set has an unusually large or small value, the median usually better represents the 'typical' value."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Find the mean of 5, 9, 11, 15.","options":["8","10","11","40"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Find the median of 6, 2, 9, 4, 11.","options":["4","6","9","7"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Find the median of 10, 20, 30, 40: {blank}","accepted":["25"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Find the mode of 4, 6, 6, 8, 8, 8, 10.","options":["6","7","8","10"],"answer":2},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"A data set has a mean of 20 with 5 values. If four of the values are 15, 18, 22, 25, find the fifth value.","accepted":["20"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which measure of central tendency is LEAST affected by an extreme outlier?","options":["Mean","Median","Both equally","Neither"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"A data set can have:","options":["Only one mode","No mode, one mode, or more than one mode","Always exactly two modes","A mode only if all values are different"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"math-stat-dispersion","subject":"math","category":"Statistics & Probability","title":"Measures of Dispersion: Range & Standard Deviation","competency":"Range and standard deviation — measuring how spread out data is","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Range"},{"type":"p","text":"Range = highest value − lowest value. Simple to compute, but sensitive to outliers."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Find the range of 12, 5, 18, 9, 22.","work":"22 − 5","answer":"17"},{"type":"h","text":"Standard deviation"},{"type":"p","text":"Measures the average distance of each data point from the mean. Steps: find the mean, find each value's deviation from the mean, square each deviation, average the squared deviations (the variance), then take the square root."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Find the standard deviation of 2, 4, 6, 8, 10.","work":"Mean = 6\nDeviations: −4, −2, 0, 2, 4\nSquared: 16, 4, 0, 4, 16 → sum = 40\nVariance = 40 ÷ 5 = 8\nSD = √8","answer":"≈ 2.83"},{"type":"tip","text":"A small standard deviation means the data is tightly clustered around the mean; a large standard deviation means the data is spread out widely."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Find the range of 8, 15, 3, 21, 11.","options":["12","15","18","21"],"answer":2},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the FIRST step in calculating standard deviation?","options":["Find the range","Find the mean","Square each value","Find the median"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Find the range of 45, 12, 38, 60, 27: {blank}","accepted":["48"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"A data set with a small standard deviation means the data is:","options":["Spread out widely","Tightly clustered around the mean","Impossible to average","Always symmetric"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Find the range of 10, 14, 18, 22, 26.","accepted":["16"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Variance is related to standard deviation by:","options":["Variance = SD × 2","Variance = SD²","Variance = √SD","They are always equal"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which measure of spread uses only the smallest and largest values?","options":["Range","Interquartile range","Standard deviation","Variance"],"answer":0}]},{"id":"math-stat-data-representation","subject":"math","category":"Statistics & Probability","title":"Data Representation & Interpretation","competency":"Reading and interpreting bar graphs, pie charts, line graphs, and histograms","estLesson":8,"estPractice":10,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Choosing and reading the right graph"},{"type":"list","items":["<b>Bar graph</b> — compares quantities across categories","<b>Pie chart</b> — shows parts of a whole as proportions of a circle","<b>Line graph</b> — shows a trend or change over a continuous variable like time","<b>Histogram</b> — shows the frequency of data within continuous numeric ranges (bins), bars touching, unlike a bar graph"]},{"type":"h","text":"Interpreting a pie chart"},{"type":"example","prompt":"A pie chart shows a school's students by grade level. Grade 7 takes up 90° of the 360° circle. What percent of students are in Grade 7?","work":"90 ÷ 360","answer":"25%"},{"type":"h","text":"Reading a histogram"},{"type":"p","text":"The height of each bar shows how many data points fall within that bin's range — read the y-axis carefully, and note that histogram bars touch (representing continuous data), unlike bar graphs."},{"type":"tip","text":"The key difference between a bar graph and a histogram: a bar graph compares separate categories (with gaps between bars), while a histogram shows the distribution of one continuous numeric variable (bars touch)."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"A pie chart shows Grade 8 taking up 72° of the circle. What percent of the total does Grade 8 represent?","options":["15%","20%","25%","30%"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which graph type is best for showing a trend over time, like monthly sales?","options":["Pie chart","Bar graph","Line graph","Histogram"],"answer":2},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"A pie chart's full circle represents {blank}°.","accepted":["360"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the main difference between a histogram and a categorical bar chart?","options":["Histograms only show percentages.","A histogram groups numerical values into intervals. A bar chart compares categories.","Bar charts require numerical intervals.","Both can display only time series."],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"A pie chart shows 'Sports' taking up 30% of student preferences out of 200 students surveyed. How many students prefer sports?","accepted":["60"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"In a frequency histogram with equal-width bins, what does the height of a bar represent?","options":["Categories being compared","The frequency of data within numeric ranges (bins)","Percentages of a whole","Trends over time only"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which type of graph would best compare the populations of 5 different cities at one point in time?","options":["Line graph","Bar graph","Histogram","Scatter plot only"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"math-stat-counting-principle","subject":"math","category":"Statistics & Probability","title":"The Fundamental Counting Principle","competency":"Multiplying choices across independent stages to count total outcomes","estLesson":8,"estPractice":10,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"The principle"},{"type":"p","text":"If one event can occur in m ways and a second independent event can occur in n ways, the two together can occur in m × n ways. This extends to any number of stages: multiply the number of choices at each stage."},{"type":"example","prompt":"A restaurant offers 4 appetizers, 6 main courses, and 3 desserts. How many different 3-course meals are possible?","work":"4 × 6 × 3","answer":"72"},{"type":"example","prompt":"A license plate has 3 letters followed by 4 digits. How many plates are possible if repetition is allowed?","work":"Letters: 26 × 26 × 26 = 17,576\nDigits: 10 × 10 × 10 × 10 = 10,000\nTotal: 17,576 × 10,000","answer":"175,760,000"},{"type":"tip","text":"Watch for the phrase 'without repetition' — if a letter or digit can't be reused, the number of choices shrinks by one at each subsequent stage instead of staying constant."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"A store sells shirts in 5 colors and 4 sizes. How many (color, size) combinations are possible?","options":["9","15","20","25"],"answer":2},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"A password consists of 2 letters chosen from A–Z followed by 3 digits from 0–9. Repetition is allowed. Which expression gives the total?","options":["26×26×10×10×10","26+26+10+10+10","(26×10)^5","26²+10³"],"answer":0},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"A meal consists of 1 of 3 soups, 1 of 5 mains, and 1 of 2 desserts. Total meal combinations = {blank}","accepted":["30"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"A 4-digit PIN is created using digits 0-9 with repetition allowed. How many PINs are possible?","options":["40","10^4","4^10","9×9×9×9"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"A café offers 6 drink options and 8 pastry options. How many (drink, pastry) pairs are possible?","accepted":["48"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"If repetition is NOT allowed, how many ways can 3 different books be arranged on a shelf with 3 available slots?","options":["3","6","9","27"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"The fundamental counting principle says that for independent stages of choices, you should:","options":["Add the number of choices at each stage","Multiply the number of choices at each stage","Subtract the smaller from the larger","Divide the total by the number of stages"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"math-stat-permutations-combinations","subject":"math","category":"Statistics & Probability","title":"Permutations & Combinations","competency":"Counting arrangements (order matters) vs. selections (order doesn't matter)","estLesson":10,"estPractice":12,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Permutations (order matters)"},{"type":"p","text":"The number of ways to arrange r items from a set of n distinct items: P(n, r) = n! / (n − r)!"},{"type":"example","prompt":"In how many ways can 3 of 5 runners finish 1st, 2nd, and 3rd place?","work":"P(5,3) = 5! / (5−3)! = 5! / 2! = 5 × 4 × 3","answer":"60"},{"type":"h","text":"Combinations (order doesn't matter)"},{"type":"p","text":"The number of ways to choose r items from a set of n distinct items, without regard to order: C(n, r) = n! / [r!(n − r)!]"},{"type":"example","prompt":"In how many ways can a committee of 3 be chosen from 8 people?","work":"C(8,3) = 8! / (3! × 5!) = (8×7×6) / (3×2×1) = 336/6","answer":"56"},{"type":"h","text":"Choosing which one to use"},{"type":"p","text":"Ask yourself: does the ORDER or ARRANGEMENT matter? If yes (rankings, seating, passwords), use permutations. If no (committees, groups, selections), use combinations."},{"type":"tip","text":"Combinations are always smaller than or equal to permutations of the same n and r, because C(n,r) = P(n,r) ÷ r! — dividing out the r! ways to arrange each selected group removes the 'order' factor."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"In how many ways can 4 of 7 finalists be awarded 1st, 2nd, 3rd, and 4th place?","options":["35","210","840","2401"],"answer":2},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"In how many ways can a committee of 4 be chosen from 10 people?","options":["210","720","5040","10000"],"answer":0},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"P(6,2) = {blank}","accepted":["30"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Does the order of selection matter in a combination?","options":["Yes, always","No, never","Only for large n","Only if r=n"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"How many ways can you choose 2 pizza toppings from 6 available toppings (order doesn't matter)?","accepted":["15"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which scenario requires a PERMUTATION (not a combination)?","options":["Choosing 3 books to bring on a trip","Selecting a committee of 5 from 20 people","Awarding gold, silver, and bronze medals to 3 of 8 athletes","Choosing 2 toppings for a pizza"],"answer":2},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"C(n,r) is related to P(n,r) by the formula:","options":["C(n,r)=P(n,r)×r!","C(n,r)=P(n,r)÷r!","C(n,r)=P(n,r)+r!","C(n,r)=P(n,r)-r!"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"math-stat-probability","subject":"math","category":"Statistics & Probability","title":"Basic Probability","competency":"Simple events, complements, and mutually exclusive vs. independent events","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Simple probability"},{"type":"p","text":"P(event) = (number of favorable outcomes) / (total number of possible outcomes), always between 0 and 1."},{"type":"example","prompt":"A bag has 4 red marbles and 6 blue marbles. Find the probability of drawing a red marble.","work":"4 / (4 + 6)","answer":"2/5"},{"type":"h","text":"Complement"},{"type":"p","text":"The complement of an event is everything that ISN'T that event. P(not A) = 1 − P(A)."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Using the same bag, find the probability of NOT drawing a red marble.","work":"1 − 2/5","answer":"3/5"},{"type":"h","text":"Mutually exclusive vs. independent events"},{"type":"p","text":"Mutually exclusive events cannot happen at the same time — P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B). Independent events don't affect each other's probability — P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B)."},{"type":"example","prompt":"A fair coin is flipped twice. Find the probability of getting heads both times.","work":"Independent events: 1/2 × 1/2","answer":"1/4"},{"type":"tip","text":"'Mutually exclusive' is about whether two outcomes of the SAME event can happen together; 'independent' is about whether two SEPARATE events affect each other's probability — don't confuse the two terms."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"A bag has 5 red marbles and 7 blue marbles. Find the probability of drawing a blue marble.","options":["5/12","7/12","5/7","7/5"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Using the same bag (5 red, 7 blue), find the probability of NOT drawing a blue marble.","options":["5/12","7/12","1","0"],"answer":0},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"A fair six-sided die is rolled. P(rolling a number greater than 4) = {blank}","accepted":["1/3","2/6","0.333…","0.333..."]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Two events that cannot happen at the same time are called:","options":["Independent","Complementary","Mutually exclusive","Conditional"],"answer":2},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"A fair coin is flipped independently 3 times. What is the probability of heads all three times?","accepted":["1/8"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"If P(A) = 0.3, what is P(not A)?","options":["0.3","0.7","1.3","0"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"A fair die is rolled and a fair coin is flipped separately. How are the events “roll a six” and “flip heads” related?","options":["Mutually exclusive events","Dependent events","Independent events","Complementary events"],"answer":2}]},{"id":"math-calc-limits","subject":"math","category":"Basic Calculus & Logic","title":"Limits: Concept & Evaluation","competency":"Understanding what a limit means; evaluating limits by substitution and factoring","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"What is a limit?"},{"type":"p","text":"The limit of f(x) as x approaches a value c describes the value f(x) gets close to as x gets close to c — it does NOT necessarily equal f(c), and f(c) doesn't even need to exist."},{"type":"h","text":"Evaluating limits by direct substitution"},{"type":"p","text":"If f is a 'nice' function (like a polynomial) at x=c, just substitute c for x."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Find lim(x→3) of (x² + 2x − 1)","work":"Substitute x = 3: 9 + 6 − 1","answer":"14"},{"type":"h","text":"Evaluating limits that produce 0/0 (indeterminate form)"},{"type":"p","text":"Factor the numerator and denominator, cancel common factors, then substitute."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Find lim(x→2) of (x² − 4)/(x − 2)","work":"Factor: (x−2)(x+2) / (x−2)\nCancel (x−2), leaving (x+2)\nSubstitute x=2","answer":"4"},{"type":"tip","text":"If direct substitution gives you 0/0, that's a signal to factor and simplify — it does NOT mean the limit doesn't exist; it usually means there's a removable hole in the graph at that point."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Find lim(x→2) of (x² + 3x − 1)","options":["9","10","11","13"],"answer":0},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Find lim(x→5) of (x² − 25)/(x − 5)","options":["0","5","10","25"],"answer":2},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Find lim(x→1) of (x² − 1)/(x − 1) = {blank}","accepted":["2"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Direct substitution in a rational limit gives 0/0. The numerator and denominator have a common factor that vanishes there. What should you try next?","options":["Conclude the limit doesn't exist","Factor and simplify, then substitute again","Set the limit equal to 0","Set the limit equal to 1"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Find lim(x→4) of (3x + 5)","accepted":["17"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"The limit of f(x) as x approaches c describes:","options":["The exact value of f(c)","The value f(x) approaches as x gets close to c","The derivative of f at c","The area under f near c"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Find lim(x→3) of (x² − 9)/(x − 3)","options":["0","3","6","9"],"answer":2}]},{"id":"math-calc-continuity","subject":"math","category":"Basic Calculus & Logic","title":"Basic Continuity","competency":"The three-part test for continuity at a point","estLesson":8,"estPractice":10,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"What does continuous mean?"},{"type":"p","text":"Informally, a function is continuous at x=c if you can draw its graph through that point without lifting your pencil — no holes, jumps, or breaks."},{"type":"h","text":"The three-part test"},{"type":"p","text":"f is continuous at x=c if all three conditions hold: (1) f(c) is defined, (2) lim(x→c) f(x) exists, (3) lim(x→c) f(x) = f(c)."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Is f(x) = (x²−4)/(x−2) continuous at x=2?","work":"f(2) is undefined (0/0) — fails condition 1 immediately","answer":"No, it has a removable discontinuity (a hole) at x=2"},{"type":"example","prompt":"Is f(x) = x² + 1 continuous at x=3?","work":"f(3) = 10, defined\nlim(x→3) f(x) = 10\nThe two are equal — all three conditions hold","answer":"Yes"},{"type":"tip","text":"Polynomials are continuous everywhere. Rational functions (fractions) are continuous everywhere EXCEPT where the denominator equals zero."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which condition is NOT part of the three-part continuity test?","options":["f(c) is defined","lim(x→c) f(x) exists","f is differentiable at c","lim(x→c) f(x) = f(c)"],"answer":2},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Is f(x) = (x² − 9)/(x − 3) continuous at x=3?","options":["Yes","No, f(3) is undefined","No, the limit doesn't exist","Cannot be determined"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"A polynomial function is continuous at {blank} (name the domain).","accepted":["every real number","all x","all real numbers"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Rational functions are discontinuous wherever:","options":["The numerator equals zero","The denominator equals zero","x equals zero","They are never discontinuous"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Is f(x) = 2x + 5 continuous at x = 10? Answer yes or no.","accepted":["yes"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"A function with a hole in its graph at x=c, where the limit exists but f(c) is undefined, is said to have a:","options":["Jump discontinuity","Removable discontinuity","Infinite discontinuity","No discontinuity"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"If lim(x→c) f(x) exists but does NOT equal f(c), the function is:","options":["Continuous at c","Discontinuous at c","Undefined everywhere","Always a polynomial"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"math-calc-derivatives","subject":"math","category":"Basic Calculus & Logic","title":"Derivatives: Definition & Basic Rules","competency":"The derivative as a rate of change; the power rule; evaluating a derivative at a point","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"What is a derivative?"},{"type":"p","text":"The derivative f'(x) gives the instantaneous rate of change of f at any point x — geometrically, the slope of the tangent line to the curve at that point."},{"type":"h","text":"The power rule"},{"type":"p","text":"For f(x)=xⁿ, f'(x) = n·x^(n−1). The derivative of a constant is 0, constants multiply through (if f(x)=k·g(x), then f'(x)=k·g'(x)), and the derivative of a sum is the sum of the derivatives."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Find the derivative of f(x) = x³ + 4x² − 2x + 7","work":"Apply the power rule to each term:\n3x² + 8x − 2 + 0","answer":"f'(x) = 3x² + 8x − 2"},{"type":"h","text":"Evaluating a derivative at a point"},{"type":"example","prompt":"Using f'(x) = 3x² + 8x − 2 from above, find f'(2).","work":"3(4) + 8(2) − 2 = 12 + 16 − 2","answer":"26"},{"type":"tip","text":"The derivative of a constant term always disappears (becomes 0) — it's easy to forget to drop a '+7' when differentiating, so double check every constant term is gone in your final answer."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Find the derivative of f(x) = x⁴","options":["4x³","x³","4x⁴","x⁵/5"],"answer":0},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Find the derivative of f(x) = 5x² − 3x + 8","options":["10x-3","10x²-3x","5x-3","10x+8"],"answer":0},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"The derivative of a constant, like f(x) = 12, is {blank}","accepted":["0"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Using f'(x) = 10x − 3 (from f(x) = 5x² − 3x + 8), find f'(4).","options":["37","40","43","47"],"answer":0},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Find the derivative of f(x) = x² − 6x + 1","accepted":["2x-6","2x - 6"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Geometrically, the derivative f'(x) represents:","options":["The area under the curve","The slope of the tangent line at x","The y-intercept","The maximum value of f"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the derivative of a constant multiplied by a function, k·g(x)?","options":["k·g'(x)","g'(x)","k+g'(x)","k²·g'(x)"],"answer":0}]},{"id":"math-calc-derivative-applications","subject":"math","category":"Basic Calculus & Logic","title":"Applications of Derivatives","competency":"Slope of the tangent line; basic rate-of-change problems","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Slope of a tangent line"},{"type":"p","text":"To find the slope of y=f(x) at a specific point x=a, find f'(x) and evaluate it at x=a. That value IS the slope of the tangent line at that point."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Find the slope of the tangent line to y=x²−3x at x=5.","work":"y' = 2x − 3\nAt x=5: 2(5) − 3 = 10 − 3","answer":"7"},{"type":"h","text":"Basic rate of change"},{"type":"p","text":"If a quantity is described by a function of time, its derivative gives the instantaneous rate of change of that quantity at any moment."},{"type":"example","prompt":"The height of a ball is given by h(t) = −5t² + 20t (meters, t in seconds). Find the ball's velocity at t=1 second.","work":"h'(t) = −10t + 20\nAt t=1: −10(1) + 20","answer":"10 m/s"},{"type":"tip","text":"'Rate of change' and 'derivative' mean the same thing in context — whenever a word problem asks how fast something is changing at an exact instant, you're being asked to evaluate a derivative."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Find the slope of the tangent line to y = x² + 4x at x = 2.","options":["6","8","10","12"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"The height of a ball is h(t) = −5t² + 30t. Find its velocity at t = 2 seconds.","options":["10 m/s","15 m/s","20 m/s","30 m/s"],"answer":0},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Find the slope of the tangent line to y = x³ at x = 2: {blank}","accepted":["12"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"If s(t) represents an object's position over time, then s'(t) represents its:","options":["Acceleration","Velocity","Distance traveled","Maximum height"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Find the slope of the tangent line to y = x² − 5x + 2 at x = 3.","accepted":["1"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"A ball's height is h(t) = −5t² + 20t. At what value of t is its velocity exactly 0 (the peak of its path)?","options":["t=1","t=2","t=4","t=10"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"What does a negative rate of change (negative derivative) at a point indicate?","options":["The function is increasing there","The function is decreasing there","The function has a maximum there","The function is undefined there"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"math-logic-propositions","subject":"math","category":"Basic Calculus & Logic","title":"Propositional Logic","competency":"Statements, negation, conjunction, disjunction, conditional, and biconditional","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Statements"},{"type":"p","text":"A proposition (statement) is a sentence that is either true or false, but not both. \"Manila is the capital of the Philippines\" is a proposition (true); \"What time is it?\" is not — it's a question."},{"type":"h","text":"The five basic connectives"},{"type":"list","items":["<b>Negation (¬p):</b> “not p” — true when p is false, and vice versa","<b>Conjunction (p ∧ q):</b> “p and q” — true only when BOTH are true","<b>Disjunction (p ∨ q):</b> “p or q” — true when AT LEAST ONE is true","<b>Conditional (p → q):</b> “if p then q” — false ONLY when p is true and q is false","<b>Biconditional (p ↔ q):</b> “p if and only if q” — true when p and q have the SAME truth value"]},{"type":"example","prompt":"Let p = 'It is raining' (true) and q = 'The ground is wet' (true). Evaluate p ∧ q.","work":"Both true → conjunction is true","answer":"True"},{"type":"example","prompt":"Let p = '2+2=5' (false) and q = 'The sky is blue' (true). Evaluate p → q.","work":"A conditional is false only when p is true and q is false\nHere p is false, so it's automatically true","answer":"True"},{"type":"tip","text":"A conditional p → q is considered true whenever p (the 'if' part) is false — this feels counterintuitive at first, but it's the standard logical convention: a false premise can't make a conditional false."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Let p = \"5 is an even number\" (false). What is ¬p?","options":["False","True","Undefined","Cannot be determined"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Let p = “Dogs are mammals” (true) and q = “Dogs are reptiles” (false). Evaluate p ∧ q.","options":["True","False","Undefined","Both"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Let p (true), q (false). Evaluate p ∨ q: {blank} (true/false)","accepted":["true"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"A conditional statement p → q is false ONLY when:","options":["p is true and q is true","p is false and q is false","p is true and q is false","p is false and q is true"],"answer":2},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Let p (false), q (false). Evaluate p ↔ q. Answer true or false.","accepted":["true"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which connective means \"p and q must BOTH be true\" for the statement to be true?","options":["Negation","Conjunction","Disjunction","Conditional"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"A sentence that is either true or false, but not both, is called a:","options":["Question","Proposition","Conjecture","Variable"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"math-logic-truth-tables","subject":"math","category":"Basic Calculus & Logic","title":"Truth Tables","competency":"Constructing and reading truth tables for logical connectives","estLesson":8,"estPractice":10,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Why truth tables?"},{"type":"p","text":"A truth table lists every possible combination of true/false values for the statements involved, and shows the resulting truth value of the whole expression for each combination."},{"type":"h","text":"Truth table for conjunction (p ∧ q)"},{"type":"p","text":"p ∧ q is true only in the one row where both p and q are true. All other rows (TF, FT, FF) are false."},{"type":"h","text":"Truth table for disjunction (p ∨ q)"},{"type":"p","text":"p ∨ q is false only in the one row where both p and q are false. All other rows are true."},{"type":"h","text":"Truth table for the conditional (p → q)"},{"type":"example","prompt":"Construct the truth table for p → q, and state which row(s) are false.","work":"TT → T\nTF → F\nFT → T\nFF → T","answer":"Only the row where p=T, q=F is false; all other rows are true"},{"type":"tip","text":"For n propositions in a truth table, you need 2ⁿ rows to cover every combination — 2 propositions need 4 rows, 3 propositions need 8 rows."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"How many rows does a truth table need for 3 propositions (p, q, r)?","options":["3","6","8","9"],"answer":2},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"In the truth table for p ∧ q, how many of the 4 rows result in TRUE?","options":["1","2","3","4"],"answer":0},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"In the truth table for p ∨ q, the row where p=False and q=False results in {blank} (true/false).","accepted":["false"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"In the truth table for p → q, the row where p=True and q=False results in:","options":["True","False","Undefined","Depends on r"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"In the truth table for p ∧ q, if p=True and q=False, what is the result? Answer true or false.","accepted":["false"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"How many rows does a truth table need for 2 propositions?","options":["2","3","4","8"],"answer":2},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"The disjunction p ∨ q is false in exactly how many of its 4 rows?","options":["0","1","2","4"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"math-logic-equivalence-reasoning","subject":"math","category":"Basic Calculus & Logic","title":"Logical Equivalence & Reasoning","competency":"De Morgan's laws, the contrapositive, and basic valid argument forms","estLesson":10,"estPractice":12,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Logical equivalence"},{"type":"p","text":"Two statements are logically equivalent if they have identical truth values in every row of a truth table — they always 'say the same thing' logically, even if worded differently."},{"type":"h","text":"De Morgan's laws"},{"type":"list","items":["¬(p ∧ q) is equivalent to (¬p ∨ ¬q)","¬(p ∨ q) is equivalent to (¬p ∧ ¬q)"]},{"type":"example","prompt":"Negate: 'It is raining AND it is cold.'","work":"Negate each and flip AND to OR","answer":"It is not raining OR it is not cold"},{"type":"h","text":"The contrapositive"},{"type":"p","text":"The contrapositive of p → q is ¬q → ¬p, and it is ALWAYS logically equivalent to the original conditional."},{"type":"example","prompt":"State the contrapositive of: 'If it is a square, then it is a rectangle.'","work":"Negate and swap: ¬q → ¬p","answer":"If it is not a rectangle, then it is not a square"},{"type":"h","text":"A basic valid argument form: modus ponens"},{"type":"p","text":"If p → q is true, and p is true, then q must be true. This pattern is always logically valid."},{"type":"tip","text":"Don't confuse the contrapositive (always logically equivalent) with the converse (q → p) or the inverse (¬p → ¬q) — those two are NOT guaranteed to share the same truth value as the original statement."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Negate: \"The exam is hard AND long.\"","options":["The exam is not hard OR not long","The exam is not hard AND not long","The exam is easy AND short","The exam is hard OR long"],"answer":0},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the contrapositive of \"If it rains, then the game is cancelled\"?","options":["If the game is cancelled, then it rains","If it doesn't rain, then the game isn't cancelled","If the game isn't cancelled, then it didn't rain","If the game is cancelled, then it didn't rain"],"answer":2},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Write the contrapositive of p → q: {blank}. Use ¬ for negation.","accepted":["¬q → ¬p","~q → ~p"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which statement is ALWAYS logically equivalent to a conditional p → q?","options":["Its converse (q→p)","Its inverse (¬p→¬q)","Its contrapositive (¬q→¬p)","None of these"],"answer":2},{"id":"q5","type":"reflect","prompt":"State the negation of: 'The sky is clear OR it is windy,' using De Morgan's law."},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"If \"p → q\" is true and \"p\" is true, what can you validly conclude?","options":["q is true (modus ponens)","q is false","p is false","Nothing can be concluded"],"answer":0},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Two statements are logically equivalent if:","options":["They use the same variables","They have identical truth values in every row of a truth table","They are both conditionals","One implies the other"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"math-algebra-1","subject":"math","category":"Word Problems (mixed review)","title":"Algebra & Word Problems","competency":"Linear equations · percent · ratio & work-rate problems · special products","estLesson":12,"estPractice":20,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Solving linear equations"},{"type":"p","text":"A linear equation is solved by isolating the variable: whatever you do to one side of the equation, you must do to the other, until the variable stands alone."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Solve for y: 4y + 5 = 21","work":"4y + 5 = 21\n4y = 21 − 5\n4y = 16\ny = 16 ÷ 4","answer":"y = 4"},{"type":"tip","text":"On the actual exam, plug your answer back into the original equation to check it quickly — it's faster than re-deriving the whole solution."},{"type":"h","text":"Percent problems"},{"type":"p","text":"Most percent problems on the exam are markups, discounts, or “what percent of” questions. Two formulas cover almost all of them: New amount = Original × (1 ± rate), and Part = Percent × Whole."},{"type":"example","prompt":"A ₱180 shirt is discounted by 25%. What is the sale price?","work":"Sale price = 180 × (1 − 0.25)\n= 180 × 0.75","answer":"₱135"},{"type":"h","text":"Ratio, proportion & work-rate problems"},{"type":"p","text":"For “if X workers take Y days, how long will Z workers take” problems, first find the total amount of work in worker-days (workers × days), then divide by the new number of workers. The total work stays constant — only how it's split changes."},{"type":"example","prompt":"5 painters can paint a fence in 8 days. At the same rate, how many days will 2 painters need?","work":"Total work = 5 × 8 = 40 painter-days\nDays needed = 40 ÷ 2","answer":"20 days"},{"type":"tip","text":"Set up ratio problems as equal fractions (a/b = c/d) and cross-multiply — it's more reliable under time pressure than reasoning proportionally in your head."},{"type":"h","text":"Special products"},{"type":"p","text":"Recognizing these patterns lets you expand or factor instantly instead of multiplying term by term: difference of squares (a + b)(a − b) = a² − b², and perfect square trinomials (a ± b)² = a² ± 2ab + b²."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Expand (x + 5)(x − 5)","work":"This matches (a + b)(a − b) with a = x, b = 5\na² − b² = x² − 5²","answer":"x² − 25"}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Solve for x: 3x − 7 = 20","options":["7","8","9","11"],"answer":2},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"A store marks up a ₱250 item by 20%. What is the new price?","options":["₱270","₱280","₱300","₱312.50"],"answer":2},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"If 3 workers finish a job in 12 days, how many days will 4 workers need at the same rate? {blank} days","accepted":["9"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which expression is equivalent to (x + 3)(x − 3)?","options":["x² − 9","x² + 9","x² − 6x + 9","x² + 6x − 9"],"answer":0},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Simplify: 5/10 + 1/4. Give the answer as a fraction in lowest terms.","accepted":["3/4"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"A train travels 240 km in 3 hours. At the same rate, how long will it take to travel 400 km?","options":["4 hours","5 hours","6 hours","7 hours"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"fillblank","prompt":"The ratio of boys to girls in a class is 3:5. If there are 40 students in total, there are {blank} boys.","accepted":["15"]},{"id":"q8","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is 15% of 240?","options":["24","30","36","42"],"answer":2}]},{"id":"eng-gram-parts-of-speech","subject":"language","category":"Grammar & Usage","title":"Parts of Speech & Determiners","competency":"The eight parts of speech; articles, demonstratives, possessives, and quantifiers as determiners","estLesson":8,"estPractice":10,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"The eight parts of speech"},{"type":"list","items":["<b>Noun</b> — names a person, place, thing, or idea","<b>Pronoun</b> — replaces a noun (he, they, this)","<b>Verb</b> — expresses an action or state of being","<b>Adjective</b> — describes a noun or pronoun","<b>Adverb</b> — describes a verb, adjective, or another adverb","<b>Preposition</b> — shows the relationship between a noun and another word (in, under, before)","<b>Conjunction</b> — connects words, phrases, or clauses","<b>Interjection</b> — expresses sudden emotion (Wow! Ouch!)"]},{"type":"h","text":"Determiners"},{"type":"p","text":"Determiners introduce a noun and specify which one or how many: articles (a, an, the), demonstratives (this, that, these, those), possessives (my, your, their), and quantifiers (some, many, few, each)."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Identify the determiners in: 'She borrowed three books from the library.'","work":"'three' is a quantifier; 'the' is an article — both introduce their nouns","answer":"three (quantifier), the (article)"},{"type":"tip","text":"'A' vs. 'an' depends on the SOUND that follows, not the letter: 'an hour' (silent h, vowel sound) but 'a university' (consonant 'y' sound despite the vowel letter u)."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Identify the part of speech of the underlined idea: \"She sang beautifully.\" (beautifully)","options":["Adjective","Adverb","Noun","Verb"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which is a demonstrative determiner?","options":["the","many","this","her"],"answer":2},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Choose the correct article: '{blank} honest man always tells the truth.' (a/an)","accepted":["an","An"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Identify the preposition: \"The cat is sitting under the table.\"","options":["cat","sitting","under","table"],"answer":2},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Identify the conjunction in: \"I wanted to go, but it started raining.\"","accepted":["but"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which word functions as a quantifier (determiner) in \"Few students passed the exam\"?","options":["students","passed","the","Few"],"answer":3},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which part of speech expresses strong emotion, like \"Wow!\" or \"Ouch!\"?","options":["Adverb","Interjection","Conjunction","Preposition"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"eng-gram-subject-verb-agreement","subject":"language","category":"Grammar & Usage","title":"Subject-Verb Agreement","competency":"Matching verbs to subjects, including tricky cases with modifiers or collective nouns between them","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"The basic rule"},{"type":"p","text":"A singular subject takes a singular verb; a plural subject takes a plural verb."},{"type":"h","text":"Tricky case: phrases between subject and verb"},{"type":"p","text":"A prepositional phrase or modifier between the subject and verb does NOT change the verb's agreement — ignore it and match the verb to the TRUE subject."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Choose the correct verb: 'The box of chocolates (is/are) on the table.'","work":"The subject is \"box\" (singular), not \"chocolates\" — ignore the prepositional phrase \"of chocolates\"","answer":"is"},{"type":"h","text":"Tricky case: collective nouns"},{"type":"p","text":"Collective nouns (team, family, committee, class) are usually treated as singular when acting as one unit."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Choose the correct verb: 'The committee (has/have) reached a decision.'","work":"\"Committee\" acts as one unit","answer":"has"},{"type":"tip","text":"When a sentence has a phrase like 'along with,' 'as well as,' or 'together with' between two nouns, the verb still agrees with the FIRST (main) subject only, not the added phrase."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Choose the correct verb: \"The list of items (was/were) misplaced.\"","options":["was","were"],"answer":0},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"In standard American English, choose the verb for a jury acting as a unit: “The jury (has/have) reached a unanimous verdict.”","options":["has","have"],"answer":0},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Choose the correct verb: 'A bouquet of roses {blank} (was/were) delivered this morning.'","accepted":["was"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Choose the correct verb: \"The players, along with their coach, (is/are) celebrating the win.\"","options":["is","are"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Choose the singular verb in formal edited English: “Neither of the answers (is/are) correct.”","accepted":["is"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which subject requires a PLURAL verb?","options":["Each of the boys","The team","The books on the shelf","The committee"],"answer":2},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"In \"The basket of apples (sits/sit) on the counter,\" what is the TRUE subject?","options":["apples","basket","counter","the"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"eng-gram-verb-tense","subject":"language","category":"Grammar & Usage","title":"Verb Tense Consistency & Sequence","competency":"Keeping verb tenses consistent within a sentence/passage; correct sequence of tenses","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Tense consistency"},{"type":"p","text":"Within a single sentence or a closely related set of sentences describing the same time frame, keep verb tenses consistent — don't shift from past to present without a clear reason."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Correct the inconsistency: 'She opened the door and sees a surprise party.'","work":"\"opened\" is past tense, so \"sees\" should also be past","answer":"She opened the door and saw a surprise party."},{"type":"h","text":"Sequence of tenses"},{"type":"p","text":"When describing an action that happened before another past action, use the past perfect (had + past participle) for the earlier action."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Choose the correct sequence: 'By the time the rescue team arrived, the hikers (already left / had already left).'","work":"The hikers leaving happened BEFORE the team arrived — an earlier past event needs the past perfect","answer":"had already left"},{"type":"tip","text":"'Had' + past participle signals that one past action happened before another past action — without it, two simple-past verbs in the same sentence can wrongly imply the actions happened at the same time or in a confusing order."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Correct the tense error: \"He finishes his homework and went outside to play.\"","options":["He finished his homework and went outside to play.","He finishes his homework and goes outside to play.","He will finish his homework and went outside to play.","No error"],"answer":0},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Choose the correct sequence: \"By the time we reached the theater, the movie (already started / had already started).\"","options":["already started","had already started"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Correct the tense: 'Yesterday, she walks to school.' → Yesterday, she {blank} to school.","accepted":["walked"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which sentence correctly uses the past perfect tense?","options":["She had eaten breakfast before she leaves.","She had eaten breakfast before she left.","She has eaten breakfast before she left.","She eat breakfast before she left."],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Correct the tense error: 'They were playing basketball when it starts to rain.'","accepted":["They were playing basketball when it started to rain.","they were playing basketball when it started to rain"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which relationship can the past perfect make explicit?","options":["An action happening right now","An action that will happen in the future","An action that happened before another past action","A general truth or habit"],"answer":2},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Identify the tense error: \"I have visited Cebu last year.\"","options":["'have visited' should be 'visited'","'last year' should be removed only","'have visited' is correct as is","There is no error"],"answer":0}]},{"id":"eng-gram-pronoun-agreement","subject":"language","category":"Grammar & Usage","title":"Pronoun Reference & Agreement","competency":"Matching pronouns to clear, correct antecedents in number and gender","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Pronoun-antecedent agreement"},{"type":"p","text":"A pronoun should agree with its antecedent and refer to it clearly. In contemporary standard English, singular they can refer to a person whose gender is unknown or to a person who uses they as a pronoun. It takes plural-form verbs, as in “they are.”"},{"type":"example","prompt":"Complete: “Every student must bring ___ own pencil.”","work":"“Every student” refers to each student individually. Singular “their” is standard here.","answer":"Every student must bring their own pencil."},{"type":"h","text":"Clear pronoun reference"},{"type":"p","text":"A pronoun should refer clearly to ONE specific antecedent — avoid ambiguous sentences where a pronoun could refer to more than one noun."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Identify the ambiguity: 'Maria told Elena that she had won the contest.'","work":"\"she\" could refer to either Maria or Elena","answer":"Ambiguous — 'she' could mean Maria or Elena; needs to be reworded for clarity"},{"type":"tip","text":"Indefinite subjects such as “everyone” take singular verbs. A later pronoun can still be singular “they,” as in “Everyone has brought their ID.”"}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Choose the sentence with correct pronoun agreement:","options":["Each of the girls brought their own lunch.","Each of the girls brought her own lunch.","Each of the girls brought its own lunch.","Each of the girls brought them own lunch."],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Identify the pronoun reference error: \"When Anna met Grace, she was already late.\"","options":["'she' is ambiguous — unclear if it refers to Anna or Grace","'met' is the wrong tense","'already' is misplaced","There is no error"],"answer":0},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Everybody must bring {blank} own umbrella. Supply only the possessive determiner or phrase.","accepted":["their","his or her","her or his"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which pronoun correctly agrees with the antecedent \"the committee\" (acting as one unit)?","options":["they","their","it","them"],"answer":2},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Identify the antecedent of “it” in “The book had lost its cover, but it was still readable.”","accepted":["the book","book"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which sentence has an AMBIGUOUS pronoun reference?","options":["Sarah gave Lily her notebook after class.","Sarah gave her own notebook to Lily.","Lily received a notebook from Sarah.","Sarah's notebook was given to Lily."],"answer":0},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which sentence uses singular “they” correctly?","options":["Someone left they notebook on the desk.","Someone left their notebook on the desk.","Someone left them notebook on the desk.","Someone left themselves notebook on the desk."],"answer":1}]},{"id":"eng-gram-parallel-structure","subject":"language","category":"Grammar & Usage","title":"Parallel Structure","competency":"Keeping items in a list, comparison, or pair grammatically consistent","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"What is parallel structure?"},{"type":"p","text":"Items joined in a list, comparison, or by a coordinating conjunction should use the same grammatical form — all nouns, all verbs in the same tense/form, or all gerunds, for example."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Correct the faulty parallelism: 'She likes reading, to write, and painting.'","work":"'reading' and 'painting' are gerunds, but 'to write' is an infinitive — make all three match","answer":"She likes reading, writing, and painting."},{"type":"h","text":"Parallel structure with correlative conjunctions"},{"type":"p","text":"Pairs like 'either...or,' 'neither...nor,' and 'not only...but also' require the same grammatical form on both sides."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Correct: 'She is not only intelligent but also works hard.'","work":"'intelligent' is an adjective, but 'works hard' is a verb phrase — match both","answer":"She is not only intelligent but also hardworking."},{"type":"tip","text":"Read the list or comparison items aloud, one at a time, right after the shared word or phrase that introduces them — if one of them sounds grammatically odd on its own, it's probably breaking the parallel structure."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which sentence is grammatically parallel?","options":["He enjoys hiking, swimming, and to fish.","He enjoys hiking, swimming, and fishing.","He enjoys to hike, swimming, and fishing.","He enjoys hiking, to swim, and fishing."],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Correct the faulty parallelism: \"The manager asked us to arrive early, finish the report, and that we should submit it by noon.\"","options":["...and to submit it by noon.","...and submitting it by noon.","...and we submit it by noon.","No correction needed."],"answer":0},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Use the base form of “throw”: “The coach wanted the players to run faster, jump higher, and {blank} farther.”","accepted":["throw"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which sentence correctly uses parallel structure with a correlative conjunction?","options":["She is either studying or she will sleep.","She is either studying or sleeping.","She either studies or will be sleeping.","She is either study or sleep."],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Correct the faulty parallelism: 'The report was clear, concise, and had good organization.'","accepted":["The report was clear, concise, and well-organized.","the report was clear, concise, and well-organized"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Parallel structure most often appears in sentences with:","options":["A single verb","Lists, comparisons, or correlative conjunctions","Only proper nouns","Passive voice"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which pair correctly demonstrates parallel structure?","options":["To err is human, forgiving is divine.","To err is human, to forgive is divine.","Erring is human, to forgive is divine.","Err is human, forgive is divine."],"answer":1}]},{"id":"eng-gram-modifiers","subject":"language","category":"Grammar & Usage","title":"Modifier Placement: Dangling & Misplaced Modifiers","competency":"Identifying and correcting dangling and misplaced modifiers","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Misplaced modifiers"},{"type":"p","text":"A modifier should be placed as close as possible to the word it describes. When it's in the wrong spot, the sentence can describe the wrong thing."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Correct the misplaced modifier: 'She almost drove her kids to school every day.'","work":"\"almost\" sits next to \"drove,\" implying she nearly drove them (but didn't) — it should modify \"every day\" instead","answer":"She drove her kids to school almost every day."},{"type":"h","text":"Dangling modifiers"},{"type":"p","text":"A dangling modifier describes a subject that isn't actually present in the sentence — the modifying phrase has nothing logical to attach to."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Correct the dangling modifier: 'Walking to school, the rain started pouring.'","work":"The rain wasn't walking to school — the sentence needs a human subject doing the walking","answer":"Walking to school, I got caught in the pouring rain. (or: 'While I was walking to school, the rain started pouring.')"},{"type":"tip","text":"To catch a dangling modifier, ask WHO or WHAT is performing the introductory action — if the subject right after the comma isn't logically capable of that action, the modifier is dangling."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Identify the sentence with a dangling modifier.","options":["Having finished the exam, Maria left the room.","Having finished the exam, the room felt quiet to Maria.","After Maria finished the exam, she left the room.","Maria left the room after finishing the exam."],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Correct the misplaced modifier: \"The waiter served sandwiches to the customers on paper plates.\"","options":["The waiter served sandwiches on paper plates to the customers.","The customers on paper plates were served sandwiches by the waiter.","On paper plates, the waiter served the customers sandwiches.","No correction needed."],"answer":0},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Correct: 'Running late for the bus, breakfast was skipped by Tom.' → Running late for the bus, {blank} skipped breakfast.","accepted":["Tom","tom"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"A dangling modifier occurs when:","options":["A modifier is too long","The word it's meant to describe is missing from the sentence","The sentence has no verb","The modifier is placed after the noun"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Correct the misplaced modifier so 'only' clearly modifies 'two slices': 'I only ate two slices of pizza, not three.'","accepted":["I ate only two slices of pizza, not three.","i ate only two slices of pizza, not three"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which sentence has a CORRECTLY placed modifier?","options":["Covered in chocolate, she gave the strawberries to her friend.","She gave her friend the strawberries covered in chocolate.","She gave covered in chocolate the strawberries to her friend.","Covered in chocolate, her friend received the strawberries from her."],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"The best strategy to fix a misplaced modifier is to:","options":["Delete the modifier entirely","Move the modifier next to the word it actually describes","Add more modifiers for clarity","Change the verb tense"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"eng-gram-sentence-boundaries","subject":"language","category":"Grammar & Usage","title":"Sentence Boundaries: Run-ons, Fragments & Comma Splices","competency":"Identifying and correcting run-on sentences, sentence fragments, and comma splices","estLesson":10,"estPractice":12,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Run-on sentences"},{"type":"p","text":"A run-on joins two complete sentences (independent clauses) without proper punctuation or a connecting word."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Correct the run-on: 'The rain stopped the sun came out.'","work":"Two complete sentences fused together — separate with a period, semicolon, or a comma+conjunction","answer":"The rain stopped. The sun came out. (or: 'The rain stopped, and the sun came out.')"},{"type":"h","text":"Comma splices"},{"type":"p","text":"A comma splice joins two independent clauses with ONLY a comma, no conjunction — this is grammatically incorrect."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Correct the comma splice: 'I was tired, I went to bed early.'","work":"Replace the comma with a period, a semicolon, or add a conjunction after the comma","answer":"I was tired, so I went to bed early."},{"type":"h","text":"Sentence fragments"},{"type":"p","text":"A fragment is an incomplete sentence — missing a subject, a verb, or expressing an incomplete thought."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Identify and correct the fragment: 'Because she was late.'","work":"This is a subordinate clause with no main clause attached — it doesn't express a complete thought on its own","answer":"Because she was late, she missed the bus. (attach it to an independent clause)"},{"type":"tip","text":"To test for a run-on or comma splice, check if you can split the sentence into two separate sentences that BOTH make complete sense on their own — if so, they need proper separation (period, semicolon, or comma+conjunction), not just a comma."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Identify the run-on sentence.","options":["I like coffee, but she prefers tea.","I like coffee she prefers tea.","I like coffee; she prefers tea.","I like coffee, and she prefers tea."],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Identify the comma splice.","options":["The store was closed, so we went home.","The store was closed, we went home.","The store was closed; we went home.","Since the store was closed, we went home."],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"reflect","prompt":"Correct the fragment by attaching it to a complete main clause: 'Although the weather was bad.'"},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which of the following is a sentence FRAGMENT?","options":["She ran quickly.","Running quickly to catch the bus.","She was running quickly.","Running quickly, she caught the bus."],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Correct the comma splice with the conjunction “so”: “He studied hard, he passed the exam.”","accepted":["He studied hard, so he passed the exam."]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"A run-on sentence occurs when:","options":["A sentence is too long","Two independent clauses are joined without proper punctuation or a conjunction","A sentence has too many adjectives","A sentence starts with 'because'"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which correction properly fixes the run-on \"She finished her work she went home\"?","options":["She finished her work, she went home.","She finished her work she, went home.","She finished her work, and she went home.","She finished, her work she went home."],"answer":2}]},{"id":"eng-gram-comparison-logic","subject":"language","category":"Grammar & Usage","title":"Comparison Logic","competency":"Comparative/superlative forms and logically complete comparisons","estLesson":8,"estPractice":10,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Comparative and superlative forms"},{"type":"p","text":"Use the comparative (-er, or 'more') to compare TWO things, and the superlative (-est, or 'most') to compare THREE OR MORE things."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Choose correctly: 'Of the two routes, this one is the (shorter/shortest).'","work":"Only two routes are being compared","answer":"shorter"},{"type":"h","text":"Logically complete comparisons"},{"type":"p","text":"A comparison must clearly state what is being compared to what — avoid comparing two things that aren't actually comparable (an illogical comparison)."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Correct the illogical comparison: 'The population of Manila is larger than Cebu.'","work":"This compares a \"population\" to a \"city\" (Cebu), not to Cebu's population","answer":"The population of Manila is larger than that of Cebu. (or: '...than Cebu's population.')"},{"type":"tip","text":"Watch for comparisons that drop a needed word like 'that of' or 'those of' — without it, you may be comparing a quantity or quality to an entire object or place instead of to the equivalent quantity/quality."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Choose correctly: \"Of all the students in class, Maria is the (smarter/smartest).\"","options":["smarter","smartest"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Correct the illogical comparison: \"The climate of Baguio is cooler than Manila.\"","options":["...cooler than that of Manila.","...cooler than Manila's.","Both A and B are correct.","No correction needed."],"answer":2},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Choose correctly: 'This is the {blank} (good) restaurant in the city.' (superlative)","accepted":["best"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which sentence has a LOGICALLY complete comparison?","options":["Her salary is higher than her sister.","Her salary is higher than her sister's.","Her salary is higher than her sister was.","Her salary is high than her sister's."],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Choose the correct comparative form: 'This bag is (heavy) than that one.'","accepted":["heavier"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"When comparing exactly TWO items, which form should you use?","options":["Superlative","Comparative","Either works the same","Neither"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Identify the error: \"The weather today is more hotter than yesterday.\"","options":["'more hotter' is a double comparative — should just be 'hotter'","'today' should be removed","'yesterday' is misplaced","No error"],"answer":0}]},{"id":"eng-gram-connectors","subject":"language","category":"Grammar & Usage","title":"Connectors & Transitions","competency":"Coordinating/subordinating conjunctions and transitional expressions","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Coordinating conjunctions"},{"type":"p","text":"FANBOYS (for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so) join two independent clauses of equal grammatical weight, usually with a comma before the conjunction."},{"type":"h","text":"Subordinating conjunctions"},{"type":"p","text":"Words like 'because,' 'although,' 'since,' 'if,' and 'while' introduce a dependent clause that relies on a main clause to form a complete sentence."},{"type":"h","text":"Transitional expressions"},{"type":"p","text":"Transitions like 'however,' 'therefore,' 'in addition,' and 'consequently' show the logical relationship between ideas (contrast, cause-effect, addition) — often set off by a semicolon before and a comma after when joining two independent clauses."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Choose the best connector (contrast): 'It started to rain; ___, we continued the game.'","work":"Need a transition showing contrast between the rain and continuing to play","answer":"however"},{"type":"example","prompt":"Choose the best connector (cause-effect): 'She studied hard; ___, she passed the exam.'","work":"Need a transition showing a result","answer":"therefore"},{"type":"tip","text":"Distinguish conjunctions from transitional expressions by punctuation: a coordinating conjunction like 'but' takes a comma before it, while a transitional expression like 'however' joining two full sentences takes a semicolon before it and a comma after it."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Choose the best connector (contrast): \"He wanted to go to the party, ___ he had too much homework.\"","options":["and","but","so","because"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Choose the best connector (concession/despite): \"___ the heavy traffic, we arrived on time.\"","options":["Because of","Despite","Due to","So that"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Choose the best transition (contrast): 'The exam was difficult; {blank}, most students passed.'","accepted":["however","nevertheless","nonetheless","still","even so","all the same","despite that"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which is a SUBORDINATING conjunction?","options":["and","but","although","or"],"answer":2},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Complete using the transition that means “for that reason”: “She saved money every month; ___, she was able to buy a car.”","accepted":["therefore","consequently","thus","hence","as a result","accordingly","for that reason"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which punctuation correctly joins two independent clauses with a transitional expression like \"however\"?","options":["Comma before, nothing after","Semicolon before, comma after","No punctuation needed","Period before, comma after"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which group of words makes up the coordinating conjunctions (FANBOYS)?","options":["for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so","because, although, since, if, while","however, therefore, moreover","in, on, at, by, with"],"answer":0}]},{"id":"eng-gram-sentence-improvement","subject":"language","category":"Grammar & Usage","title":"Sentence Improvement & Correct Usage","competency":"Choosing the clearest, most grammatically correct version of a sentence","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"What sentence-improvement questions test"},{"type":"p","text":"These items give a sentence (often with an underlined portion) and ask you to choose the best replacement — testing grammar, clarity, and conciseness all at once. The original version is often one of the choices, in case it's already correct."},{"type":"h","text":"A step-by-step approach"},{"type":"list","items":["Read the whole sentence first, not just the underlined part","Identify the specific error (agreement? tense? wordiness? redundancy?)","Eliminate choices that fix one error but introduce a new one","Choose the option that is both grammatically correct AND the most concise"]},{"type":"example","prompt":"Improve: 'Due to the fact that it was raining, the game was postponed.'","work":"\"due to the fact that\" is wordy; a concise conjunction works better","answer":"Because it was raining, the game was postponed."},{"type":"tip","text":"Prefer a concise revision when it preserves the intended meaning, tone, and necessary detail."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Choose the best revision of: \"The reason why he was late is because his car broke down.\"","options":["The reason he was late is that his car broke down.","He was late because his car broke down.","Both A and B are acceptable improvements.","No improvement needed."],"answer":2},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Choose the best revision of: \"She is a person who is always punctual.\"","options":["She is always punctual.","She is a person that is always punctual.","She, who is a person, is always punctual.","No improvement needed."],"answer":0},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Improve for conciseness: 'At this point in time, we need to decide.' → {blank}, we need to decide.","accepted":["Now","now"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Choose the best revision of: \"In my opinion, I think the plan will succeed.\"","options":["I think the plan will succeed.","In my opinion, the plan will succeed.","Both A and B remove the redundancy.","No improvement needed."],"answer":2},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Improve: 'He returned back to his hometown.'","accepted":["He returned to his hometown.","he returned to his hometown"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the main issue with \"The team, they won the championship\"?","options":["Subject-verb agreement","A redundant pronoun ('they' repeats 'the team')","Wrong tense","A dangling modifier"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"When two revisions are grammatically correct, what makes one a better choice?","options":["It uses longer words.","It preserves the intended meaning and removes unnecessary words.","It changes the original claim.","It always has the fewest characters."],"answer":1}]},{"id":"eng-gram-error-identification","subject":"language","category":"Grammar & Usage","title":"Error Identification (Spot-the-Error)","competency":"Locating the specific underlined portion of a sentence that contains a grammatical error","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"How error-identification items work"},{"type":"p","text":"The sentence is divided into several underlined parts (often labeled A, B, C, D), and you must identify WHICH part contains an error — or choose 'no error' if the sentence is correct as written."},{"type":"h","text":"A systematic scanning approach"},{"type":"list","items":["Check subject-verb agreement first (a very common error type)","Check pronoun agreement and reference","Check verb tense consistency","Check word choice (commonly confused words like affect/effect, its/it's, than/then)"]},{"type":"example","prompt":"Find the error: 'Neither of the students (A) have finished (B) their assignment (C) on time (D).'","work":"\"Neither\" is singular, so it should pair with \"has,\" not \"have\"","answer":"B — should be 'has finished'"},{"type":"tip","text":"Commonly confused word pairs (its/it's, their/there/they're, affect/effect, than/then) are frequent targets in error-identification items — memorize the distinctions, since these errors are easy to miss if you're only checking for 'obvious' grammar mistakes."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Find the error: \"Each of the boys (A) were given (B) a chance to speak (C) during the meeting (D).\"","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Find the error: \"The effect of the storm (A) were (B) felt across (C) the entire province (D).\"","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Identify the correct word: 'The medicine had a positive {blank} (affect/effect) on her recovery.'","accepted":["effect"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Find the error: \"She don't (A) like (B) going to (C) crowded places (D).\"","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":0},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Correct the confused word pair: 'Its raining outside.' (its/it's)","accepted":["It's raining outside.","it's raining outside"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Find the error: “By the time she arrived (A), the meeting (B) has already ended (C) and the room was empty (D).”","options":["A","B","C","D"],"answer":2},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"In error-identification items, when should you choose \"No error\"?","options":["When you're unsure which part is wrong","Only when you've checked every part and found none incorrect","Never — there's always an error","When the sentence is very long"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"eng-gram-sentence-completion","subject":"language","category":"Grammar & Usage","title":"Sentence Completion (Fill-in-the-Blank with Context Clues)","competency":"Using context clues, logic, and grammatical structure to complete sentences","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Reading for logical signals"},{"type":"p","text":"Sentence-completion items often contain signal words that tell you the logical relationship needed: 'although,' 'despite,' and 'but' signal a contrast; 'because,' 'since,' and 'as a result' signal a cause; 'and,' 'also,' and 'in addition' signal a continuation of the same idea."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Complete (signal: contrast): 'Although the lecture was long, the students remained ___.'","work":"\"although\" signals contrast with something you might expect (boredom/restlessness), so the blank should express the opposite","answer":"attentive (or a similar word meaning engaged/focused)"},{"type":"h","text":"Checking grammatical fit"},{"type":"p","text":"Beyond meaning, the word must also fit the sentence's grammar — the correct part of speech, correct verb tense, and correct number agreement."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Complete (past tense needed, signaled by 'before reaching'): 'The scientists ___ several experiments before reaching a conclusion.'","work":"Needs a past-tense verb","answer":"conducted (or 'performed', 'ran')"},{"type":"tip","text":"Before choosing an answer, reread the completed sentence in full — a word that fits the general meaning may still be grammatically wrong (wrong tense, wrong part of speech), so always check the whole sentence makes sense once your choice is plugged in."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Complete: \"Despite the heavy rain, the volunteers ___ working.\"","options":["stopped","continued","avoided","refused"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Complete: \"The company's profits declined; ___, management decided to cut costs.\"","options":["however","consequently","although","meanwhile"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Complete: 'Because the bridge was closed, drivers had to {blank}.' (a word meaning 'take a different route')","accepted":["detour","reroute"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Complete: \"The novel was both ___ and thought-provoking, keeping readers engaged until the last page.\"","options":["boring","captivating","forgettable","confusing"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Complete (past tense, meaning 'approved' or 'accepted'): 'The committee ___ the proposal after careful review.'","accepted":["approved","accepted","endorsed"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which signal word indicates a CONTRAST is coming in a sentence-completion item?","options":["and","because","although","therefore"],"answer":2},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which signal word indicates a CAUSE-EFFECT relationship?","options":["but","as a result","however","in contrast"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"eng-vocab-synonyms-antonyms","subject":"language","category":"Vocabulary","title":"Synonyms & Antonyms","competency":"Identifying words with similar (synonym) and opposite (antonym) meanings","estLesson":8,"estPractice":10,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Synonyms"},{"type":"p","text":"Words with similar (though rarely 100% identical) meanings. Context often determines which synonym is the BEST fit, since near-synonyms can carry different shades of meaning or formality."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Choose the best synonym for 'meticulous' in: 'The auditor was meticulous in checking every receipt.'","work":"Meticulous means extremely careful and precise about details","answer":"Thorough / precise"},{"type":"h","text":"Antonyms"},{"type":"p","text":"Words with opposite meanings. Watch for degree — some antonym pairs are direct opposites (hot/cold), while others are more about intensity (like/love vs. dislike/hate)."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Choose the best antonym for 'reluctant' in: 'She was reluctant to volunteer.'","work":"Reluctant means unwilling or hesitant; its opposite is being willing/eager","answer":"Eager"},{"type":"tip","text":"When a question asks for a synonym or antonym 'as used in the sentence,' the word may have multiple meanings — always check the sentence's context rather than picking the first definition that comes to mind."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Choose the best synonym for \"diligent.\"","options":["Lazy","Hardworking","Careless","Forgetful"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Choose the best antonym for \"generous.\"","options":["Kind","Selfish","Wealthy","Friendly"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Give a synonym for 'ancient.' {blank}","accepted":["old","aged","antique"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Choose the best antonym for \"abundant\" in: \"Water was abundant in the region.\"","options":["Plentiful","Scarce","Clean","Fresh"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Give an antonym for the word 'genuine.'","accepted":["fake","false","artificial","counterfeit"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which pair of words are synonyms?","options":["Brave / cowardly","Ancient / modern","Joyful / cheerful","Generous / stingy"],"answer":2},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Choose the best synonym for \"reluctant\" in: \"He was reluctant to speak in public.\"","options":["Eager","Hesitant","Confident","Loud"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"eng-vocab-analogy","subject":"language","category":"Vocabulary","title":"Analogy","competency":"Identifying and completing word-relationship analogies","estLesson":8,"estPractice":10,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Recognizing analogy types"},{"type":"p","text":"An analogy tests whether you can identify the relationship between a pair of words and apply that same relationship to a new pair. Common types include cause-effect, tool-to-user, category-to-member, and degree."},{"type":"example","prompt":"PILOT is to AIRPLANE as CAPTAIN is to ____?","work":"A pilot operates/is in charge of an airplane; apply the same relationship","answer":"SHIP"},{"type":"h","text":"Working through unfamiliar analogies"},{"type":"p","text":"State the relationship in a full sentence first ('A ___s a B' or 'A is a type of B'), then test each choice by plugging it into that same sentence pattern."},{"type":"example","prompt":"AUTHOR is to BOOK as COMPOSER is to ____?","work":"An author creates a book; apply: a composer creates a ____","answer":"SYMPHONY (or SONG/MUSIC)"},{"type":"tip","text":"If the relationship isn't obvious, try flipping the direction — sometimes 'A is a type of B' is easier to spot than 'B contains A,' even though they describe the same connection."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"FISH is to SCHOOL as WOLF is to ____?","options":["Forest","Pack","Cave","Hunt"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"THERMOMETER is to TEMPERATURE as SCALE is to ____?","options":["Fish","Weight","Number","Balance"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"mcq","prompt":"ACORN is to OAK as CHICKEN EGG is to _____.","accepted":["chick","bird"],"options":["Nest","Chick","Feather","Shell"],"answer":1},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"CHAPTER is to BOOK as SCENE is to ____?","options":["Actor","Play","Camera","Theater"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"reflect","prompt":"State, in a full sentence, the relationship shown in: 'FEVER is to ILLNESS.'"},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"ARTIST is to PAINTING as SCULPTOR is to ____?","options":["Museum","Statue","Chisel","Marble"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"TIMID is to BOLD as STINGY is to ____?","options":["Selfish","Generous","Wealthy","Cautious"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"eng-vocab-context","subject":"language","category":"Vocabulary","title":"Contextual Vocabulary (Word Meaning from Context)","competency":"Determining a word's meaning using surrounding context clues","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Types of context clues"},{"type":"list","items":["<b>Definition/restatement</b> — the meaning is directly explained nearby, often after a comma or 'that is'","<b>Example clue</b> — specific examples hint at the general meaning","<b>Contrast clue</b> — a word like 'but,' 'however,' or 'unlike' signals the opposite meaning","<b>Inference clue</b> — the overall situation implies the meaning, with no direct signal word"]},{"type":"example","prompt":"Determine the meaning of 'ubiquitous' in: 'Smartphones have become ubiquitous, appearing everywhere from classrooms to grocery stores.'","work":"The phrase \"appearing everywhere\" right after is a restatement/example clue","answer":"Present or found everywhere"},{"type":"example","prompt":"Determine the meaning of 'affable' in: 'Unlike his stern older brother, Marco was affable and quick to smile.'","work":"\"unlike\" signals a contrast with \"stern\" — so affable is roughly the opposite of stern","answer":"Friendly and easy to talk to"},{"type":"tip","text":"When no obvious clue word appears, look at the sentence's overall TONE and SITUATION — context clues aren't always a single nearby word; sometimes the whole scenario implies the meaning."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Determine the meaning of \"meager\" in: \"Despite his meager salary, he managed to save a little each month.\"","options":["Generous","Very small or insufficient","Steady","Unpredictable"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Determine the meaning of \"candid\" in: \"She gave a candid answer, holding nothing back, unlike her usual guarded responses.\"","options":["Vague","Honest and direct","Angry","Formal"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Determine the meaning of 'lethargic' in: 'After staying up all night, he felt lethargic and could barely keep his eyes open.' The word means {blank}.","accepted":["tired","sluggish","exhausted","lacking energy"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Determine the meaning of \"novice\" in: \"As a novice cook, she often needed to check the recipe twice.\"","options":["Expert","Beginner","Professional","Chef"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Determine the meaning of 'concise' in: 'The report was concise, summarizing the entire project in just two paragraphs.'","accepted":["brief","short","to the point"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which type of context clue directly explains a word's meaning nearby, often after a comma?","options":["Contrast clue","Definition/restatement clue","Inference clue","Example clue"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which signal word most likely introduces a CONTRAST context clue?","options":["Similarly","For example","Unlike","In addition"],"answer":2}]},{"id":"eng-vocab-diction-idioms","subject":"language","category":"Vocabulary","title":"Diction & Idiomatic Expressions","competency":"Word choice appropriateness (diction) and common English idioms","estLesson":8,"estPractice":10,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Diction"},{"type":"p","text":"Diction is word choice — using words that fit the context's tone, formality, and precise meaning. Formal writing avoids slang and overly casual phrasing; precise diction avoids vague words when a specific word is available."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Which is more appropriate for a formal report: 'The results were kind of surprising' or 'The results were unexpected'?","work":"'kind of' is casual/vague; 'unexpected' is precise and formal","answer":"The results were unexpected."},{"type":"h","text":"Idiomatic expressions"},{"type":"p","text":"An idiom is a phrase whose meaning can't be understood by taking each word literally — it's a fixed expression with a figurative meaning known through common usage."},{"type":"example","prompt":"What does the idiom 'to hit the books' mean?","work":"Not literal hitting — a common figurative idiom","answer":"To study intensively"},{"type":"example","prompt":"What does 'once in a blue moon' mean?","work":"A figurative idiom for rarity","answer":"Rarely; not very often"},{"type":"tip","text":"Idioms cannot usually be translated word-for-word into another language and still make sense — recognize them as whole fixed units with a figurative meaning, not as literal instructions."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"What does the idiom \"to break the ice\" mean?","options":["To literally break something frozen","To start a conversation and ease tension in a social situation","To end a friendship","To cause an argument"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which word choice is most appropriate for a formal academic essay?","options":["The experiment's results were awesome.","The experiment's results were significant.","The experiment's results were super cool.","The experiment's results were like, really good."],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Complete the idiom: 'It's raining cats and {blank}.'","accepted":["dogs"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"What does the idiom \"to spill the beans\" mean?","options":["To make a mess","To reveal a secret","To cook a meal","To lose an argument"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"reflect","prompt":"Explain the meaning of the idiom 'burning the midnight oil.'","model":"It means working or studying late into the night."},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which sentence gives the most specific evidence about wind conditions?","options":["It was breezy.","The weather was bad.","Wind gusts reached 70 kilometers per hour.","There was some wind."],"answer":2},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"An idiom's meaning is typically understood:","options":["By translating each word literally","Through common usage, as a fixed figurative expression","Only by native speakers of any language","By checking a dictionary definition of each word separately"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"lang-vocab-1","subject":"language","category":"Word Skills (mixed review)","title":"Vocabulary & Analogies","estLesson":10,"estPractice":15,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Reading analogies"},{"type":"p","text":"An analogy question gives you one pair of words and asks you to find a second pair with the same relationship. The first step is always to name the relationship in a short sentence before looking at the choices."},{"type":"list","items":["<b>Function</b> — KNIFE : CUT (a knife is used to cut)","<b>Part to whole</b> — PAGE : BOOK (a page is part of a book)","<b>Cause and effect</b> — GERM : DISEASE (a germ causes disease)","<b>Degree</b> — WARM : HOT (a weaker and stronger version of the same thing)","<b>Synonym / antonym</b> — HAPPY : GLAD, or HAPPY : SAD"]},{"type":"example","prompt":"HAMMER is to NAIL as NEEDLE is to ____?","work":"Relationship: a hammer is the tool used to drive a nail.\nApply the same relationship: a needle is the tool used to pull ___ through cloth.","answer":"THREAD"},{"type":"h","text":"Word roots as shortcuts"},{"type":"p","text":"When a word is unfamiliar, breaking it into its Latin or Greek root often gets you close enough to answer."},{"type":"list","items":["<b>bene-</b> (good) — benevolent, beneficial","<b>mal-</b> (bad) — malicious, malnourished","<b>-phobia</b> (fear) — claustrophobia, xenophobia","<b>omni-</b> (all) — omnipresent, omniscient","<b>-cred-</b> (belief) — credible, incredulous"]},{"type":"h","text":"Context clues for vocabulary-in-context"},{"type":"p","text":"When a question asks what a word means “as used in the sentence,” ignore what you already think the word means and look for a clue in the surrounding words: a restatement, an example, or a contrast signaled by words like “but” or “however.”"},{"type":"example","prompt":"“The manager's decision seemed arbitrary, but on closer look it followed a clear, documented policy.” What does arbitrary most likely mean here?","work":"The word “but” signals a contrast with “followed a clear, documented policy.”\nSo arbitrary must mean the opposite of that — not based on a clear rule.","answer":"random / not based on reason"},{"type":"tip","text":"If two answer choices are synonyms of each other, neither is usually correct — a well-written question has one clearly best answer, so eliminate both and choose from what's left."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Choose the word most nearly OPPOSITE to “frugal.”","options":["Thrifty","Extravagant","Modest","Cautious"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"DOCTOR is to PATIENT as TEACHER is to ____.","options":["School","Student","Lesson","Classroom"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"mcq","prompt":"The word “candid” most nearly means:","options":["Honest","Nervous","Reserved","Confused"],"answer":0},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Despite the heavy rain, the volunteers remained ____ in their effort to finish before nightfall.","options":["indifferent","steadfast","hesitant","forgetful"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"fillblank","prompt":"A person who is “taciturn” tends to talk very {blank}.","accepted":["little","rarely","seldom"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"PEN is to WRITER as BRUSH is to ____.","options":["Canvas","Painter","Paint","Easel"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"The word “ephemeral” is most nearly OPPOSITE to:","options":["Permanent","Brief","Fragile","Beautiful"],"answer":0},{"id":"q8","type":"short","prompt":"Give one synonym for the word “meticulous.”","accepted":["careful","precise","thorough","exact","fastidious"]}]},{"id":"fil-gram-bahagi-pananalita","subject":"filipino","category":"Gramatika at Wastong Gamit","title":"Mga Bahagi ng Pananalita","competency":"Pangngalan, panghalip, pandiwa, pang-uri, pang-abay, pang-ukol, pangatnig, pandamdam","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Ang walong bahagi ng pananalita"},{"type":"list","items":["<b>Pangngalan</b> — tumutukoy sa tao, bagay, lugar, o pangyayari (hal. guro, Maynila, pag-ibig)","<b>Panghalip</b> — humahalili sa pangngalan (hal. siya, ako, kanila)","<b>Pandiwa</b> — nagsasaad ng kilos o galaw (hal. kumain, tumakbo, nag-aral)","<b>Pang-uri</b> — naglalarawan sa pangngalan o panghalip (hal. maganda, malaki, masipag)","<b>Pang-abay</b> — naglalarawan sa pandiwa, pang-uri, o ibang pang-abay (hal. mabilis, kahapon, palagi)","<b>Pang-ukol</b> — nag-uugnay sa isang salita sa iba pang bahagi ng pangungusap (hal. sa, ng, para sa)","<b>Pangatnig</b> — nag-uugnay ng mga salita, parirala, o sugnay (hal. at, ngunit, dahil)","<b>Pandamdam</b> — nagpapahayag ng damdamin (hal. Naku! Aray! Wow!)"]},{"type":"example","prompt":"Tukuyin ang bahagi ng pananalita ng bawat salita: 'Mabilis tumakbo ang batang matalino.'","work":"mabilis — pang-abay (naglalarawan sa pandiwang tumakbo)\ntumakbo — pandiwa\nbata — pangngalan\nmatalino — pang-uri","answer":"mabilis=pang-abay, tumakbo=pandiwa, bata=pangngalan, matalino=pang-uri"},{"type":"tip","text":"Ang pang-uri ay naglalarawan ng PANGNGALAN, habang ang pang-abay ay naglalarawan ng PANDIWA (o ibang pang-abay/pang-uri) — madaling malito ang dalawa kaya tingnan kung anong uri ng salita ang nilalarawan."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Anong bahagi ng pananalita ang salitang 'maganda' sa pangungusap na 'Maganda ang bulaklak'?","options":["Pangngalan","Pang-uri","Pang-abay","Pandiwa"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Alin sa mga sumusunod ang HALIMBAWA ng pang-ukol?","options":["at","sa","masaya","kumain"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Ang salitang 'bahay' ay isang {blank} (bahagi ng pananalita).","accepted":["pangngalan"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Tukuyin ang pandiwa sa pangungusap: 'Kumanta nang malakas ang mag-aaral.'","options":["Kumanta","malakas","mag-aaral","nang"],"answer":0},{"id":"q5","type":"reflect","prompt":"Magbigay ng isang halimbawa ng pandamdam at gamitin ito sa isang maikling pangungusap."},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Ang salitang 'siya' sa pangungusap na 'Siya ay guro' ay:","options":["Pangngalan","Panghalip","Pang-uri","Pangatnig"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Alin ang gumaganap bilang pangatnig sa pangungusap na 'Nag-aral siya ngunit hindi siya nakapasa'?","options":["Nag-aral","ngunit","nakapasa","siya"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"fil-gram-aspekto-pandiwa","subject":"filipino","category":"Gramatika at Wastong Gamit","title":"Aspekto ng Pandiwa","competency":"Pangnagdaan, pangkasalukuyan, at panghinaharap na aspekto","estLesson":8,"estPractice":10,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Ang tatlong aspekto ng pandiwa"},{"type":"p","text":"Ang aspekto ng pandiwa ay nagpapakita kung ganap na, kasalukuyang ginaganap, o hindi pa nagaganap ang kilos."},{"type":"list","items":["<b>Pangnagdaan (Perpektibo)</b> — ganap na naganap ang kilos (hal. kumain, sumulat, nag-aral)","<b>Pangkasalukuyan (Imperpektibo)</b> — kasalukuyang ginaganap o nauulit (hal. kumakain, sumusulat, nag-aaral)","<b>Panghinaharap (Kontemplatibo)</b> — hindi pa naganap, balak lang gawin (hal. kakain, susulat, mag-aaral)"]},{"type":"example","prompt":"Ibigay ang tatlong aspekto ng pandiwang-ugat na 'basa' (bumasa).","work":"Pangnagdaan: bumasa\nPangkasalukuyan: bumabasa\nPanghinaharap: babasa","answer":"bumasa, bumabasa, babasa"},{"type":"example","prompt":"Tukuyin ang aspekto ng pandiwa sa pangungusap: 'Nag-aaral ang mga bata ngayon.'","work":"Ang salitang 'nag-aaral' ay may inuulit na pantig na senyales ng patuloy na kilos","answer":"Pangkasalukuyan (Imperpektibo)"},{"type":"tip","text":"Isang madaling paraan para makilala ang aspekto: kung inuulit ang unang pantig ng ugat (gaya ng 'ka-' sa 'kumakain'), kadalasan ito ay pangkasalukuyan (patuloy na nagaganap)."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Ano ang aspekto ng “sumulat” sa “Sumulat siya ng liham kahapon”?","options":["Pangnagdaan","Pangkasalukuyan","Panghinaharap","Walang aspekto"],"answer":0},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Ano ang aspekto ng pandiwang 'kakain'?","options":["Pangnagdaan","Pangkasalukuyan","Panghinaharap","Walang aspekto"],"answer":2},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Ang pangkasalukuyang anyo ng pandiwang 'tumakbo' ay {blank}.","accepted":["tumatakbo"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Piliin ang tamang panghinaharap na anyo ng 'maglaba':","options":["Naglaba","Naglalaba","Maglalaba","Laba"],"answer":2},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Isulat ang pangnagdaang anyo ng pandiwang 'magluto.'","accepted":["nagluto"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Ang pandiwang 'nagsasalita' sa pangungusap na 'Nagsasalita siya sa harap ng klase' ay nasa anong aspekto?","options":["Pangnagdaan","Pangkasalukuyan","Panghinaharap","Wala sa nabanggit"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Alin ang pandiwang nasa aspektong naganap sa pangungusap na “Kumanta siya kahapon”?","options":["Kumanta","Kumakanta","Kakanta","Kumakanta pa"],"answer":0}]},{"id":"fil-gram-ayos-pangungusap","subject":"filipino","category":"Gramatika at Wastong Gamit","title":"Ayos ng Pangungusap","competency":"Karaniwang ayos (paurong) at di-karaniwang ayos (may pananda na 'ay')","estLesson":8,"estPractice":10,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Karaniwang ayos"},{"type":"p","text":"Sa karaniwang ayos ng pangungusap sa Filipino, ang PANAGURI ang nauuna, sinusundan ng SIMUNO. Halimbawa: 'Kumain ang bata.' (Panaguri: Kumain; Simuno: ang bata)"},{"type":"h","text":"Di-karaniwang ayos"},{"type":"p","text":"Dito, ang SIMUNO ang nauuna, sinusundan ng pananda na 'ay' bago ang PANAGURI. Halimbawa: 'Ang bata ay kumain.' Ginagamit ito para bigyang-diin ang paksa o sa pormal na pagsulat."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Baguhin sa di-karaniwang ayos ang pangungusap: 'Nagluto ang nanay ng adobo.'","work":"Simuno muna, pagkatapos ay 'ay', pagkatapos ang panaguri","answer":"Ang nanay ay nagluto ng adobo."},{"type":"tip","text":"Ang pananda na 'ay' ang pinakamadaling senyales ng di-karaniwang ayos — kung wala itong 'ay' bago ang pandiwa, malamang karaniwang ayos ang gamit."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Alin ang HALIMBAWA ng karaniwang ayos?","options":["Ang guro ay nagturo.","Nagturo ang guro.","Siya ay guro.","Ang bata ay matalino."],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Alin ang HALIMBAWA ng di-karaniwang ayos?","options":["Kumanta ang mag-aaral.","Ang mag-aaral ay kumanta.","Tumakbo si Pedro.","Bumili siya ng tinapay."],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Baguhin sa di-karaniwang ayos: 'Bumili ang lalaki ng bulaklak.' → {blank}","accepted":["Ang lalaki ay bumili ng bulaklak.","ang lalaki ay bumili ng bulaklak"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Sa karaniwang ayos, alin ang unang bahagi ng pangungusap?","options":["Ang simuno","Ang panaguri","Ang pananda na 'ay'","Ang pang-uri"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Baguhin sa karaniwang ayos: 'Ang mga bata ay naglalaro sa parke.'","accepted":["Naglalaro ang mga bata sa parke.","naglalaro ang mga bata sa parke"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Ang salitang 'ay' sa di-karaniwang ayos ay tinatawag na:","options":["Pang-uri","Pananda","Pandiwa","Pang-ukol"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Bakit ginagamit ang di-karaniwang ayos?","options":["Para sa impormal na usapan lamang","Para bigyang-diin ang simuno o sa pormal na pagsulat","Dahil mas mahaba ito","Wala itong dahilan"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"fil-gram-panghalip-panao","subject":"filipino","category":"Gramatika at Wastong Gamit","title":"Panghalip Panao at mga Kaso Nito","competency":"Ang-kaso (palagyo), Ng-kaso (paari), at Sa-kaso (palayon)","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Tatlong kaso ng panghalip panao"},{"type":"list","items":["<b>Palagyo (Ang-kaso)</b> — ginagamit bilang simuno ng pangungusap (hal. ako, ikaw, siya, kami, tayo, kayo, sila)","<b>Paari (Ng-kaso)</b> — nagpapahiwatig ng pagmamay-ari o pagganap ng kilos (hal. ko, mo, niya, namin, natin, ninyo, nila)","<b>Palayon (Sa-kaso)</b> — nagpapahiwatig ng direksyon o taong tinutungo ng kilos (hal. sa akin, sa iyo, sa kaniya, sa amin, sa atin, sa inyo, sa kanila)"]},{"type":"example","prompt":"Piliin ang tamang panghalip (palagyo): '___ ang bumili ng regalo.'","work":"Kailangan ng ang-kaso dahil ito ang simuno","answer":"Siya"},{"type":"example","prompt":"Piliin ang tamang panghalip (palayon): 'Ibinigay niya ang libro ___.'","work":"Kailangan ng sa-kaso dahil ito ang tinuturuan ng kilos","answer":"sa akin"},{"type":"tip","text":"Kilalanin ang kaso sa tungkulin nito sa pangungusap: kung SIMUNO — palagyo; kung nagpapakita ng PAGMAMAY-ARI — paari; kung TINUTUNGO ng kilos — palayon."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Alin ang HALIMBAWA ng panghalip sa Palagyo (Ang-kaso)?","options":["ko","siya","sa kaniya","niya"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Alin ang HALIMBAWA ng panghalip sa Paari (Ng-kaso)?","options":["ako","niya","sa amin","tayo"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Punan ng panghalip na nasa anyong NG at tumutukoy sa nagsasalita: “Ito ang bahay {blank}.”","accepted":["ko"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Sa pangungusap na 'Sumulat siya sa akin,' ang salitang 'sa akin' ay panghalip sa anong kaso?","options":["Palagyo","Paari","Palayon","Walang kaso"],"answer":2},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Isulat ang panghalip sa Palagyo (unang panauhan, isahan).","accepted":["ako"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Ang panghalip na 'natin' ay nasa anong kaso?","options":["Palagyo","Paari","Palayon","Wala sa tatlo"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Alin ang tamang gamit ng panghalip sa Palayon?","options":["Kumain siya.","Ito ay kaniya.","Ibinigay ko sa kaniya ang libro.","Siya ay masipag."],"answer":2}]},{"id":"fil-gram-bantas-baybay","subject":"filipino","category":"Gramatika at Wastong Gamit","title":"Bantas at Wastong Baybay","competency":"Wastong gamit ng bantas at ispeling, kasama ang 2013 Ortograpiyang Filipino ng KWF","estLesson":10,"estPractice":12,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"2013 na Ortograpiyang Filipino ng KWF"},{"type":"p","text":"Base sa Ortograpiyang Filipino (2013) ng Komisyon sa Wikang Filipino (KWF), binubuo ng 28 titik ang alpabetong Filipino — ang 26 titik ng alpabetong Ingles kasama ang Ñ at Ng. Pinapayagan ang mga letrang c, f, j, q, v, x, z sa mga hiram na salita (lalo na sa mga pangngalang pantangi at teknikal na termino), bagaman mas ginustong gamitin ang katumbas na Filipino spelling kung mayroon (hal. iskedyul sa halip na schedule)."},{"type":"h","text":"Gitling (-)"},{"type":"p","text":"Ginagamit ang gitling sa: (1) pag-uulit ng salita (hal. araw-araw), (2) pagdugtong ng unlapi sa salitang may unang malaking titik o numero (hal. pang-Pilipino, i-10), at (3) ilang salitang may magkalapit na katinig-patinig na maaaring malito kung isasama (hal. pag-aaral)."},{"type":"h","text":"Kuwit (,) at iba pang bantas"},{"type":"p","text":"Ginagamit ang kuwit sa pagitan ng mga bagay sa isang serye, bago ang pangatnig na 'ngunit/subalit' sa compound na pangungusap, at pagkatapos ng panimulang parirala."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Ituwid ang maling baybay: 'Pumunta kami sa palengke, parke, at eskwela.'","work":"Ang 'eskwela' ay hindi tamang baybay ayon sa ortograpiyang Filipino","answer":"eskuwela (o paaralan)"},{"type":"tip","text":"Kapag hindi sigurado sa tamang baybay ng isang hiram na salita, alalahanin ang panuntunan ng KWF: mas gusto ang salitang naka-Filipino spelling (gaya ng 'kompyuter,' hindi 'computer') kaysa sa orihinal na banyagang anyo."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Ayon sa 2013 Ortograpiyang Filipino ng KWF, ilang titik ang binubuo ng modernong alpabetong Filipino?","options":["26","27","28","30"],"answer":2},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Alin sa mga sumusunod ang TAMANG baybay?","options":["Iskedyul","Schedule","Sked-yul","Iskejul"],"answer":0},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Piliin ang tamang gamit ng gitling (repetitive form ng 'araw'): 'Ang mga bata ay naglalaro sa {blank}.'","accepted":["araw-araw"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Alin sa mga sumusunod ang dalawang karagdagang titik sa alpabetong Filipino na wala sa alpabetong Ingles?","options":["Ñ at Ng","C at F","J at Q","V at X"],"answer":0},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Isulat ang wastong Filipino spelling ng salitang Ingles na 'computer.'","accepted":["kompyuter"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Alin ang isang sitwasyong nangangailangan ng gitling sa pagitan ng unlapi at kasunod na salita o bilang?","options":["Kapag ang salitang-ugat ay nagsisimula sa malaking titik o numero","Kapag ang salita ay mahaba","Palagi, sa lahat ng unlapi","Hindi na ginagamit ang gitling sa modernong ortograpiya"],"answer":0},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Aling bantas ang ginagamit bago ang 'ngunit' sa isang compound na pangungusap?","options":["Tuldok","Kuwit","Tandang pananong","Gitling"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"fil-gram-talasalitaan","subject":"filipino","category":"Gramatika at Wastong Gamit","title":"Paggamit ng Salita sa Konteksto","competency":"Pag-unawa sa kahulugan ng salita batay sa konteksto ng pangungusap","estLesson":8,"estPractice":10,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Pahiwatig mula sa konteksto"},{"type":"p","text":"Kadalasan, malalaman ang kahulugan ng isang hindi pamilyar na salita sa pamamagitan ng pagtingin sa mga salitang nakapaligid dito — mga halimbawa, kasingkahulugan, o kasalungat na pahiwatig sa loob ng pangungusap o parapo."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Ano ang kahulugan ng salitang 'mapagpakumbaba' batay sa: 'Bagamat siya ang pinakamahusay sa klase, mapagpakumbaba pa rin siyang tumanggap ng tulong mula sa iba'?","work":"Ang 'bagamat' ay nagpapahiwatig ng pagsalungat — kahit magaling, nananatiling hindi mayabang","answer":"Hindi mayabang; may kababaang-loob"},{"type":"h","text":"Salitang may maraming kahulugan (polysemous words)"},{"type":"p","text":"Maingat na basahin ang buong pangungusap dahil maraming salita sa Filipino ang may iba't ibang kahulugan depende sa konteksto (hal. 'baba' bilang bahagi ng mukha o bilang kilos na 'pagbaba')."},{"type":"tip","text":"Kapag may salitang hindi pamilyar, huwag agad tumigil — ipagpatuloy munang basahin ang buong pangungusap o parapo dahil kadalasan doon matatagpuan ang pahiwatig sa kahulugan."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Ano ang ibig sabihin ng 'masinop' sa: 'Masinop siyang mag-ayos ng kaniyang mga gamit bago matulog'?","options":["Magulo","Maayos at maingat","Mabilis","Tamad"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Batay sa: 'Kahit hirap na hirap, hindi siya sumuko — talagang matiyaga siya,' ano ang ibig sabihin ng 'matiyaga'?","options":["Madaling sumuko","Palaaway","Nagtitiis at nagpupursige","Tamad"],"answer":2},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Ang salitang 'tahimik' sa 'Tahimik ang silid, walang ingay na naririnig' ay nangangahulugang {blank}.","accepted":["walang ingay","payapa","walang maririnig na ingay"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Ano ang kahulugan ng 'maramot' batay sa: 'Ayaw niyang magbigay kahit kaunti — talagang maramot siya sa pera'?","options":["Mapagbigay","Hindi gustong magbahagi","Masaya","Mayaman"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"reflect","prompt":"Ibigay ang kahulugan ng 'magaan ang loob' batay sa: 'Kahit malaki ang problema, magaan ang loob niya dahil alam niyang tutulungan siya ng pamilya.'"},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Aling pahiwatig sa pangungusap ang tumutulong maunawaan ang kahulugan ng isang salita?","options":["Ang bilang ng mga titik nito","Mga kasingkahulugan o kasalungat sa parehong pangungusap","Ang unang titik nito","Kung ito ay pangngalan o hindi"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Ano ang tinatawag na salitang may higit sa isang kahulugan depende sa gamit?","options":["Kasingkahulugan","Kasalungat","Salitang may maraming kahulugan","Idyoma"],"answer":2}]},{"id":"read-main-idea","subject":"reading","category":"Reading Skills","title":"Main Idea Identification","competency":"Distinguishing the passage's central idea from its supporting details","estLesson":8,"estPractice":10,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"What is the main idea?"},{"type":"p","text":"The main idea is the central point the ENTIRE passage supports — it should be broad enough to cover every paragraph, but specific enough that it's actually discussed (not just a vague topic label)."},{"type":"h","text":"Strategy: the 'umbrella' test"},{"type":"p","text":"After reading, ask: does this statement cover the whole passage like an umbrella, or does it only fit one paragraph? A true main-idea choice should NOT be contradicted or left out by any part of the passage."},{"type":"example","prompt":"'Cats sleep an average of 12 to 16 hours a day. Kittens and older cats tend to sleep even more. This isn't laziness — much of a cat's sleep is light rest that lets it stay alert to sounds and movement, ready to react quickly if needed. Scientists believe this pattern developed from their history as both hunters and potential prey in the wild.' What is the main idea?","work":"Every sentence supports one point: cats' long sleep pattern isn't laziness, but a trait shaped by their evolutionary role as hunter and prey","answer":"A cat's long sleeping pattern is not laziness, but an evolved trait related to being both predator and prey."},{"type":"tip","text":"Eliminate main-idea choices that are true statements but only describe ONE paragraph or detail — the correct answer must summarize the passage as a whole."}],"passage":"The jeepney has been a fixture of Philippine streets since the years following World War II, when surplus American military jeeps were stripped down, lengthened, and decorated to carry passengers along fixed routes. Over time, the vehicle became more than transportation — its chrome ornaments, painted murals, and hand-lettered route signs turned each jeepney into a rolling piece of folk art, and the vehicle came to symbolize Filipino resourcefulness and creativity. In recent years, however, the government has pushed a modernization program requiring operators to replace aging jeepneys with vehicles that meet stricter safety and emissions standards. Many drivers and small operators have opposed the shift, citing the high cost of new vehicles and the loss of a cultural icon, while supporters argue that modernization is necessary to reduce pollution and improve road safety. The debate reflects a broader tension in the country: balancing the preservation of a cherished tradition against the practical demands of a growing, modernizing transportation system.","questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the main idea of the passage?","options":["Jeepneys were originally made from American military jeeps.","The jeepney's history, cultural significance, and the current debate over modernizing it reflect a tension between tradition and progress.","Jeepney drivers oppose the modernization program.","Modernization will reduce pollution in the Philippines."],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which sentence from the passage BEST supports the main idea?","options":["The jeepney has been a fixture of Philippine streets since the years following World War II.","The debate reflects a broader tension in the country: balancing the preservation of a cherished tradition against the practical demands of a growing, modernizing transportation system.","Many drivers and small operators have opposed the shift.","...chrome ornaments, painted murals, and hand-lettered route signs..."],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"According to the passage, jeepneys originated from surplus American {blank} after World War II.","accepted":["military jeeps","jeeps"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which of the following is a supporting DETAIL rather than the main idea?","options":["The overall tension between tradition and modernization","The jeepney symbolizes Filipino creativity and resourcefulness","The passage's central argument","The passage's overall structure"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"reflect","prompt":"In one sentence, state the main idea of the passage in your own words."},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why do supporters favor the jeepney modernization program, according to the passage?","options":["To preserve cultural heritage","To reduce pollution and improve road safety","To lower vehicle costs for operators","To increase the number of jeepney routes"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which title would best fit this passage?","options":["The History of World War II Vehicles","The Jeepney: Cultural Icon Facing a Modern Crossroads","How to Modernize Public Transportation","Filipino Folk Art Traditions"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"read-supporting-details","subject":"reading","category":"Reading Skills","title":"Supporting Details vs. Central Idea","competency":"Distinguishing details that support the central idea from the central idea itself","estLesson":8,"estPractice":10,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"The relationship between details and the central idea"},{"type":"p","text":"Supporting details are the specific facts, statistics, examples, or explanations that DEVELOP or PROVE the central idea. The central idea is the 'so what' — the point the details are there to support."},{"type":"h","text":"Strategy: ask 'why is this detail here?'"},{"type":"p","text":"For any given sentence in a passage, ask what larger point it's helping to prove. If you can't connect it to a larger point, it may be a minor detail rather than the central idea itself."},{"type":"example","prompt":"'The Philippine eagle can grow to over a meter tall, with a wingspan reaching two meters. It typically lays only one egg every two years, and it can take up to two years to raise a single chick to independence. Because of this extremely slow reproduction rate, the species is highly vulnerable to population decline.' Which detail most directly supports why the eagle is vulnerable?","work":"The physical size is a detail but not directly about vulnerability; the SLOW reproduction rate is the detail that directly explains it","answer":"Its slow reproduction rate — laying only one egg every two years"},{"type":"tip","text":"Not every true detail in a passage is equally important — rank details by how directly they connect to the passage's central point, since exam questions often ask for the detail that MOST supports (not just relates to) the main idea."}],"passage":"Bayanihan refers to a spirit of communal unity and cooperation among Filipinos, most famously illustrated by the traditional image of neighbors working together to physically carry a family's entire house, on bamboo poles, to a new location. While this literal house-moving practice is now rare, the underlying value remains a defining feature of Filipino community life. It appears when neighbors pool resources after a typhoon, when strangers help push a stalled jeepney out of traffic, or when an entire barangay contributes food and labor for a wedding or funeral. Sociologists note that bayanihan tends to intensify during crises, when formal government assistance is slow to arrive and communities rely on informal networks instead. Critics, however, caution that romanticizing bayanihan can sometimes excuse the government from investing in stronger, more reliable public services, since communities are expected to fill the gap themselves.","questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the central idea of the passage?","options":["Filipinos used to move houses using bamboo poles.","Bayanihan, the Filipino spirit of communal cooperation, remains significant today but is also viewed critically by some.","Typhoons are common in the Philippines.","The government should provide more public services."],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is a supporting DETAIL, not the central idea?","options":["Bayanihan is a defining feature of Filipino community life.","Neighbors help push a stalled jeepney out of traffic.","The passage discusses both the value and criticism of bayanihan.","Bayanihan intensifies during crises."],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"According to the passage, the traditional image of bayanihan involves neighbors carrying a family's house using {blank}.","accepted":["bamboo poles","bamboo"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"What detail do critics use to caution against romanticizing bayanihan?","options":["It only happens during typhoons.","It can excuse the government from investing in stronger public services.","It is no longer practiced at all.","It is only practiced in rural areas."],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"According to the passage, when does bayanihan tend to intensify?","accepted":["during crises","crises","when government assistance is slow"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which detail BEST supports the idea that bayanihan is still relevant today, even though house-moving is rare?","options":["Bayanihan was 'most famously illustrated' by house-moving.","Neighbors pool resources after a typhoon and help with weddings or funerals.","Sociologists study bayanihan.","Government assistance can be slow."],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the relationship between the passage's supporting details and its central idea?","options":["The details contradict the central idea.","The details illustrate and provide evidence for the central idea.","The details are unrelated to the central idea.","The central idea is only a supporting detail."],"answer":1}]},{"id":"read-inference","subject":"reading","category":"Reading Skills","title":"Inference: Drawing Conclusions with Textual Evidence","competency":"Making logical inferences that are directly supported by the passage's stated facts","estLesson":8,"estPractice":10,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"What counts as a valid inference?"},{"type":"p","text":"An inference is a conclusion that isn't stated directly but logically follows from what IS stated. A valid inference must be supported by specific evidence in the passage — not just be a 'reasonable idea' in general."},{"type":"h","text":"Strategy: find the evidence first"},{"type":"p","text":"Before selecting an inference answer, identify the specific sentence(s) in the passage that would support it. If you can't point to textual evidence, the inference is likely too much of a stretch (or outright wrong)."},{"type":"example","prompt":"'By 1991, Mount Pinatubo had been dormant for over 500 years, so few scientists were monitoring it closely. When it began showing signs of activity in April 1991, volcanologists rushed to install monitoring equipment, and their warnings led to the evacuation of tens of thousands of people before the volcano's massive eruption in June.' What can be inferred about the evacuation's effect on casualties?","work":"The passage states warnings led to evacuation BEFORE a massive eruption — this implies the evacuation likely reduced deaths, even though no casualty number is given directly","answer":"The evacuation likely prevented a much higher death toll, since people were moved to safety before the eruption occurred."},{"type":"tip","text":"Beware of inference answer choices that ARE true in the real world but aren't actually supported by anything stated in THIS passage — the correct inference must be traceable to specific textual evidence, not outside/prior knowledge."}],"passage":"From the 1560s until 1815, Spanish galleons connected Manila and Acapulco. On the eastward voyage to Acapulco, they carried Asian goods such as silk and porcelain. On the westward return to Manila, they brought silver from the Americas. Some of this silver reached China through merchants who supplied goods to Manila.\n\nThe crossing could take months. Voyages faced storms, disease, and the risk of attack, and some ships never arrived. Despite those risks, merchants from several regions gathered in Manila to buy, sell, and exchange goods.\n\nThe last galleon returned to Manila in 1815, while the struggle for Mexican independence was still underway. The long-running connection depended on two ports located in different parts of the Spanish Empire.","questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"What does the final paragraph support about the end of the trade?","options":["It continued after Mexico gained independence.","The trade ended during the struggle for Mexican independence.","It ended when all Pacific storms stopped.","It ended because Chinese silk ran out."],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"What can be inferred about the risk level of the voyage?","options":["It was completely safe.","It carried significant danger, since not every galleon completed the crossing.","It only took a few days.","It was only dangerous near Manila."],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"According to the passage, the westward voyage brought American {blank} to Manila.","accepted":["silver"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"What role did Manila play in this trade?","options":["It manufactured all the goods shipped to Acapulco.","It was a commercial hub where merchants from several regions traded.","It served as a port only for ships from Europe.","It traded exclusively with Japan."],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Based on the dates given, about how many years did the galleon trade last?","accepted":["250","about 250","250 years","about 250 years"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"What can be inferred from the passage about the flow of silver?","options":["All the silver stayed in Acapulco.","The trade linked buyers and sellers across several regions beyond the two ports.","China sent silver to Acapulco on the eastward voyage.","Merchants used only porcelain for payment."],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which detail best supports the inference that the voyage was both lengthy and dangerous?","options":["The last galleon returned in 1815.","It took months and exposed crews to storms, disease, and attacks.","The ships carried silk and porcelain.","Some silver made its way to China."],"answer":1}]},{"id":"read-tone-mood-purpose","subject":"reading","category":"Reading Skills","title":"Tone, Mood & Author's Purpose","competency":"Pagkilala sa tono, mood, at layunin ng may-akda sa isang tekstong Filipino","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Tone vs. mood"},{"type":"p","text":"Tone is the author's attitude toward the subject (e.g., critical, admiring, humorous, solemn) — it's about the WRITER's stance. Mood is the emotional atmosphere the passage creates IN THE READER (e.g., tense, peaceful, nostalgic)."},{"type":"h","text":"Author's purpose"},{"type":"p","text":"Authors generally write to inform, persuade, entertain, or express/reflect. Identifying the purpose means asking: what is this text trying to DO to/for the reader?"},{"type":"example","prompt":"A news article states facts about typhoon damage without offering opinions. What is the likely purpose?","work":"No persuasion or personal reflection, just factual reporting","answer":"To inform"},{"type":"example","prompt":"An op-ed argues passionately that a new policy will harm small farmers, using emotionally charged language.","work":"The passage argues a position and uses emotional language to convince","answer":"To persuade"},{"type":"tip","text":"Word choice is the biggest clue to tone — look for specific adjectives, adverbs, and connotations (positive or negative) the author chooses, rather than just the topic itself, since the same topic can be written about with very different tones."}],"passage":"Kadalasan, ipinagmamalaki natin ang kakayahang magsalita ng Ingles nang matatas, subalit kung minsan ay napapabayaan na natin ang ating sariling wika. Marami sa mga kabataan ngayon ang mas komportableng magsalita ng Taglish kaysa sa purong Filipino, at ilan sa kanila ay ikinahihiya pa nga ang paggamit nito sa harap ng ibang tao. Ngunit ang wika ay hindi lamang paraan ng pakikipag-usap — ito ay saksi sa kasaysayan, kultura, at pagkakakilanlan ng isang bansa. Kapag pinabayaan natin ang ating wika, unti-unti rin nating nalilimutan ang bahagi ng ating pagka-Filipino. Kaya naman, mahalagang ipagpatuloy nating gamitin, pag-aralan, at ipagmalaki ang wikang Filipino, hindi bilang kahalili sa Ingles, kundi bilang karagdagang yaman na tanging atin.","questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Ano ang tono ng may-akda sa sipi?","options":["Nanunuya","Nagmamalasakit at nananawagan","Walang malasakit","Nangungutya"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Ano ang pangunahing layunin ng may-akda?","options":["Aliwin ang mambabasa","Hikayatin ang mambabasa na pahalagahan at gamitin ang wikang Filipino","Magbigay lamang ng datos tungkol sa wika","Ikuwento ang kasaysayan ng Pilipinas"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Ayon sa sipi, ang wika ay saksi sa kasaysayan, kultura, at {blank} ng isang bansa.","accepted":["pagkakakilanlan"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Anong damdamin ang malamang na maramdaman ng isang mambabasang Pilipino habang binabasa ang sipi?","options":["Pagmamalaki at pagninilay","Galit sa may-akda","Kalungkutan lamang","Walang pakialam"],"answer":0},{"id":"q5","type":"reflect","prompt":"Batay sa sipi, ano ang sanhi kung bakit ikinahihiya ng ilang kabataan ang paggamit ng purong Filipino?"},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Ang paggamit ng salitang 'ipagmalaki' sa huling bahagi ng sipi ay nagpapakita ng anong tono?","options":["Negatibo","Positibo at nag-aanyaya","Neutral","Mapanuya"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Alin sa mga sumusunod ang uri ng teksto batay sa layunin nito?","options":["Naglalahad (Informative)","Nanghihikayat (Persuasive)","Naglalarawan lamang (Purely descriptive)","Pang-aliw (Entertainment)"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"read-authors-viewpoint","subject":"reading","category":"Reading Skills","title":"Author's Viewpoint & Perspective","competency":"Identifying the author's stance, bias, and how it shapes the presentation of information","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"What is author's viewpoint?"},{"type":"p","text":"The author's viewpoint (or perspective) is the position or stance the writer takes on the subject — it shapes which facts get emphasized, which get left out, and what language is used to describe them."},{"type":"h","text":"Strategy: look for loaded language and selective emphasis"},{"type":"p","text":"An author's viewpoint often reveals itself through word choice with positive or negative connotations, and through which side of an issue gets more space or stronger evidence."},{"type":"example","prompt":"'The new factory will create jobs, but critics worry — perhaps too cautiously — about its environmental impact.' What is the author's likely viewpoint?","work":"The phrase 'perhaps too cautiously' subtly dismisses the critics' concern, suggesting the author leans toward supporting the factory","answer":"The author appears to favor the factory, subtly downplaying environmental concerns."},{"type":"tip","text":"Distinguish a passage's TOPIC (what it's about) from the author's VIEWPOINT (their stance on it) — two authors can write about the exact same topic with completely opposite viewpoints."}],"passage":"The Philippines, blessed with more sunlight than most countries on Earth, has been remarkably slow to embrace solar power at scale. While neighboring nations have raced to install solar farms over the past decade, the Philippines still relies heavily on imported coal and oil, exposing the country to volatile global fuel prices and needless carbon emissions. Proponents of solar energy point out that the technology's cost has fallen dramatically, making it more affordable than ever, and that rooftop solar installations could even allow remote island communities to generate their own power instead of waiting for costly transmission lines. Yet outdated regulations and a lack of political will continue to favor established fossil-fuel interests. It is difficult to see this delay as anything other than a missed opportunity — one that leaves the country needlessly dependent on imported fuel despite having one of the most abundant renewable resources in the region.","questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the author's viewpoint on the Philippines' slow adoption of solar power?","options":["The author is neutral and presents no opinion.","The author views it critically, as a missed opportunity.","The author strongly supports continued reliance on coal and oil.","The author believes solar power is not cost-effective."],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which phrase from the passage MOST clearly reveals the author's critical viewpoint?","options":["blessed with more sunlight than most countries","It is difficult to see this delay as anything other than a missed opportunity","the technology's cost has fallen dramatically","rooftop solar installations"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"According to the passage, the Philippines' continued reliance on coal and oil exposes it to volatile global fuel prices and needless {blank} emissions.","accepted":["carbon"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"What does the author suggest is preventing wider solar adoption in the Philippines?","options":["Lack of sunlight","Outdated regulations and a lack of political will","High cost of solar technology","Lack of scientific knowledge"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"reflect","prompt":"According to the passage, what benefit could rooftop solar offer remote island communities?","model":"Communities could generate electricity locally without relying on costly transmission lines."},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which word in the passage carries a clearly negative connotation, revealing the author's stance?","options":["Proponents","Needlessly","Abundant","Installations"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"An author's viewpoint is best identified by looking for:","options":["Only the topic being discussed","Word choice, emphasis, and selective framing of facts","The length of the passage","The number of paragraphs"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"read-paragraph-structure","subject":"reading","category":"Reading Skills","title":"Paragraph Structure & Organization","competency":"Pag-unawa kung paano nagkakaugnay-ugnay at nagkakasunod-sunod ang mga ideya sa isang tekstong Filipino","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Common organizational patterns"},{"type":"list","items":["<b>Chronological/sequence</b> — events or steps presented in time order (first, then, next, finally)","<b>Cause and effect</b> — one event shown as leading to another","<b>Compare and contrast</b> — similarities and differences between two or more things","<b>Problem and solution</b> — an issue is presented, followed by a proposed fix"]},{"type":"h","text":"Strategy: watch for transition and signal words"},{"type":"p","text":"Signal words often reveal the passage's structure: 'first/next/finally' suggest sequence; 'because/as a result' suggest cause-effect; 'similarly/on the other hand' suggest compare-contrast; 'the challenge is/one solution is' suggest problem-solution."},{"type":"example","prompt":"'First, the rice fields are flooded. Next, seedlings are transplanted by hand. Finally, after several months, the rice is harvested.' What organizational pattern is this?","work":"The signal words 'first, next, finally' clearly mark time order","answer":"Chronological / sequence"},{"type":"tip","text":"A passage can combine more than one pattern — for example, a problem-solution passage might also use cause-and-effect to explain WHY the problem happened before presenting the fix."}],"passage":"Ang pagtatanim ng palay sa Pilipinas ay sumasailalim sa ilang yugto bago maani. Una, inihahanda ang lupa sa pamamagitan ng pag-araro at pagpapatag nito upang maging angkop para sa pagtatanim. Pagkatapos, isinasabog ang mga binhi sa isang maliit na bahagi ng bukid upang tumubo bilang mga punla. Kapag sapat na ang laki ng mga punla, karaniwang pagkalipas ng dalawampu't limang araw, inililipat ang mga ito sa pangunahing bukid sa pamamagitan ng manwal na pagtatanim, kung saan ang mga magsasaka ay nakayuko habang isinasabit ang bawat punla sa banlik. Sa loob ng tatlo hanggang apat na buwan, dumadaan ang palay sa iba't ibang yugto ng paglaki bago ito ganap na hinog. Sa wakas, inaani ang palay, karaniwan sa pamamagitan ng pag-gapas, at pagkatapos ay ginigiik upang ihiwalay ang butil mula sa dayami.","questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Anong uri ng organisasyon ang ginamit sa sipi?","options":["Paghahambing at pagtutulad","Sanhi at bunga","Kronolohikal / sunud-sunod na proseso","Suliranin at solusyon"],"answer":2},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Ano ang UNANG hakbang sa proseso ayon sa sipi?","options":["Pag-aani ng palay","Paghahanda ng lupa sa pamamagitan ng pag-araro","Pagtatanim ng mga punla sa bukid","Pagbibilad ng palay"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Ayon sa sipi, karaniwang pagkalipas ng {blank} na araw, inililipat na ang mga punla sa pangunahing bukid.","accepted":["dalawampu't lima","25"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Anong salitang panandang panahon (signal word) ang tumutulong ipakita ang pagkakasunod-sunod ng mga hakbang?","options":["Ngunit","Una, pagkatapos, sa wakas","Halimbawa","Katulad nito"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Ayon sa sipi, anong hakbang ang ginagawa pagkatapos gapasin ang palay?","accepted":["paggigiik","paggiik","paggigiik ng palay","paggiik ng palay"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Ilang buwan, ayon sa sipi, ang tinatagal ng palay bago ganap na hinog?","options":["Isa hanggang dalawa","Tatlo hanggang apat","Anim hanggang pito","Walo hanggang siyam"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Ano ang layunin ng paggigiik, batay sa sipi?","options":["Upang hugasan ang palay","Upang ihiwalay ang butil mula sa dayami","Upang patubigan ang bukid","Upang araruhin ang lupa"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"read-passage-mapping","subject":"reading","category":"Reading Skills","title":"Passage Mapping: Skimming & Scanning for Structure","competency":"Previewing a passage's structure before answering questions, to read more efficiently","estLesson":8,"estPractice":10,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Skimming vs. scanning"},{"type":"p","text":"Skimming means quickly reading the first and last sentence of each paragraph (plus the title) to get the passage's overall shape and main points, BEFORE reading it in full or answering questions. Scanning means searching quickly for a specific word, name, date, or number in response to a specific question, without reading every word."},{"type":"h","text":"Building a mental (or literal) passage map"},{"type":"p","text":"As you skim, jot down a one-phrase label for what each paragraph covers. This 'map' lets you jump straight to the right paragraph when a question asks about a specific detail, instead of re-reading the whole passage each time."},{"type":"example","prompt":"A 4-paragraph passage covers: Para 1 — a species' habitat; Para 2 — threats it faces; Para 3 — conservation efforts; Para 4 — future outlook. A question asks about specific conservation programs. Which paragraph should you go to directly?","work":"Match the question's topic to the paragraph label from your map","answer":"Paragraph 3"},{"type":"tip","text":"Passage mapping is most useful on LONGER passages with several paragraphs and multiple questions — for a short single-paragraph passage, it's usually faster to just read the whole thing carefully instead of mapping it first."}],"passage":"(1) The Philippine eagle, one of the largest and most powerful eagles in the world, is found only in the forests of the Philippines, primarily on the islands of Mindanao, Luzon, Samar, and Leyte. It depends on large, undisturbed tracts of old-growth forest to hunt and to build its massive nests high in the canopy.\n\n(2) Deforestation is the species' single greatest threat. As logging, mining, and agricultural expansion clear the forests the eagle depends on, its habitat has shrunk dramatically over the past century, and the birds are increasingly forced into smaller, fragmented patches of forest that cannot support a stable population.\n\n(3) In response, the Philippine Eagle Foundation has run a captive breeding program since the 1990s, successfully hatching and releasing eagles bred in captivity back into protected forest reserves. The foundation also works with local communities, offering alternative livelihoods to reduce reliance on logging.\n\n(4) Despite these efforts, conservationists estimate only a few hundred breeding pairs remain, and continued forest loss means the species' long-term survival is still far from guaranteed.","questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"If a question asks specifically about the eagle's habitat requirements, which paragraph should you scan first?","options":["Paragraph 1","Paragraph 2","Paragraph 3","Paragraph 4"],"answer":0},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"If a question asks about the captive breeding program, which paragraph should you scan first?","options":["Paragraph 1","Paragraph 2","Paragraph 3","Paragraph 4"],"answer":2},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"According to Paragraph 2, what is described as the species' single greatest threat? {blank}","accepted":["deforestation"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the overall organizational pattern of this passage, based on your skim of all four paragraphs?","options":["Habitat, then threat, then response, then current outlook","Random unrelated facts","A single chronological story","A comparison between two species"],"answer":0},{"id":"q5","type":"reflect","prompt":"Based on skimming just the first sentence of each paragraph, what is Paragraph 4 mainly about?","model":"Paragraph 4 describes the species’ uncertain future and the outlook for its survival."},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which islands does the passage name as habitats for the Philippine eagle?","options":["Cebu, Bohol, Palawan, Panay","Mindanao, Luzon, Samar, Leyte","Luzon, Visayas, Mindanao only","Palawan and Mindanao only"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the main benefit of passage mapping before answering questions?","options":["It replaces the need to read the passage at all","It lets you jump directly to the relevant paragraph instead of re-reading the whole passage for each question","It guarantees every answer will be correct","It is required for every reading passage, regardless of length"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"read-terraces-1","subject":"reading","category":"Passage Practice (mixed review)","title":"The Ifugao Rice Terraces","competency":"Main idea vs. detail · inference · vocabulary in context","estLesson":10,"estPractice":15,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Main idea vs. supporting detail"},{"type":"p","text":"The main idea is what the whole passage is mostly about or arguing — it has to hold true for the passage as a whole, not just one paragraph. Supporting details are the facts, examples, or numbers used to back that idea up. When a question asks for the main idea, the correct choice is usually the answer that is broad enough to summarize every paragraph, but not so broad that it isn't actually discussed."},{"type":"tip","text":"For main-idea questions, eliminate choices that are true but too narrow (only about one paragraph) and choices that are too broad (not really discussed in the passage)."},{"type":"h","text":"Answering inference questions"},{"type":"p","text":"An inference is a conclusion that isn't stated outright but is clearly supported by what is stated. The correct inference must follow logically from the passage — not just be a reasonable idea in general.","work":"The stalls were already stocked by 7 a.m., and vendors had arrived before sunrise. This supports the inference that the vendors did some setup early that morning.","answer":"The vendors likely stocked the stalls before 7 a.m."},{"type":"example","prompt":"“By 7 a.m. the market stalls were already fully stocked, though the vendors said they had arrived before sunrise.” What can be inferred?","work":"The passage states the stalls were fully stocked by 7 a.m. and that vendors arrived before sunrise.\nThe most directly supported conclusion is about the time it took to set up, not about anything else (like prices or crowd size, which aren't mentioned).","answer":"Setting up the stalls took the vendors several hours before the market opened."},{"type":"h","text":"Vocabulary in context"},{"type":"p","text":"To find what a word means “as used in the passage,” try substituting each answer choice into the original sentence. The correct choice is the one that keeps the sentence's meaning and tone intact."},{"type":"tip","text":"Mentally underline the sentence where the question's keyword appears — the answer is almost always drawn from that sentence and the one right before or after it, not the whole passage."}],"passage":"The rice terraces of Ifugao are a longstanding example of agricultural engineering in the Cordillera. The age of particular terraces and the timing of widespread wet-rice cultivation are subjects of archaeological research. Using local materials and labor, the Ifugao people shaped entire mountainsides into stepped fields, developing an irrigation system that channels water from forested peaks down through each terrace in sequence. This method conserves water, limits soil erosion, and allows rice to be grown on slopes too steep for ordinary farming. UNESCO inscribed five clusters as the Rice Terraces of the Philippine Cordilleras World Heritage Site in 1995, citing both their engineering ingenuity and the living culture that sustains them. In recent decades, however, younger generations have increasingly moved to cities for work, leaving fewer farmers to maintain the terraces' stone walls and water channels. Local and national programs now offer training and incentives for terrace farming, recognizing that the structures survive only as long as people continue to tend them.","questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the main idea of the passage?","options":["The general history of UNESCO heritage sites","How the Ifugao terraces work, and why their survival now depends on people continuing to farm them","The process of rice irrigation in general","Why young Filipinos are moving to cities"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"According to the passage, what specific problem threatens the terraces today?","options":["Lack of rainfall","Erosion caused by machinery","A shrinking number of farmers as youth move to cities","Government neglect of UNESCO sites"],"answer":2},{"id":"q3","type":"mcq","prompt":"As used in the passage, the word “ingenuity” most nearly means:","options":["Age","Cleverness","Simplicity","Cost"],"answer":1},{"id":"q4","type":"fillblank","prompt":"The terraces were recognized as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in the year {blank}.","accepted":["1995"]},{"id":"q5","type":"mcq","prompt":"Based on the passage, the irrigation system depends most directly on:","options":["Rainfall collected in nearby cities","Water flowing from forested peaks above the terraces","Pumps installed in the 1990s","Water imported from lowland rivers"],"answer":1},{"id":"q6","type":"reflect","prompt":"In one or two sentences, explain why the passage says the terraces “survive only as long as people continue to tend them.”"}]},{"id":"sci-bio-cell-structure","subject":"science","category":"Biology","title":"Cell Structure & Organelles","competency":"Prokaryotic vs. eukaryotic cells; structure and function of major organelles","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Prokaryotic vs. eukaryotic cells"},{"type":"p","text":"Prokaryotic cells (bacteria, archaea) lack a nucleus and membrane-bound organelles. Eukaryotic cells (plants, animals, fungi, protists) have a true nucleus and membrane-bound organelles."},{"type":"h","text":"Key organelles and their functions"},{"type":"list","items":["<b>Nucleus</b> — stores DNA, controls cell activities","<b>Mitochondria</b> — produces ATP (cellular energy) via respiration","<b>Ribosomes</b> — synthesize proteins","<b>Endoplasmic reticulum</b> — rough ER processes proteins; smooth ER synthesizes lipids","<b>Golgi apparatus</b> — packages and ships proteins","<b>Lysosomes</b> — digest waste and worn-out organelles","<b>Chloroplasts</b> — site of photosynthesis (plants only)","<b>Cell wall</b> — rigid outer layer (plants, fungi, bacteria — not animals)","<b>Cell membrane</b> — selectively permeable barrier controlling what enters/exits"]},{"type":"example","prompt":"A cell has a cell wall, chloroplasts, and a large central vacuole. Is this a plant or animal cell?","work":"Cell walls and chloroplasts are only found in plant cells","answer":"Plant cell"},{"type":"tip","text":"Mitochondria and chloroplasts are the only organelles with their own DNA — a clue to their evolutionary origin as once-independent bacteria (the endosymbiotic theory)."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"In a typical aerobic animal cell, which organelle is the primary site of ATP production?","options":["Nucleus","Mitochondria","Ribosome","Golgi apparatus"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which structure is found in plant cells but NOT animal cells?","options":["Nucleus","Mitochondria","Cell wall","Ribosome"],"answer":2},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"The organelle that packages and ships proteins out of the cell is the {blank}.","accepted":["Golgi apparatus","Golgi"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which type of cell lacks a true nucleus?","options":["Plant cell","Animal cell","Prokaryotic cell","Eukaryotic cell"],"answer":2},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Name the organelle responsible for digesting waste and worn-out cell parts.","accepted":["lysosome","lysosomes"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which organelle is the site of photosynthesis?","options":["Mitochondria","Chloroplast","Nucleus","Vacuole"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"What do mitochondria and chloroplasts have in common that supports the endosymbiotic theory?","options":["They are both found only in animal cells","They both contain their own DNA","They are both part of the cell membrane","They both lack a membrane"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"sci-bio-cell-division","subject":"science","category":"Biology","title":"Cell Division: Mitosis & Meiosis","competency":"Phases of mitosis; how meiosis differs from mitosis and produces genetic variation","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Mitosis"},{"type":"p","text":"Mitosis produces two genetically identical daughter cells from one parent cell, used for growth and repair. Phases in order: Prophase (chromosomes condense), Metaphase (chromosomes align at the cell's equator), Anaphase (sister chromatids separate and move to opposite poles), Telophase (nuclear membranes reform), followed by Cytokinesis (the cell physically divides)."},{"type":"h","text":"Meiosis"},{"type":"p","text":"Meiosis produces four genetically different daughter cells, each with HALF the chromosome number (haploid), used to make sex cells (gametes). It involves two rounds of division (Meiosis I and Meiosis II), and includes crossing over (exchange of genetic material between homologous chromosomes) during Prophase I, which increases genetic variation."},{"type":"example","prompt":"A human somatic (body) cell has 46 chromosomes. How many chromosomes will each daughter cell have after mitosis? After meiosis?","work":"Mitosis keeps the same chromosome number; meiosis halves it","answer":"Mitosis: 46; Meiosis: 23"},{"type":"tip","text":"Remember PMAT for the order of mitosis phases: Prophase, Metaphase, Anaphase, Telophase. Meiosis simply repeats this whole sequence twice (Meiosis I and Meiosis II)."},{"type":"tip","text":"The typical diagram shows four haploid products. In human egg formation, unequal divisions yield one functional egg and polar bodies."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"During which phase of mitosis do chromosomes align at the cell's equator?","options":["Prophase","Metaphase","Anaphase","Telophase"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the main purpose of mitosis?","options":["Producing sex cells","Growth and repair of body cells","Reducing chromosome number","Creating genetic variation"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"A human egg cell (produced by meiosis) has {blank} chromosomes.","accepted":["23"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"What process during Prophase I of meiosis increases genetic variation?","options":["Cytokinesis","Crossing over","DNA replication","Mitosis"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"In the usual model of meiosis followed by cytokinesis, how many haploid cells result from one diploid parent cell?","accepted":["4","four"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which phase of mitosis involves sister chromatids separating and moving to opposite poles?","options":["Prophase","Metaphase","Anaphase","Telophase"],"answer":2},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which statement correctly distinguishes meiosis from mitosis?","options":["Meiosis produces genetically identical cells; mitosis does not","Meiosis produces gametes with half the chromosome number; mitosis produces identical body cells","Mitosis involves two rounds of division; meiosis involves one","Meiosis only occurs in plants"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"sci-bio-diffusion-osmosis","subject":"science","category":"Biology","title":"Diffusion & Osmosis","competency":"Passive transport across cell membranes; tonicity and its effect on cells","estLesson":8,"estPractice":10,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Diffusion"},{"type":"p","text":"Diffusion is the movement of particles from an area of HIGH concentration to an area of LOW concentration, until equilibrium is reached — no energy required (passive transport)."},{"type":"h","text":"Osmosis"},{"type":"p","text":"Osmosis is the diffusion of WATER specifically, across a selectively permeable membrane, from an area of low solute concentration to an area of high solute concentration."},{"type":"h","text":"Tonicity and its effect on cells"},{"type":"list","items":["<b>Hypotonic solution</b> (lower solute concentration outside) — water moves INTO the cell; it may swell or burst","<b>Hypertonic solution</b> (higher solute concentration outside) — water moves OUT of the cell; it shrinks (crenation in animal cells, plasmolysis in plant cells)","<b>Isotonic solution</b> — equal concentration, no net water movement"]},{"type":"example","prompt":"A red blood cell is placed in a hypertonic solution. What happens to the cell?","work":"Water moves OUT of the cell toward the higher solute concentration outside","answer":"The cell shrinks (crenates)."},{"type":"tip","text":"To remember direction: water always moves toward the side with MORE solute (higher concentration of dissolved particles), diluting it — think of it as water trying to 'balance out' the concentration."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is diffusion?","options":["Movement of water only, across a membrane","Movement of particles from high to low concentration","Movement of particles from low to high concentration, requiring energy","Movement of particles only within cells"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"A plant cell is placed in a hypotonic solution. What happens?","options":["Water leaves the cell; it shrinks","Water enters the cell; it may swell (but the cell wall prevents bursting)","No water movement occurs","The cell dissolves"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"The diffusion of water across a selectively permeable membrane is called {blank}.","accepted":["osmosis"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"A red blood cell placed in a hypotonic solution will likely:","options":["Shrink","Swell and possibly burst","Stay the same size","Turn into a plant cell"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Does diffusion require energy (ATP) to occur? Answer yes or no.","accepted":["no"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"A cell in an isotonic solution will:","options":["Swell","Shrink","Show no net change in water movement","Immediately burst"],"answer":2},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Plasmolysis (the shrinking of a plant cell's cytoplasm away from its cell wall) occurs when a plant cell is placed in:","options":["A hypotonic solution","A hypertonic solution","An isotonic solution","Pure water"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"sci-bio-photosynthesis","subject":"science","category":"Biology","title":"Photosynthesis: Light & Dark Reactions","competency":"The overall equation, and the light-dependent and light-independent (Calvin cycle) reactions","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"The overall process"},{"type":"p","text":"Photosynthesis converts light energy into chemical energy (glucose), using carbon dioxide and water, and releasing oxygen as a byproduct. Overall equation: 6CO₂ + 6H₂O + light energy → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂. It occurs in the chloroplasts."},{"type":"h","text":"Light-dependent reactions"},{"type":"p","text":"Occur in the thylakoid membranes. Light energy is absorbed by chlorophyll, splits water molecules (releasing O₂), and produces ATP and NADPH (energy carriers) to be used in the next stage."},{"type":"h","text":"Light-independent reactions (Calvin cycle)"},{"type":"p","text":"Occur in the stroma. Uses the ATP and NADPH from the light reactions, along with CO₂, to build glucose. Doesn't directly require light, but depends on the products of the light reactions."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Which stage of photosynthesis directly produces oxygen gas?","work":"Water splitting happens during the light-dependent reactions","answer":"The light-dependent reactions (in the thylakoid membrane)"},{"type":"tip","text":"The Calvin cycle is often called the 'dark reactions,' but this is a misleading name — it doesn't require darkness, it just doesn't directly use light; it depends on the ATP/NADPH supplied by the light reactions instead."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"What are the reactants (starting materials) of photosynthesis?","options":["Glucose and oxygen","Carbon dioxide and water","Water and oxygen","ATP and NADPH"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Where do the light-dependent reactions of photosynthesis occur?","options":["Stroma","Mitochondria","Thylakoid membrane","Nucleus"],"answer":2},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"The light-independent reactions of photosynthesis are also known as the {blank} cycle.","accepted":["Calvin"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"What two energy-carrying molecules are produced by the light-dependent reactions and used in the Calvin cycle?","options":["Glucose and oxygen","ATP and NADPH","CO2 and H2O","DNA and RNA"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"What gas is released as a byproduct of the light-dependent reactions?","accepted":["oxygen","O2","O₂"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Where in the chloroplast does the Calvin cycle take place?","options":["Thylakoid membrane","Stroma","Cell wall","Nucleus"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which statement about the 'dark reactions' (Calvin cycle) is TRUE?","options":["They can only occur at night","They don't directly use light, but depend on products from the light reactions","They occur in the mitochondria","They release oxygen directly"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"sci-bio-cellular-respiration","subject":"science","category":"Biology","title":"Cellular Respiration","competency":"Glycolysis, the Krebs cycle, and the electron transport chain","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"The overall process"},{"type":"p","text":"Cellular respiration breaks down glucose in the presence of oxygen to release energy (ATP). Overall equation: C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O + energy (ATP). It's essentially the reverse of photosynthesis."},{"type":"h","text":"The three stages"},{"type":"list","items":["<b>Glycolysis</b> — occurs in the cytoplasm; breaks glucose into two molecules of pyruvate; produces a small net gain of ATP; doesn't require oxygen","<b>Krebs cycle</b> (citric acid cycle) — occurs in the mitochondrial matrix; breaks down pyruvate further, releasing CO₂ and producing electron carriers (NADH, FADH₂)","<b>Electron transport chain</b> (ETC) — occurs in the inner mitochondrial membrane; uses the electron carriers from earlier stages to produce the MAJORITY of the cell's ATP, with oxygen as the final electron acceptor (forming water)"]},{"type":"example","prompt":"Which stage of cellular respiration produces the most ATP?","work":"The electron transport chain generates the vast majority of ATP compared to glycolysis or the Krebs cycle","answer":"The electron transport chain"},{"type":"tip","text":"Glycolysis is the only stage of cellular respiration that doesn't need oxygen — it's why some cells (like your muscle cells during intense exercise) can still produce a little ATP anaerobically through fermentation when oxygen is scarce."},{"type":"tip","text":"Oxidative phosphorylation couples electron transport to ATP synthesis. Electron transport builds a proton gradient. ATP synthase uses that gradient to make ATP."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Where does glycolysis take place?","options":["Mitochondrial matrix","Cytoplasm","Inner mitochondrial membrane","Nucleus"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which stage produces most of the ATP in typical aerobic cellular respiration?","options":["Glycolysis","The citric acid cycle directly","Oxidative phosphorylation","Fermentation"],"answer":2},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"In aerobic respiration, the final electron acceptor in the mitochondrial electron transport chain is {blank}.","accepted":["oxygen","O2","O₂"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the end product of glycolysis?","options":["Glucose","Pyruvate","Carbon dioxide","ATP only"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Does glycolysis require oxygen to occur? Answer yes or no.","accepted":["no"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"In eukaryotic cells, where does the Krebs cycle take place?","options":["Cytoplasm","Mitochondrial matrix","Inner mitochondrial membrane","Nucleus"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which reduced electron carriers deliver electrons to the respiratory electron transport chain?","options":["ATP and ADP","NADH and FADH₂","O₂ and CO₂","Glucose and pyruvate"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"sci-bio-genetics-punnett","subject":"science","category":"Biology","title":"Mendelian Genetics & Punnett Squares","competency":"Dominant/recessive alleles, genotype vs. phenotype, monohybrid crosses, and sex-linked inheritance","estLesson":10,"estPractice":12,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Key vocabulary"},{"type":"list","items":["<b>Allele</b> — a version of a gene","<b>Genotype</b> — the genetic makeup (e.g., Bb)","<b>Phenotype</b> — the observable trait (e.g., brown eyes)","<b>Dominant allele</b> (capital letter) — masks the recessive allele when present","<b>Recessive allele</b> (lowercase letter) — only expressed when NO dominant allele is present (homozygous recessive)","<b>Homozygous</b> — two identical alleles (BB or bb)","<b>Heterozygous</b> — two different alleles (Bb)"]},{"type":"example","prompt":"Cross a heterozygous brown-eyed parent (Bb) with a blue-eyed parent (bb). What are the possible offspring genotypes and their ratio?","work":"Bb × bb → offspring: Bb, Bb, bb, bb (a 2×2 Punnett square)","answer":"1/2 Bb (brown-eyed), 1/2 bb (blue-eyed) — a 1:1 ratio"},{"type":"h","text":"Sex-linked inheritance"},{"type":"p","text":"Genes located on the X chromosome show sex-linked inheritance. Since males have only one X chromosome (XY), a single recessive allele on their X chromosome will be expressed — this is why sex-linked recessive conditions (like red-green color blindness) are more common in males."},{"type":"tip","text":"In sex-linked Punnett squares, always write the allele attached to the X (like Xᴮ or Xᵇ), and remember the Y chromosome carries no matching allele — so a male with even one recessive X-linked allele will show that trait."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"In genetics, what term describes the observable physical trait of an organism?","options":["Genotype","Phenotype","Allele","Chromosome"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"A genotype of 'Tt' is described as:","options":["Homozygous dominant","Homozygous recessive","Heterozygous","Not a valid genotype"],"answer":2},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Cross Tt × Tt (both heterozygous for tall). What fraction of offspring are expected to be homozygous recessive (tt)? {blank}","accepted":["1/4","25%"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why are many X-linked recessive traits more common in people with one X chromosome than in people with two?","options":["A single recessive allele on the only X chromosome can be expressed.","People with one X chromosome inherit two copies of every X-linked gene.","The allele is always found on the Y chromosome.","The trait is caused by an extra autosome."],"answer":0},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"A father is colorblind (XᶜY) and the mother is a carrier (XᶜX). What is the probability that a son will be colorblind?","accepted":["1/2","50%"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Cross BB × bb. What is the genotype of ALL offspring?","options":["BB","Bb","bb","Both BB and bb"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which best describes a 'homozygous dominant' genotype?","options":["Two different alleles, one dominant","Two identical dominant alleles (e.g., BB)","Two identical recessive alleles (e.g., bb)","One allele only"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"sci-bio-dna-protein-synthesis","subject":"science","category":"Biology","title":"DNA Replication, Transcription & Protein Synthesis","competency":"How DNA copies itself, and how genetic information becomes a protein","estLesson":10,"estPractice":12,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"DNA replication"},{"type":"p","text":"Before a cell divides, its DNA must be copied. DNA unwinds, and each original strand serves as a template for building a new complementary strand (semi-conservative replication — each new DNA molecule has one original and one new strand)."},{"type":"h","text":"Transcription"},{"type":"p","text":"DNA's genetic code is copied into a messenger RNA (mRNA) molecule, occurring in the nucleus. DNA's base pairing rule applies, except RNA uses uracil (U) instead of thymine (T) to pair with adenine (A)."},{"type":"h","text":"Translation (protein synthesis)"},{"type":"p","text":"mRNA travels to a ribosome, where its sequence is read in three-letter codons, each specifying a particular amino acid. Amino acids are linked together in the specified order to build a protein."},{"type":"example","prompt":"A DNA template strand reads: T-A-C-G-G-A. What is the complementary mRNA sequence produced during transcription?","work":"DNA→mRNA pairing: T pairs with A, A pairs with U, C pairs with G, G pairs with C, G pairs with C, A pairs with U","answer":"A-U-G-C-C-U"},{"type":"tip","text":"The flow of genetic information follows the 'central dogma': DNA → (transcription) → RNA → (translation) → Protein. Memorizing this one-way flow helps you keep replication, transcription, and translation straight."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the process of copying DNA's genetic code into mRNA called?","options":["Replication","Transcription","Translation","Mutation"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"A DNA template strand reads 3′–ATGC–5′. What mRNA sequence is made, written 5′ to 3′?","options":["T-A-C-G","U-A-C-G","A-T-G-C","U-T-C-G"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"In RNA, the base {blank} replaces thymine and pairs with adenine.","accepted":["uracil"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Where does translation (protein synthesis from mRNA) take place?","options":["Nucleus","Mitochondria","Ribosome","Golgi apparatus"],"answer":2},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"What is a three-base sequence in mRNA that specifies an amino acid or a stop signal called?","accepted":["codon"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"DNA replication is described as 'semi-conservative' because:","options":["Half the DNA is destroyed","Each new DNA molecule has one original strand and one newly made strand","DNA only replicates half the time","Only half of each gene is copied"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the correct order of the 'central dogma' of molecular biology?","options":["Protein → RNA → DNA","DNA → RNA → Protein","RNA → DNA → Protein","Protein → DNA → RNA"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"sci-bio-mutations","subject":"science","category":"Biology","title":"Mutation Types","competency":"Point mutations (substitution, insertion, deletion) and their effects on protein","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"What is a mutation?"},{"type":"p","text":"A mutation is any change in an organism's DNA sequence. Mutations can be harmful, beneficial, or have no effect (neutral), and they are the ultimate source of genetic variation."},{"type":"h","text":"Point mutations"},{"type":"p","text":"Substitution — one base is swapped for another; may cause a silent mutation (no change in amino acid, due to redundancy in the genetic code), a missense mutation (changes to a different amino acid), or a nonsense mutation (creates a premature stop codon, usually severely disruptive). Insertion — one or more bases are added. Deletion — one or more bases are removed."},{"type":"h","text":"Frameshift mutations"},{"type":"p","text":"Insertions or deletions that are NOT in multiples of three shift the entire reading frame downstream, changing every codon after the mutation point — usually far more disruptive than a single substitution."},{"type":"example","prompt":"A single base is deleted from a DNA sequence, and the deletion is not a multiple of three. What type of mutation is this, and why is it typically severe?","work":"Any non-multiple-of-three insertion/deletion shifts every codon reading frame after that point","answer":"A frameshift mutation — it shifts the reading frame, altering every codon (and amino acid) that follows"},{"type":"tip","text":"A substitution mutation changes at most ONE amino acid (or none, if silent) — but a frameshift mutation from an insertion or deletion (not in multiples of 3) can scramble the entire protein sequence downstream of the mutation."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"A mutation that changes one DNA base into another is called a:","options":["Insertion","Deletion","Substitution","Frameshift"],"answer":2},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"A substitution mutation that does NOT change the resulting amino acid (due to redundancy in the genetic code) is called a:","options":["Missense mutation","Nonsense mutation","Silent mutation","Frameshift mutation"],"answer":2},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"A substitution mutation that creates a premature stop codon is called a {blank} mutation.","accepted":["nonsense"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why do insertions/deletions not in multiples of three tend to be more disruptive than substitutions?","options":["They add extra chromosomes","They shift the reading frame, changing every codon downstream","They only affect non-coding DNA","They always create a new gene"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"What is the general term for any change in an organism's DNA sequence?","accepted":["mutation"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"A missense mutation results in:","options":["No change to the protein","A premature stop codon","A different amino acid being incorporated into the protein","The deletion of the entire gene"],"answer":2},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Mutations are considered the ultimate source of:","options":["Cell division","Genetic variation","Protein folding","Osmosis"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"sci-bio-body-systems","subject":"science","category":"Biology","title":"Human Body Systems & Key Hormones","competency":"Circulatory, respiratory, digestive, nervous, and endocrine systems; insulin, glucagon, adrenaline, thyroxine","estLesson":10,"estPractice":12,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Five major body systems"},{"type":"list","items":["<b>Circulatory</b> — heart pumps blood through vessels, delivering oxygen/nutrients and removing waste","<b>Respiratory</b> — lungs exchange oxygen and carbon dioxide with the blood","<b>Digestive</b> — breaks down food into absorbable nutrients (mouth → esophagus → stomach → small intestine → large intestine)","<b>Nervous</b> — brain, spinal cord, and nerves control and coordinate body functions via electrical signals","<b>Endocrine</b> — glands release hormones directly into the bloodstream to regulate body processes more slowly than the nervous system"]},{"type":"h","text":"Key hormones"},{"type":"list","items":["<b>Insulin</b> (from the pancreas) — lowers blood glucose by helping cells absorb it","<b>Glucagon</b> (from the pancreas) — raises blood glucose by triggering the liver to release stored glucose","<b>Adrenaline/epinephrine</b> (from the adrenal glands) — triggers the 'fight or flight' response, increasing heart rate and energy availability","<b>Thyroxine</b> (from the thyroid gland) — regulates metabolic rate"]},{"type":"example","prompt":"After a meal, blood sugar rises. Which hormone is released to lower it, and from where?","work":"Insulin is released to bring blood glucose down","answer":"Insulin, from the pancreas"},{"type":"tip","text":"Insulin and glucagon work as an opposing pair (antagonistic hormones) to keep blood sugar balanced — insulin lowers it after eating, glucagon raises it during fasting. If you remember one, you can reason out the other's opposite function."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which organ system is primarily responsible for gas exchange (oxygen in, carbon dioxide out)?","options":["Circulatory","Respiratory","Digestive","Endocrine"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which hormone raises blood glucose levels by signaling the liver to release stored glucose?","options":["Insulin","Glucagon","Adrenaline","Thyroxine"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"The hormone that regulates the body's metabolic rate, released by the thyroid gland, is {blank}.","accepted":["thyroxine"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which hormone triggers the 'fight or flight' response?","options":["Insulin","Glucagon","Adrenaline (epinephrine)","Thyroxine"],"answer":2},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Which organ releases both insulin and glucagon?","accepted":["pancreas"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which body system uses electrical signals to control and coordinate body functions?","options":["Circulatory","Nervous","Digestive","Endocrine"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which option gives the correct order of these selected organs in the digestive tract?","options":["Stomach → mouth → small intestine → large intestine","Mouth → esophagus → stomach → small intestine → large intestine","Mouth → stomach → esophagus → large intestine → small intestine","Esophagus → mouth → stomach → intestines"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"sci-bio-ecology","subject":"science","category":"Biology","title":"Ecology: Food Webs, Trophic Levels & Symbiosis","competency":"Food chains/webs, trophic levels and energy flow, and types of symbiotic relationships","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Food chains and food webs"},{"type":"p","text":"A food chain shows a single linear path of energy transfer (e.g., grass → grasshopper → frog → snake). A food web shows the many interconnected food chains within an ecosystem, since most organisms eat and are eaten by more than one species."},{"type":"h","text":"Trophic levels and energy flow"},{"type":"p","text":"Producers (autotrophs, e.g. plants) make their own food via photosynthesis. Primary consumers (herbivores) eat producers. Secondary consumers (carnivores/omnivores) eat primary consumers. Tertiary consumers eat secondary consumers. Decomposers break down dead organisms, recycling nutrients. Only about 10% of energy transfers from one trophic level to the next (the rest is lost as heat) — this is why food chains rarely exceed 4-5 levels."},{"type":"h","text":"Symbiotic relationships"},{"type":"list","items":["<b>Mutualism</b> — both species benefit (e.g., bees and flowers)","<b>Commensalism</b> — one benefits, the other is unaffected (e.g., barnacles on a whale)","<b>Parasitism</b> — one benefits, the other is harmed (e.g., a tapeworm in a host)"]},{"type":"example","prompt":"A clownfish lives among a sea anemone's stinging tentacles, gaining protection from predators, while the anemone gets food scraps and improved water circulation from the fish. What type of symbiosis is this?","work":"Both species benefit","answer":"Mutualism"},{"type":"tip","text":"Because only about 10% of energy passes to the next trophic level, there is always far more biomass and energy available at the producer level than at the top of the food chain — this is why top predators are relatively rare compared to plants."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the primary source of energy for nearly all food chains?","options":["Decomposers","The sun","Herbivores","Water"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"In the food chain grass → grasshopper → frog → snake, what is the frog's trophic level?","options":["Producer","Primary consumer","Secondary consumer","Tertiary consumer"],"answer":2},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Using the ecological rule of thumb often taught in introductory classes, roughly {blank}% of energy is transferred to the next trophic level.","accepted":["10"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"A tick feeds on a dog's blood, harming the dog while benefiting itself. What type of symbiosis is this?","options":["Mutualism","Commensalism","Parasitism","Competition"],"answer":2},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"What term describes organisms, like plants, that produce their own food through photosynthesis?","accepted":["producers","autotrophs"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Barnacles attach to a whale's skin, getting a free ride and access to food, without helping or harming the whale. What type of symbiosis is this?","options":["Mutualism","Commensalism","Parasitism","Predation"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why do food chains rarely have more than 4-5 trophic levels?","options":["There aren't enough species","Only about 10% of energy transfers between levels, so energy runs out","Predators refuse to eat past the 5th level","Producers stop growing after 5 levels"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"sci-bio-evolution","subject":"science","category":"Biology","title":"Evolution, Natural Selection & Speciation","competency":"Darwin's theory of natural selection; how new species form","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Natural selection"},{"type":"p","text":"Darwin's theory: within a population, individuals show variation in traits; some traits give a survival/reproductive advantage in a given environment; individuals with advantageous traits are more likely to survive and reproduce, passing those traits to offspring; over many generations, advantageous traits become more common in the population."},{"type":"h","text":"Evidence for evolution"},{"type":"list","items":["<b>Fossil record</b> — shows change in species over time","<b>Comparative anatomy</b> — homologous structures (similar bone structure, different function, like a human arm and a bat wing) suggest common ancestry","<b>Molecular biology</b> — similarities in DNA/protein sequences between species indicate relatedness"]},{"type":"h","text":"Speciation"},{"type":"p","text":"Speciation is the formation of new, distinct species, usually occurring when populations become reproductively isolated from each other (e.g., by a geographic barrier) and accumulate enough genetic differences over time that they can no longer interbreed."},{"type":"example","prompt":"A population of finches is split by a new river into two groups that can no longer interbreed. Over thousands of years, each group adapts to its own side's food sources and eventually cannot successfully breed with the other group, even when reunited. What process has occurred?","work":"Reproductive isolation followed by genetic divergence leading to inability to interbreed","answer":"Speciation"},{"type":"tip","text":"Natural selection does not act on individuals over their own lifetime — an individual doesn't 'evolve.' Selection acts on populations over many generations, favoring pre-existing variation that already happened to help survival."},{"type":"tip","text":"Selection acts through differences in survival and reproduction among individuals. Evolution is a change in the heritable characteristics of a population over generations."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"According to natural selection, which individuals are more likely to survive and reproduce?","options":["Those chosen randomly","Those with traits advantageous for their environment","Only the largest individuals","Only the fastest-reproducing individuals"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"A human arm and a bat wing have similar underlying bone structure but different functions. This is an example of:","options":["Analogous structures","Homologous structures","Vestigial structures","Convergent evolution only"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"The formation of new, distinct species, often due to reproductive isolation, is called {blank}.","accepted":["speciation"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is a common cause of reproductive isolation leading to speciation?","options":["Identical habitats","A geographic barrier separating populations","Increased food supply","Faster reproduction rates"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"According to natural selection, does an individual organism 'evolve' during its own lifetime? Answer yes or no.","accepted":["no"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which type of evidence for evolution comes from comparing DNA and protein sequences between species?","options":["Fossil record","Comparative anatomy","Molecular biology","Behavioral evidence"],"answer":2},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which statement describes how natural selection leads to evolution?","options":["Individuals evolve new traits because they need them.","Individuals with advantageous heritable traits tend to leave more offspring, so those traits can become more common.","All members of a population reproduce equally.","Environmental change guarantees that every organism adapts."],"answer":1}]},{"id":"sci-bio-taxonomy","subject":"science","category":"Biology","title":"Taxonomy & Classification","competency":"The hierarchical classification system and binomial nomenclature","estLesson":8,"estPractice":10,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"The taxonomic hierarchy"},{"type":"p","text":"Organisms are classified into a nested hierarchy, from broadest to most specific: Domain, Kingdom, Phylum, Class, Order, Family, Genus, Species. Each level narrows down the group into more closely related organisms."},{"type":"h","text":"Binomial nomenclature"},{"type":"p","text":"Every species is given a two-part scientific name: Genus (capitalized) + species (lowercase), both italicized (e.g., Homo sapiens). This universal naming system avoids the confusion of common names, which vary by language and region."},{"type":"h","text":"The three domains"},{"type":"list","items":["<b>Bacteria</b> — prokaryotic, no nucleus","<b>Archaea</b> — prokaryotic, often found in extreme environments, structurally distinct from bacteria","<b>Eukarya</b> — eukaryotic (has a nucleus), includes plants, animals, fungi, and protists"]},{"type":"example","prompt":"Which taxonomic level groups organisms MORE closely related than 'Class' but LESS closely related than 'Family'?","work":"The hierarchy from broad to narrow is Domain, Kingdom, Phylum, Class, Order, Family, Genus, Species — Order sits between Class and Family","answer":"Order"},{"type":"tip","text":"A common mnemonic for the order of taxonomic ranks (Domain, Kingdom, Phylum, Class, Order, Family, Genus, Species) is 'Dear King Philip Came Over For Good Soup.'"}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the correct order of taxonomic ranks, from broadest to most specific?","options":["Species, Genus, Family, Order, Class, Phylum, Kingdom, Domain","Domain, Kingdom, Phylum, Class, Order, Family, Genus, Species","Kingdom, Domain, Phylum, Order, Class, Family, Genus, Species","Domain, Phylum, Kingdom, Class, Family, Order, Genus, Species"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"In the scientific name 'Panthera leo' (lion), what does 'Panthera' represent?","options":["Species","Genus","Family","Domain"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"The naming system that gives every species a two-part Genus + species name is called binomial {blank}.","accepted":["nomenclature"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which domain consists of eukaryotic organisms, including plants, animals, fungi, and protists?","options":["Bacteria","Archaea","Eukarya","Protista"],"answer":2},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"In a scientific name, is the genus name capitalized or lowercase?","accepted":["capitalized","capital"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why is binomial nomenclature useful across different countries and languages?","options":["It uses only English words","It provides one universal, standardized name for each species","It changes based on region","It only applies to animals"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which is the most specific of the eight major ranks listed in the lesson?","options":["Genus","Family","Order","Species"],"answer":3}]},{"id":"sci-chem-atomic-structure","subject":"science","category":"Chemistry","title":"Atomic Structure & Isotopes","competency":"Protons, neutrons, electrons; atomic number, mass number, and isotopes","estLesson":8,"estPractice":10,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Subatomic particles"},{"type":"list","items":["<b>Proton</b> — positive charge, in the nucleus, determines the element's identity","<b>Neutron</b> — no charge, in the nucleus, adds mass","<b>Electron</b> — negative charge, orbits the nucleus in energy levels, involved in bonding"]},{"type":"h","text":"Atomic number and mass number"},{"type":"p","text":"Atomic number = number of protons (defines the element). Mass number = protons + neutrons. In a neutral atom, the number of electrons equals the number of protons."},{"type":"h","text":"Isotopes"},{"type":"p","text":"Isotopes are atoms of the SAME element (same number of protons) with DIFFERENT numbers of neutrons, giving them different mass numbers."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Carbon-12 has 6 protons and 6 neutrons. Carbon-14 has 6 protons and 8 neutrons. Are these the same element?","work":"Both have 6 protons (same atomic number), just different neutron counts","answer":"Yes, both are carbon — they are isotopes of each other, differing only in neutron number"},{"type":"tip","text":"The mass number is written after the element name or as a superscript before the symbol (like carbon-14 or ¹⁴C) — subtract the atomic number (protons) from the mass number to find the number of neutrons."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"What determines an element's identity (atomic number)?","options":["Number of neutrons","Number of protons","Number of electrons only","Total mass"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"An atom has 17 protons and 18 neutrons. What is its mass number?","options":["17","18","35","1"],"answer":2},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Atoms of the same element with different numbers of neutrons are called {blank}.","accepted":["isotopes"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"In a neutral atom, the number of electrons equals the number of:","options":["Neutrons","Protons","Protons plus neutrons","Isotopes"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Chlorine-35 has 17 protons. How many neutrons does it have?","accepted":["18"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which subatomic particle has essentially no charge?","options":["Proton","Neutron","Electron","Ion"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which subatomic particle is primarily involved in chemical bonding?","options":["Proton","Neutron","Electron","Nucleus"],"answer":2}]},{"id":"sci-chem-electron-configuration","subject":"science","category":"Chemistry","title":"Electron Configuration","competency":"Energy levels, orbitals (s, p, d, f), and writing electron configurations","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Energy levels and orbitals"},{"type":"p","text":"Shells contain subshells labeled s, p, d, and f. Each orbital holds at most two electrons. The s, p, d, and f subshells contain 1, 3, 5, and 7 orbitals, giving them capacities of 2, 6, 10, and 14 electrons."},{"type":"h","text":"Writing electron configurations"},{"type":"p","text":"Write each occupied subshell with its shell number, subshell letter, and electron count as a superscript. A common filling sequence for neutral atoms begins 1s, 2s, 2p, 3s, 3p, 4s, 3d, 4p. Some ground-state configurations, including chromium and copper, are exceptions to this simple prediction."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Write the electron configuration for oxygen (atomic number 8).","work":"Fill in order: 1s² (2 electrons) = 2\n2s² (2 more) = 4\n2p⁴ (4 more to reach 8 total)","answer":"1s²2s²2p⁴"},{"type":"h","text":"Valence electrons"},{"type":"p","text":"The electrons in the outermost energy level (highest number) are called valence electrons, and they determine how an atom bonds with others."},{"type":"tip","text":"The number of valence electrons for main-group elements can often be read directly from the periodic table's group number (for groups 1-2 and 13-18) — this shortcut is faster than writing the full configuration every time."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the maximum number of electrons an 's' orbital can hold?","options":["2","6","8","10"],"answer":0},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Write the electron configuration for nitrogen (atomic number 7).","options":["1s²2s²2p³","1s²2s²2p⁵","1s²2s³2p²","1s²2s²2p⁷"],"answer":0},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"For main-group elements, electrons in the outermost occupied shell are called {blank} electrons.","accepted":["valence"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the maximum number of electrons in an entire p subshell (three orbitals)?","options":["2","6","8","10"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Write the electron configuration for fluorine (atomic number 9).","accepted":["1s²2s²2p⁵","1s2 2s2 2p5","1s^2 2s^2 2p^5"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the maximum number of electrons in an entire d subshell (five orbitals)?","options":["2","6","10","14"],"answer":2},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"How many valence electrons does an element in Group 2 of the periodic table typically have?","options":["1","2","7","8"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"sci-chem-periodic-trends","subject":"science","category":"Chemistry","title":"Periodic Table Trends","competency":"Atomic radius, ionization energy, and electronegativity trends across periods and groups","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Atomic radius"},{"type":"p","text":"Atomic radius DECREASES across a period (left to right) because increasing nuclear charge pulls electrons in tighter, and INCREASES down a group because each row adds a new energy level (shell) further from the nucleus."},{"type":"h","text":"Ionization energy"},{"type":"p","text":"Ionization energy (the energy needed to remove an electron) INCREASES across a period (atoms hold their electrons more tightly) and DECREASES down a group (outer electrons are farther from the nucleus and more loosely held)."},{"type":"h","text":"Electronegativity"},{"type":"p","text":"Electronegativity (an atom's tendency to attract electrons in a bond) INCREASES across a period and DECREASES down a group — following the same general pattern as ionization energy."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Which has a larger atomic radius: sodium (Na) or chlorine (Cl), both in Period 3?","work":"Atomic radius decreases left to right across a period; sodium is farther left","answer":"Sodium (Na)"},{"type":"tip","text":"Nearly all these trends point toward the SAME corner of the periodic table (upper right, excluding noble gases) for 'strongest attraction to electrons' — fluorine has the highest electronegativity of any element, a useful anchor point to remember the whole pattern."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Moving across a period (left to right), atomic radius generally:","options":["Increases","Decreases","Stays the same","Increases then decreases"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Moving down a group, ionization energy generally:","options":["Increases","Decreases","Stays the same","Becomes zero"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"The element with the highest electronegativity on the periodic table is {blank}.","accepted":["fluorine"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which element has the LARGER atomic radius: potassium (K) or sodium (Na), both in Group 1?","options":["Potassium","Sodium","They are equal","Cannot be determined"],"answer":0},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Does ionization energy generally increase or decrease moving left to right across a period?","accepted":["increase","increases"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Electronegativity measures an atom's:","options":["Total mass","Tendency to attract electrons in a bond","Number of neutrons","Physical size only"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why does atomic radius increase moving down a group?","options":["Nuclear charge decreases","A new electron energy level (shell) is added, farther from the nucleus","Atoms lose electrons","There is no consistent trend down a group"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"sci-chem-bonding-polarity","subject":"science","category":"Chemistry","title":"Bonding & Molecular Polarity","competency":"Ionic, covalent, and metallic bonding; predicting molecular polarity","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Three main bond types"},{"type":"list","items":["<b>Ionic bonding</b> — electrons are TRANSFERRED from a metal to a nonmetal, forming oppositely charged ions that attract each other (e.g., NaCl)","<b>Covalent bonding</b> — electrons are SHARED between two nonmetals (e.g., H₂O, CO₂)","<b>Metallic bonding</b> — metal atoms share a 'sea' of delocalized electrons, giving metals their conductivity and malleability"]},{"type":"h","text":"Polar vs. nonpolar covalent bonds"},{"type":"p","text":"When two atoms share electrons UNEQUALLY (due to a difference in electronegativity), the bond is polar, creating a slightly negative end and slightly positive end. When atoms share electrons EQUALLY (identical or very similar electronegativity), the bond is nonpolar."},{"type":"h","text":"Molecular polarity"},{"type":"p","text":"A molecule's overall polarity depends on both its bond polarities AND its molecular shape/symmetry — a molecule with polar bonds can still be nonpolar overall if the bond polarities cancel out due to symmetry (like CO₂), while an asymmetric molecule with polar bonds (like H₂O) is polar overall."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Water (H₂O) has polar O-H bonds and a bent molecular shape. Is the molecule polar overall?","work":"The bent shape means the bond polarities do NOT cancel out","answer":"Yes, water is a polar molecule"},{"type":"tip","text":"A quick check for bond type: metal + nonmetal → ionic; nonmetal + nonmetal → covalent. For polarity, check the electronegativity difference: a large difference leans ionic, a small difference is nonpolar covalent, and a moderate difference is polar covalent."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which type of bond involves the complete transfer of electrons from a metal to a nonmetal?","options":["Covalent","Ionic","Metallic","Hydrogen"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which type of bond involves electrons shared between two nonmetal atoms?","options":["Ionic","Covalent","Metallic","None of these"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"The bonding in metals, where electrons form a shared 'sea' around positive metal ions, is called {blank} bonding.","accepted":["metallic"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Carbon dioxide (CO₂) has polar C=O bonds, but is a NONPOLAR molecule overall. Why?","options":["CO2 has no bonds","Its linear, symmetric shape causes the bond polarities to cancel out","Carbon and oxygen have identical electronegativity","CO2 is not a real molecule"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"A bond between two atoms of the same element (like in O₂) shares electrons equally. Is this bond polar or nonpolar?","accepted":["nonpolar"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which property is characteristic of metallic bonding?","options":["Brittleness","Electrical conductivity due to delocalized electrons","Complete electron transfer","No electron interaction at all"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"What determines whether a molecule is polar overall?","options":["Only the number of atoms","Both individual bond polarities AND the molecule's overall shape/symmetry","Only the molecule's mass","Only the temperature"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"sci-chem-mole-stoichiometry","subject":"science","category":"Chemistry","title":"Mole Concept, Molar Mass & Stoichiometry","competency":"Avogadro's number, molar mass calculations, and balancing chemical equations","estLesson":10,"estPractice":12,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"The mole and Avogadro's number"},{"type":"p","text":"A mole is a counting unit for atoms/molecules, equal to 6.022 × 10²³ particles (Avogadro's number) — just like 'a dozen' always means 12, regardless of what's being counted."},{"type":"h","text":"Molar mass"},{"type":"p","text":"The molar mass of a substance (in grams per mole) is numerically equal to its atomic/molecular mass from the periodic table, added up for a compound."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Find the molar mass of water (H₂O). (H ≈ 1 g/mol, O ≈ 16 g/mol)","work":"2(1) + 16 = 2 + 16","answer":"18 g/mol"},{"type":"h","text":"Balancing chemical equations"},{"type":"p","text":"Chemical equations must be balanced so the same number of each type of atom appears on both sides (conservation of mass) — adjust the coefficients (numbers in front of formulas), never the subscripts within a formula."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Balance: H₂ + O₂ → H₂O","work":"As written, 2 O atoms on the left but 1 on the right; balance by putting a 2 in front of H₂O (giving 4 H and 2 O on the right), then adjust H₂ to 2 to match","answer":"2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O"},{"type":"tip","text":"Always balance the most complex molecule first, and balance elements that appear alone (like O₂ or H₂) last — this order usually requires the fewest adjustments."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"How many particles are in one mole of a substance?","options":["6.022 × 10²³","1000","100","12"],"answer":0},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Find the molar mass of carbon dioxide, CO₂. (C ≈ 12 g/mol, O ≈ 16 g/mol)","options":["28 g/mol","32 g/mol","44 g/mol","12 g/mol"],"answer":2},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Balance this equation: {blank}N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃ (fill in the coefficient for N₂)","accepted":["1"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"When balancing a chemical equation, what is allowed to change?","options":["The subscripts within a formula","The coefficients in front of each formula","The chemical formulas themselves","Nothing can be changed"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Find the molar mass of methane, CH₄. (C ≈ 12 g/mol, H ≈ 1 g/mol)","accepted":["16","16 g/mol"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Balancing chemical equations enforces which fundamental law?","options":["Law of conservation of energy","Law of conservation of mass","Newton's third law","Boyle's law"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is Avogadro's number used to represent?","options":["The mass of one atom","The number of particles in one mole","The number of protons in an atom","The volume of one mole of gas"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"sci-chem-states-gas-laws","subject":"science","category":"Chemistry","title":"States of Matter & Gas Laws","competency":"Solid, liquid, gas, plasma; Boyle's law, Charles's law, and the combined/ideal gas law","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"States of matter"},{"type":"list","items":["<b>Solid</b> — fixed shape and volume, particles tightly packed and vibrate in place","<b>Liquid</b> — fixed volume, takes the shape of its container, particles close but able to move past each other","<b>Gas</b> — no fixed shape or volume, particles far apart and move freely","<b>Plasma</b> — ionized gas, found in stars and lightning"]},{"type":"h","text":"Boyle's law"},{"type":"p","text":"At constant temperature, pressure and volume are inversely related: P₁V₁ = P₂V₂. As volume decreases, pressure increases."},{"type":"h","text":"Charles's law"},{"type":"p","text":"At constant pressure, volume and temperature (in Kelvin) are directly related: V₁/T₁ = V₂/T₂. As temperature increases, volume increases."},{"type":"example","prompt":"A gas occupies 4 L at 2 atm. If the pressure increases to 8 atm (temperature constant), what is the new volume?","work":"P₁V₁ = P₂V₂\n2×4 = 8×V₂\n8 = 8V₂","answer":"1 L"},{"type":"tip","text":"Always convert temperature to Kelvin (K = °C + 273.15.15) before using it in ANY gas law calculation — using Celsius directly will give an incorrect answer."},{"type":"tip","text":"The exact conversion is T(K) = t(°C) + 273.15. Use 273 only when an approximation is specified."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which state of matter has a fixed volume but takes the shape of its container?","options":["Solid","Liquid","Gas","Plasma"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"A gas occupies 6 L at 3 atm. If the pressure decreases to 2 atm (temperature constant), what is the new volume?","options":["4 L","9 L","2 L","6 L"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"For the exact Celsius-to-kelvin conversion, add {blank} to the numerical Celsius value.","accepted":["273.15"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Boyle's law describes the relationship between:","options":["Volume and temperature","Pressure and volume (at constant temperature)","Pressure and temperature","Moles and volume"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"According to Charles's law, if temperature increases at constant pressure, does volume increase or decrease?","accepted":["increase","increases"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which state of matter consists of ionized gas, found in stars and lightning?","options":["Solid","Liquid","Gas","Plasma"],"answer":3},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"A gas at 200 K occupies 2 L. If the temperature increases to 400 K (pressure constant), what is the new volume (using Charles's law)?","options":["1 L","2 L","4 L","8 L"],"answer":2}]},{"id":"sci-chem-solutions-concentration","subject":"science","category":"Chemistry","title":"Solutions & Concentration","competency":"Solute, solvent, and calculating molarity","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Solutions vocabulary"},{"type":"p","text":"A solution is a homogeneous mixture of a solute (the substance being dissolved, usually the smaller amount) dissolved in a solvent (the substance doing the dissolving, usually the larger amount). Water is often called the 'universal solvent' because it dissolves so many substances."},{"type":"h","text":"Molarity"},{"type":"p","text":"Molarity (M) is the most common concentration unit: M = moles of solute ÷ liters of solution."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Find the molarity of a solution made by dissolving 2 moles of NaCl in 4 liters of water.","work":"M = 2 mol ÷ 4 L","answer":"0.5 M"},{"type":"h","text":"Dilution"},{"type":"p","text":"Diluting a solution adds more solvent without adding more solute, using the formula M₁V₁ = M₂V₂ (moles of solute stays constant before and after dilution)."},{"type":"example","prompt":"500 mL of a 2 M solution is diluted to 2000 mL. What is the new concentration?","work":"M₁V₁ = M₂V₂\n2(500) = M₂(2000)\n1000 = 2000M₂","answer":"0.5 M"},{"type":"tip","text":"In a solution, the solute is generally the substance present in the SMALLER amount, and the solvent is present in the LARGER amount — for saltwater, salt is the solute and water is the solvent, even though this isn't a strict rule (concentrated solutions can be an exception)."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"In a saltwater solution, which substance is the solvent?","options":["Salt","Water","Both equally","Neither"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Find the molarity of a solution made by dissolving 3 moles of solute in 1.5 liters of solution.","options":["0.5 M","2 M","18 M","3 M"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Molarity is calculated as moles of solute divided by {blank} of solution.","accepted":["liters","liter"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"200 mL of a 4 M solution is diluted to 800 mL. What is the new concentration?","options":["1 M","2 M","4 M","16 M"],"answer":0},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Find the molarity of a solution with 5 moles of solute dissolved in 2.5 liters of solution.","accepted":["2","2 M"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"What happens to the number of MOLES of solute when a solution is diluted with more solvent?","options":["It increases","It decreases","It stays the same","It becomes zero"],"answer":2},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which formula correctly relates concentration and volume before and after dilution?","options":["M1+V1=M2+V2","M1V1=M2V2","M1/V1=M2/V2 only if moles change","M1-V1=M2-V2"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"sci-chem-acids-bases-ph","subject":"science","category":"Chemistry","title":"Acids, Bases & the pH Scale","competency":"Properties of acids and bases; the pH scale and its logarithmic nature","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Acids and bases"},{"type":"p","text":"In the Arrhenius model, acids increase hydronium (H₃O⁺) in water and bases increase hydroxide (OH⁻). Acids turn blue litmus red. Bases turn red litmus blue."},{"type":"h","text":"The pH scale"},{"type":"p","text":"At 25 °C, a neutral aqueous solution has pH 7. Below 7 is acidic and above 7 is basic at that temperature. Neutral pH changes with temperature, and concentrated solutions can fall outside the familiar 0–14 range. The pH scale is logarithmic."},{"type":"example","prompt":"How does the hydrogen ion activity at pH 4 compare with that at pH 6?","work":"The difference is 2 pH units, and each unit is a 10x change: 10×10","answer":"It is 100 times as high at pH 4."},{"type":"tip","text":"Because the pH scale is logarithmic, never compare pH values by simple subtraction to get a 'how many times' answer — a difference of 1 pH unit means 10x, a difference of 2 means 100x (10²), a difference of 3 means 1000x (10³), and so on."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"At 25 °C, how is an aqueous solution with pH 3 classified?","options":["Neutral","Acidic","Basic","Its pH gives no information."],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"How many times higher is hydrogen ion activity at pH 2 than at pH 5?","options":["10 times","100 times","1,000 times","10,000 times"],"answer":2},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"At 25 °C, a neutral aqueous solution has a pH of {blank}.","accepted":["7"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"In the Arrhenius model, which ion do acids increase in water?","options":["OH⁻","H₃O⁺ (often written H⁺)","Na⁺","Cl⁻"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Does blue litmus paper turn red in an acid or a base?","accepted":["acid","an acid"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"In the Arrhenius model, which ion do bases increase in water?","options":["H⁺","OH⁻","O²⁻","H₂O"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"The pH scale is described as 'logarithmic' because:","options":["It only applies to acids","Each whole-number change represents a tenfold change in H⁺ concentration","It only goes from 0 to 10","pH values cannot be measured precisely"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"sci-chem-redox","subject":"science","category":"Chemistry","title":"Oxidation-Reduction (Redox) Reactions","competency":"Identifying oxidation and reduction; oxidizing and reducing agents","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Oxidation and reduction"},{"type":"p","text":"Oxidation is the LOSS of electrons; reduction is the GAIN of electrons. These always occur together in a redox reaction. A helpful mnemonic: 'OIL RIG' — Oxidation Is Loss, Reduction Is Gain (of electrons)."},{"type":"h","text":"Oxidizing and reducing agents"},{"type":"p","text":"The oxidizing agent CAUSES oxidation in another substance by accepting electrons (so the oxidizing agent itself is reduced). The reducing agent CAUSES reduction in another substance by donating electrons (so the reducing agent itself is oxidized)."},{"type":"example","prompt":"In the reaction Zn + Cu²⁺ → Zn²⁺ + Cu, which substance is oxidized, and which is reduced?","work":"Zn goes from 0 charge to +2, losing 2 electrons (oxidized); Cu²⁺ goes from +2 to 0, gaining 2 electrons (reduced)","answer":"Zn is oxidized; Cu²⁺ is reduced"},{"type":"tip","text":"Track oxidation states (charges) before and after the reaction — if an element's charge becomes MORE positive, it lost electrons (oxidized); if it becomes MORE negative (or less positive), it gained electrons (reduced)."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"What happens during oxidation?","options":["Gain of electrons","Loss of electrons","Gain of protons","Loss of protons"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"In 2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO, magnesium’s oxidation state changes from 0 to +2. Is it oxidized or reduced?","options":["Oxidized","Reduced","Neither","Both"],"answer":0},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"The mnemonic 'OIL RIG' helps remember: Oxidation Is Loss, Reduction Is {blank}.","accepted":["gain"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"The reducing agent in a redox reaction is the substance that:","options":["Accepts electrons and is itself reduced","Donates electrons and is itself oxidized","Neither gains nor loses electrons","Always contains oxygen"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"In the reaction Mg + Cu²⁺ → Mg²⁺ + Cu, is Cu²⁺ oxidized or reduced?","accepted":["reduced"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"What must always occur together in a redox reaction?","options":["Two oxidations","Two reductions","One oxidation and one reduction","Neither oxidation nor reduction"],"answer":2},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"The oxidizing agent in a reaction is itself:","options":["Oxidized","Reduced","Neutral","Unchanged"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"sci-chem-organic-basics","subject":"science","category":"Chemistry","title":"Organic Chemistry Basics","competency":"Common functional groups and IUPAC naming for alkanes","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"What is organic chemistry?"},{"type":"p","text":"Organic chemistry is the study of carbon-containing compounds. Carbon's ability to form 4 stable bonds allows it to build long chains, rings, and complex structures — the basis of all known life."},{"type":"h","text":"Common functional groups"},{"type":"list","items":["<b>Hydroxyl (-OH)</b> — found in alcohols","<b>Carboxyl (-COOH)</b> — found in organic (carboxylic) acids","<b>Amino (-NH₂)</b> — found in amines and amino acids","<b>Carbonyl (C=O)</b> — found in aldehydes and ketones"]},{"type":"h","text":"IUPAC naming for alkanes"},{"type":"p","text":"Alkanes are hydrocarbons with only single bonds, named by the number of carbon atoms in the longest chain, using the prefixes: 1=meth-, 2=eth-, 3=prop-, 4=but-, 5=pent-, 6=hex-, 7=hept-, 8=oct-, 9=non-, 10=dec-, all ending in '-ane.'"},{"type":"example","prompt":"Name the alkane with 5 carbon atoms in a straight chain.","work":"The prefix for 5 carbons is 'pent-', plus the alkane ending '-ane'","answer":"Pentane"},{"type":"tip","text":"The general formula for any straight-chain alkane is CₙH₂ₙ₊₂ — for example, pentane (5 carbons) has 2(5)+2 = 12 hydrogens, giving the formula C₅H₁₂."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"What functional group is found in alcohols?","options":["Carboxyl (-COOH)","Hydroxyl (-OH)","Amino (-NH2)","Carbonyl (C=O)"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the IUPAC name for the straight-chain alkane with 4 carbon atoms?","options":["Methane","Propane","Butane","Pentane"],"answer":2},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"The functional group -COOH, found in organic acids, is called the {blank} group.","accepted":["carboxyl"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Using the general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂, how many hydrogen atoms does hexane (6 carbons) have?","options":["12","14","16","6"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"What is the name of the –NH₂ group found in the simplest structural representation of a primary amine?","accepted":["amino","amino group","-NH2","NH2","–NH₂"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why is carbon uniquely suited to forming the backbone of organic molecules?","options":["It only forms one bond","It can form 4 stable bonds, allowing chains, rings, and complex structures","It is radioactive","It cannot bond with hydrogen"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the IUPAC name for the alkane with 3 carbon atoms?","options":["Methane","Ethane","Propane","Butane"],"answer":2}]},{"id":"sci-chem-sig-figs","subject":"science","category":"Chemistry","title":"Scientific Notation & Significant Figures","competency":"Rules for counting significant figures and rounding calculated answers","estLesson":8,"estPractice":10,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Rules for counting significant figures"},{"type":"list","items":["All non-zero digits are significant","Zeros BETWEEN non-zero digits are significant (e.g., 205 has 3 sig figs)","Leading zeros (before the first non-zero digit) are NEVER significant (e.g., 0.0025 has 2 sig figs)","Trailing zeros after a decimal point are significant. In a whole number such as 1,200, trailing zeros can be ambiguous. Use scientific notation to state the intended precision."]},{"type":"h","text":"Sig figs in calculations"},{"type":"p","text":"For multiplication/division, round your answer to match the FEWEST significant figures among the numbers used. For addition/subtraction, round to match the fewest decimal places among the numbers used."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Multiply 2.5 × 3.42, and round to the correct number of significant figures.","work":"2.5 × 3.42 = 8.55\n2.5 has 2 sig figs (the fewest), so round the answer to 2 sig figs","answer":"8.6"},{"type":"tip","text":"Scientific notation makes counting significant figures easy — every digit written in the coefficient (before ×10ⁿ) is significant, with no ambiguity about zeros, which is why scientists often prefer it for precise measurements."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"How many significant figures are in the number 0.0450?","options":["2","3","4","5"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"How many significant figures are in 1.200 × 10³?","options":["4","1","2","3"],"answer":0},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Multiply 4.2 × 1.35 and round to the correct number of significant figures: {blank}","accepted":["5.7"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"How many significant figures are in the number 305?","options":["1","2","3","4"],"answer":2},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"How many significant figures are in the number 100. (with a decimal point shown)?","accepted":["3"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"For addition and subtraction, you round your final answer based on:","options":["The most significant figures used","The fewest decimal places used","Scientific notation only","The largest number used"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why is scientific notation useful for significant figures?","options":["It hides insignificant digits","Every digit in the coefficient is clearly significant, avoiding ambiguity with zeros","It only works for very small numbers","It removes the need for sig figs entirely"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"sci-phys-kinematics","subject":"science","category":"Physics","title":"Kinematics & Projectile Motion","competency":"Displacement, velocity, acceleration; the kinematic equations; projectile motion basics","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Key definitions"},{"type":"list","items":["<b>Displacement</b> — change in position (a vector, has direction)","<b>Velocity</b> — rate of change of displacement (v = d/t)","<b>Acceleration</b> — rate of change of velocity (a = Δv/t)"]},{"type":"h","text":"A key kinematic equation"},{"type":"p","text":"For constant acceleration: v = v₀ + at (final velocity = initial velocity + acceleration × time). Another useful one: d = v₀t + ½at²."},{"type":"example","prompt":"A car starts from rest (v₀=0) and accelerates at 4 m/s² for 5 seconds. Find its final velocity.","work":"v = v₀ + at = 0 + 4(5)","answer":"20 m/s"},{"type":"h","text":"Projectile motion"},{"type":"p","text":"An object launched into the air (ignoring air resistance) undergoes two INDEPENDENT motions simultaneously: constant horizontal velocity (no horizontal acceleration), and vertical motion under constant downward acceleration due to gravity (g ≈ 9.8 m/s²)."},{"type":"example","prompt":"A ball is thrown horizontally off a cliff. What happens to its horizontal velocity as it falls?","work":"Horizontal velocity is unaffected by gravity — only vertical velocity changes","answer":"It stays constant (unchanged) throughout the fall"},{"type":"tip","text":"Always analyze projectile motion by splitting it into separate horizontal and vertical components — the ONLY thing connecting them is time, since both motions happen over the same time interval."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"A car starts from rest and accelerates at 3 m/s² for 6 seconds. Find its final velocity.","options":["9 m/s","18 m/s","24 m/s","3 m/s"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"In projectile motion (ignoring air resistance), what happens to the horizontal velocity of the object during flight?","options":["It increases due to gravity","It decreases due to gravity","It stays constant","It becomes zero at the peak"],"answer":2},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"The acceleration due to gravity near Earth's surface is approximately {blank} m/s².","accepted":["9.8"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the definition of acceleration?","options":["The rate of change of position","The rate of change of velocity","The total distance traveled","The force applied to an object"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"A ball's velocity changes from 0 to 15 m/s in 3 seconds. Find its acceleration.","accepted":["5","5 m/s²"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"For a projectile launched horizontally with negligible air resistance, what happens to the magnitude of its vertical velocity during flight?","options":["It stays constant","It increases due to gravity","It decreases to zero immediately","It has no vertical velocity"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which quantity is a vector (has both magnitude and direction)?","options":["Speed","Distance","Displacement","Time"],"answer":2}]},{"id":"sci-phys-newtons-laws","subject":"science","category":"Physics","title":"Newton's Laws of Motion","competency":"The three laws of motion and applying F=ma","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Newton's First Law (Inertia)"},{"type":"p","text":"An object at rest stays at rest, and an object in motion stays in motion at constant velocity, unless acted on by a net external force."},{"type":"h","text":"Newton's Second Law"},{"type":"p","text":"F = ma (force = mass × acceleration). The net force on an object determines its acceleration — more force means more acceleration; more mass means less acceleration for the same force."},{"type":"example","prompt":"A 10 kg object experiences a net force of 40 N. Find its acceleration.","work":"a = F/m = 40/10","answer":"4 m/s²"},{"type":"h","text":"Newton's Third Law"},{"type":"p","text":"For every action, there is an equal and opposite reaction — when object A exerts a force on object B, object B exerts an equal force back on object A, in the opposite direction."},{"type":"example","prompt":"A swimmer pushes water backward to move forward. What law explains this?","work":"The swimmer's push (action) on the water is met with an equal, opposite push (reaction) from the water on the swimmer, propelling them forward","answer":"Newton's Third Law"},{"type":"tip","text":"Newton's Third Law pairs of forces act on TWO DIFFERENT objects (never the same object) — this is why they don't cancel out, even though they're equal and opposite."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"A 5 kg object experiences a net force of 20 N. Find its acceleration.","options":["2 m/s²","4 m/s²","25 m/s²","100 m/s²"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which law states that an object at rest stays at rest unless acted on by a net force?","options":["Newton's First Law","Newton's Second Law","Newton's Third Law","Law of gravitation"],"answer":0},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Newton's Second Law is expressed by the formula F = {blank}","accepted":["ma","m*a","m×a"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"A rocket expels gas downward, propelling itself upward. Which law explains this?","options":["Newton's First Law","Newton's Second Law","Newton's Third Law","Law of conservation of mass"],"answer":2},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"A net force of 30 N is applied to a mass, producing an acceleration of 6 m/s². Find the mass.","accepted":["5","5 kg"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Newton's Third Law pairs of forces act on:","options":["The same object","Two different objects","Only massless objects","Only objects at rest"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"According to Newton's First Law, an object in motion will continue at constant velocity UNLESS:","options":["It runs out of energy","A net external force acts on it","Time passes","It reaches the speed of light"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"sci-phys-work-energy","subject":"science","category":"Physics","title":"Work, Energy & Conservation of Energy","competency":"Work, kinetic and potential energy, and the law of conservation of energy","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Work"},{"type":"p","text":"Work = force × distance (in the direction of the force): W = Fd. Measured in joules (J)."},{"type":"example","prompt":"A force of 20 N pushes a box 5 meters. Find the work done.","work":"W = Fd = 20 × 5","answer":"100 J"},{"type":"h","text":"Kinetic and potential energy"},{"type":"p","text":"Kinetic energy (energy of motion): KE = ½mv². Gravitational potential energy (stored energy due to height): PE = mgh (g ≈ 9.8 m/s²)."},{"type":"example","prompt":"A 2 kg object moves at 3 m/s. Find its kinetic energy.","work":"KE = ½(2)(3²) = ½(2)(9)","answer":"9 J"},{"type":"h","text":"Conservation of energy"},{"type":"p","text":"Energy cannot be created or destroyed, only transformed from one form to another. In an ideal system (no friction/air resistance), total mechanical energy (KE + PE) remains constant."},{"type":"example","prompt":"A 1 kg ball is dropped from a height of 5 m. Find its speed just before hitting the ground (ignore air resistance).","work":"All PE converts to KE: mgh = ½mv²\ngh = ½v²\nv² = 2gh = 2(9.8)(5) = 98","answer":"v = √98 ≈ 9.9 m/s"},{"type":"tip","text":"In a conservation-of-energy problem, set the total energy at the start equal to the total energy at the end — this works even when you don't know the mass, since mass often cancels out of both sides of the equation."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"A constant 15 N force points in the direction of an object’s 4 m displacement. How much work does this force do?","options":["19 J","60 J","3.75 J","11 J"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"A 4 kg object moves at 5 m/s. Find its kinetic energy.","options":["10 J","20 J","50 J","100 J"],"answer":2},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Relative to ground level, find the gravitational potential energy of a 3 kg object 10 m above the ground (g = 9.8 m/s²): {blank} J.","accepted":["294"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"The law of conservation of energy states that energy:","options":["Can be created but not destroyed","Can be destroyed but not created","Cannot be created or destroyed, only transformed","Is always lost as heat"],"answer":2},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"A 2 kg ball is dropped from a height of 10 m. Using conservation of energy, find its speed just before hitting the ground (use g=9.8 m/s², ignore air resistance).","accepted":["14","14 m/s"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the formula for kinetic energy?","options":["KE=mgh","KE=½mv²","KE=Fd","KE=ma"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the SI unit for both work and energy?","options":["Newton","Watt","Joule","Pascal"],"answer":2}]},{"id":"sci-phys-momentum","subject":"science","category":"Physics","title":"Momentum & Collisions","competency":"Momentum, impulse, and conservation of momentum in collisions","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Momentum"},{"type":"p","text":"Momentum = mass × velocity: p = mv. A vector quantity, measured in kg·m/s."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Find the momentum of a 1000 kg car moving at 20 m/s.","work":"p = mv = 1000 × 20","answer":"20,000 kg·m/s"},{"type":"h","text":"Conservation of momentum"},{"type":"p","text":"In a closed system (no external forces), total momentum before a collision equals total momentum after the collision."},{"type":"h","text":"Elastic vs. inelastic collisions"},{"type":"p","text":"Elastic collision — objects bounce apart, both momentum AND kinetic energy are conserved. Inelastic collision — objects may stick together or deform, momentum is conserved but kinetic energy is NOT (some converts to heat/sound/deformation)."},{"type":"example","prompt":"A 2 kg cart moving at 6 m/s collides and sticks to a stationary 4 kg cart (perfectly inelastic collision). Find their combined velocity after the collision.","work":"Momentum before = momentum after:\n(2)(6) + (4)(0) = (2+4)(v)\n12 = 6v","answer":"2 m/s"},{"type":"tip","text":"In a perfectly inelastic collision, always combine the masses into ONE object moving at a SINGLE final velocity — this is the key setup difference from an elastic collision, where the two objects keep separate velocities after bouncing apart."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Find the momentum of a 500 kg car moving at 15 m/s.","options":["33.3 kg·m/s","515 kg·m/s","7,500 kg·m/s","7.5 kg·m/s"],"answer":2},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"In an elastic collision, which quantities are conserved?","options":["Only momentum","Only kinetic energy","Both momentum and kinetic energy","Neither"],"answer":2},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"A 3 kg cart moving at 4 m/s collides and sticks to a stationary 3 kg cart. Find their combined velocity after the collision: {blank} m/s","accepted":["2"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"In an inelastic collision, which quantity is NOT conserved?","options":["Momentum","Mass","Kinetic energy","Charge"],"answer":2},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Find the momentum of a 2 kg object moving at 8 m/s.","accepted":["16","16 kg·m/s","16 kg m/s"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the formula for momentum?","options":["p=mv","p=½mv²","p=Fd","p=ma"],"answer":0},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"In a closed system with no external forces, total momentum:","options":["Always increases","Always decreases","Remains constant before and after a collision","Depends only on kinetic energy"],"answer":2}]},{"id":"sci-phys-circular-motion","subject":"science","category":"Physics","title":"Circular Motion","competency":"Centripetal force and acceleration for objects moving in a circular path","estLesson":8,"estPractice":10,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Centripetal acceleration and force"},{"type":"p","text":"An object moving in a circle at constant speed is still accelerating, because its DIRECTION is constantly changing. This acceleration points toward the CENTER of the circle: a_c = v²/r. The force causing this acceleration is the centripetal force: F_c = mv²/r."},{"type":"example","prompt":"A 2 kg ball moves in a circle of radius 0.5 m at a speed of 4 m/s. Find the centripetal force required.","work":"F = mv²/r = 2(4²)/0.5 = 2(16)/0.5 = 32/0.5","answer":"64 N"},{"type":"h","text":"What provides the centripetal force?"},{"type":"p","text":"Centripetal force isn't a new, separate type of force — it's provided by an existing force (tension in a string, gravity for orbiting planets, friction for a car turning a corner) that happens to point toward the center."},{"type":"tip","text":"Don't confuse centripetal force with 'centrifugal force' — the outward push you FEEL in a turning car is your own inertia (Newton's First Law) resisting the change in direction, not an actual outward force acting on you."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"A 1 kg ball moves in a circle of radius 2 m at a speed of 4 m/s. Find the centripetal force required.","options":["2 N","4 N","8 N","16 N"],"answer":2},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Centripetal acceleration always points:","options":["Away from the center","Toward the center of the circle","Tangent to the circle","In the direction of velocity"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"The formula for centripetal force is F = mv² / {blank}","accepted":["r","radius"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"What provides the centripetal force for a car turning a corner?","options":["Gravity only","Friction between the tires and the road","Air resistance","Newton's Third Law"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"An object moves in a circle at constant SPEED. Is it accelerating? Answer yes or no.","accepted":["yes"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is commonly mistaken for an actual outward force during circular motion, when it's really just inertia?","options":["Centripetal force","Gravitational force","Centrifugal force (the 'feeling' of being pushed outward)","Normal force"],"answer":2},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"If the radius of a circular path DECREASES (same speed), the required centripetal force:","options":["Increases","Decreases","Stays the same","Becomes zero"],"answer":0}]},{"id":"sci-phys-simple-machines","subject":"science","category":"Physics","title":"Simple Machines","competency":"The six simple machines and mechanical advantage","estLesson":8,"estPractice":10,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"The six simple machines"},{"type":"list","items":["<b>Lever</b> — a rigid bar that pivots on a fulcrum","<b>Pulley</b> — a wheel with a rope/cable, changes the direction (or amount) of force","<b>Wheel and axle</b> — a wheel attached to a smaller axle, rotating together","<b>Inclined plane</b> — a sloped surface, reduces the force needed to raise an object (at the cost of distance)","<b>Wedge</b> — two inclined planes back to back, used to split or cut","<b>Screw</b> — an inclined plane wrapped around a cylinder"]},{"type":"h","text":"Mechanical advantage"},{"type":"p","text":"Mechanical advantage (MA) tells you how much a machine multiplies your input force: MA = output force ÷ input force (or, for an inclined plane, MA = length of slope ÷ height)."},{"type":"example","prompt":"An inclined plane is 6 m long and 2 m high. Find its mechanical advantage.","work":"MA = length/height = 6/2","answer":"3"},{"type":"h","text":"The trade-off"},{"type":"p","text":"Simple machines don't create energy — they trade force for distance (or vice versa). A machine that reduces the force needed always requires the input to move a GREATER distance to compensate."},{"type":"tip","text":"Simple machines make work easier, not less — the total work (force × distance) stays roughly the same (ignoring friction); you're just trading a smaller force over a longer distance, or vice versa."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which simple machine is a rigid bar that pivots on a fulcrum?","options":["Pulley","Lever","Wedge","Screw"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"An inclined plane is 8 m long and 2 m high. Find its ideal mechanical advantage.","options":["2","4","6","16"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"The ideal mechanical advantage of an inclined plane is its length divided by its {blank}.","accepted":["height"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which simple machine is essentially an inclined plane wrapped around a cylinder?","options":["Wedge","Screw","Lever","Wheel and axle"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Do simple machines reduce the total amount of work needed to move an object (ignoring friction)? Answer yes or no.","accepted":["no"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"A pulley system primarily helps by:","options":["Creating new energy","Changing the direction (or amount) of force needed","Eliminating the need for force entirely","Increasing the object's mass"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"If a simple machine reduces the input force needed, what must happen to compensate?","options":["The input distance must increase","The output force must decrease","Energy is destroyed","Nothing else changes"],"answer":0}]},{"id":"sci-phys-waves-sound","subject":"science","category":"Physics","title":"Wave Properties, the Doppler Effect & Sound","competency":"Wavelength, frequency, amplitude, wave speed; the Doppler effect; properties of sound","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Basic wave properties"},{"type":"list","items":["<b>Wavelength (λ)</b> — distance between two consecutive identical points on a wave (e.g., crest to crest)","<b>Frequency (f)</b> — number of wave cycles per second, measured in Hertz (Hz)","<b>Amplitude</b> — the maximum displacement from rest position, related to a wave's energy/intensity"]},{"type":"h","text":"Wave speed equation"},{"type":"p","text":"v = fλ (wave speed = frequency × wavelength)."},{"type":"example","prompt":"A wave has a frequency of 5 Hz and a wavelength of 2 m. Find its speed.","work":"v = fλ = 5 × 2","answer":"10 m/s"},{"type":"h","text":"The Doppler effect"},{"type":"p","text":"The apparent change in frequency (pitch, for sound) of a wave due to relative motion between the source and observer. As a source moves TOWARD an observer, frequency appears higher (waves compressed); as it moves AWAY, frequency appears lower (waves stretched)."},{"type":"example","prompt":"An ambulance siren sounds higher-pitched as it approaches you, then lower-pitched as it passes and moves away. What phenomenon is this?","work":"","answer":"The Doppler effect"},{"type":"tip","text":"Sound is a longitudinal wave (particles vibrate parallel to the direction of travel), while light and water waves are transverse (particles vibrate perpendicular to the direction of travel)."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"A wave has a frequency of 8 Hz and a wavelength of 3 m. Find its speed.","options":["11 m/s","24 m/s","2.7 m/s","5 m/s"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"What happens to the apparent frequency of a sound as its source moves TOWARD a stationary observer?","options":["It appears lower (pitch drops)","It appears higher (pitch rises)","It stays the same","It becomes silent"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"The distance between two consecutive crests of a wave is called its {blank}.","accepted":["wavelength"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which best describes amplitude?","options":["The number of wave cycles per second","The distance between two crests","The maximum displacement from rest position, related to energy","The speed of the wave"],"answer":2},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"A wave travels at 20 m/s with a wavelength of 4 m. Find its frequency.","accepted":["5","5 Hz"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Sound waves traveling through air are classified as which type of wave?","options":["Transverse","Longitudinal","Electromagnetic","Standing only"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"The Doppler effect explains:","options":["Why waves reflect off surfaces","The apparent change in frequency due to relative motion between source and observer","Why sound travels faster than light","The bending of light through a lens"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"sci-phys-optics-em-spectrum","subject":"science","category":"Physics","title":"Reflection, Refraction & the EM Spectrum","competency":"Laws of reflection and refraction, converging/diverging lenses, and the electromagnetic spectrum","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Reflection"},{"type":"p","text":"The law of reflection: the angle of incidence equals the angle of reflection, both measured from the normal (a line perpendicular to the surface)."},{"type":"h","text":"Refraction"},{"type":"p","text":"Refraction is the change in direction when light enters a medium with a different refractive index at an oblique angle. At normal incidence its speed can change without bending."},{"type":"h","text":"Lenses"},{"type":"p","text":"A convex (converging) lens is thicker in the middle and bends light rays inward to a focal point — used to correct farsightedness and in magnifying glasses. A concave (diverging) lens is thinner in the middle and spreads light rays outward — used to correct nearsightedness."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Light passes from air into water and bends toward the normal. Is water optically denser or less dense than air?","work":"Light bends toward the normal when entering a denser medium","answer":"Water is optically denser than air"},{"type":"h","text":"The electromagnetic spectrum"},{"type":"p","text":"Ordered from longest wavelength (lowest energy/frequency) to shortest wavelength (highest energy/frequency): radio waves, microwaves, infrared, visible light, ultraviolet, X-rays, gamma rays."},{"type":"tip","text":"A helpful mnemonic for the EM spectrum, from longest to shortest wavelength: 'Raging Martians Invaded Venus Using X-ray Guns' (Radio, Microwave, Infrared, Visible, Ultraviolet, X-ray, Gamma)."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"According to the law of reflection, the angle of incidence:","options":["Is always 90°","Equals the angle of reflection","Is always greater than the angle of reflection","Has no relationship to the angle of reflection"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which type of lens is used to correct nearsightedness?","options":["Convex (converging) lens","Concave (diverging) lens","Flat lens","No lens is used"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Ordering the EM spectrum from longest to shortest wavelength, which type of wave has the LONGEST wavelength? {blank}","accepted":["radio waves","radio"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why does a light ray bend when it enters a medium of different refractive index at an oblique angle?","options":["Its frequency changes at the boundary.","Its speed changes as it crosses the boundary.","It has no wavelength in the new medium.","It always reflects back into the first medium."],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Which type of electromagnetic wave has the SHORTEST wavelength (highest energy)?","accepted":["gamma rays","gamma"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"A convex (converging) lens is:","options":["Thinner in the middle than at the edges","Thicker in the middle than at the edges","Always made of a diverging material","Only used for nearsightedness"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which type of electromagnetic wave has a longer wavelength than visible light but shorter than microwaves?","options":["Ultraviolet","X-rays","Infrared","Gamma rays"],"answer":2}]},{"id":"sci-phys-electricity-circuits","subject":"science","category":"Physics","title":"Electricity, Circuits & Fields","competency":"Ohm's law, series/parallel circuits, and electric/magnetic fields","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Ohm's law"},{"type":"p","text":"V = IR (voltage = current × resistance). Voltage is measured in volts (V), current in amperes (A), resistance in ohms (Ω)."},{"type":"example","prompt":"A circuit has a resistance of 5 Ω and a current of 2 A. Find the voltage.","work":"V = IR = 5 × 2","answer":"10 V"},{"type":"h","text":"Series vs. parallel circuits"},{"type":"p","text":"In a series circuit, components are connected in a single loop — the same current flows through all of them, and if one breaks, the whole circuit stops. In a parallel circuit, components are connected in separate branches — voltage is the same across each branch, and if one branch breaks, current still flows through the others."},{"type":"h","text":"Electric and magnetic fields"},{"type":"p","text":"An electric field surrounds any charged object, exerting a force on other charges (like charges repel, opposite charges attract). A magnetic field surrounds a magnet or a moving electric charge; a changing magnetic field can also induce an electric current (electromagnetic induction)."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Why do Christmas lights on an older series circuit all go out when just one bulb burns out?","work":"In a series circuit, current has only one path — breaking that path anywhere stops current everywhere","answer":"Because all the bulbs share a single current path — breaking it anywhere stops the whole circuit"},{"type":"tip","text":"A moving electric charge always creates a magnetic field, and a changing magnetic field always creates an electric field — this deep connection between electricity and magnetism is why we call it 'electromagnetism.'"}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"A circuit has a resistance of 4 Ω and a current of 3 A. Find the voltage.","options":["0.75 V","7 V","12 V","1.3 V"],"answer":2},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"In a series circuit, if one bulb burns out:","options":["Only that bulb goes out; others stay lit","The entire circuit stops working","The voltage doubles","Nothing changes"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Ohm's law is expressed as V = I × {blank}","accepted":["R","r","resistance"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"In a parallel circuit, if one branch breaks:","options":["The whole circuit stops","Current still flows through the other branches","The voltage becomes zero everywhere","The resistance becomes infinite everywhere"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"A circuit has a voltage of 12 V and a resistance of 3 Ω. Find the current (using Ohm's law).","accepted":["4","4 A"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"What surrounds a moving electric charge or an electric current?","options":["An electric field only","A magnetic field","No field at all","Only gravity"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Like electric charges (both positive or both negative):","options":["Attract each other","Repel each other","Have no effect on each other","Cancel each other out completely"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"sci-phys-thermodynamics","subject":"science","category":"Physics","title":"Heat, Temperature & Thermodynamics","competency":"Distinguishing heat from temperature; the laws of thermodynamics","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Heat vs. temperature"},{"type":"p","text":"For a classical ideal gas, absolute temperature is proportional to average translational kinetic energy per particle. Heat is energy transferred because of a temperature difference. Internal energy depends on the quantity and state of the material."},{"type":"h","text":"The laws of thermodynamics"},{"type":"list","items":["<b>Zeroth law</b> — if two objects are each in thermal equilibrium with a third object, they are in thermal equilibrium with each other","<b>First law</b> — energy cannot be created or destroyed, only converted between forms","<b>Second law</b> — heat flows spontaneously from hot to cold. The total entropy of an isolated system cannot decrease.","<b>Third law</b> — the entropy of a perfect crystal approaches zero as its temperature approaches absolute zero."]},{"type":"example","prompt":"A cup of hot coffee is left on a table and gradually cools to room temperature. Which law of thermodynamics explains why heat flows FROM the coffee TO the room, and not the reverse?","work":"","answer":"The Second Law of Thermodynamics"},{"type":"tip","text":"Temperature compares how hot samples are. To compare changes in internal energy when heating the same material, consider both the temperature change and the amount of material."},{"type":"tip","text":"Heat is energy transferred because of a temperature difference. Internal energy is a property of a system. For an isolated system, total entropy increases in irreversible processes and stays constant in reversible ones."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"For a classical ideal gas, what does absolute temperature measure?","options":["It equals the total mass of the gas.","It is proportional to the particles’ average translational kinetic energy.","It measures the gas’s total volume.","It is the same as heat transferred."],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which law of thermodynamics states that heat naturally flows from hot to cold, never spontaneously the reverse?","options":["Zeroth law","First law","Second law","Third law"],"answer":2},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"The law of thermodynamics stating that energy cannot be created or destroyed, only converted between forms, is the {blank} law.","accepted":["first"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why is temperature alone insufficient to compare the internal energies of two samples?","options":["Only the sample temperature matters.","Internal energy is extensive and depends on the amount and state of the material.","Heat is a substance stored in a sample.","Every sample of water has the same internal energy."],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"In an isolated system undergoing a spontaneous irreversible process, does total entropy increase or decrease?","accepted":["increase","increases"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"The Zeroth law of thermodynamics establishes the basis for:","options":["Measuring temperature (thermal equilibrium)","Measuring pressure","The speed of light","Newton's laws of motion"],"answer":0},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"As temperature approaches absolute zero, what happens to entropy, according to the Third Law?","options":["It increases without limit","It approaches a minimum","It becomes infinite","It stays constant"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"sci-phys-modern-physics","subject":"science","category":"Physics","title":"Modern Physics Basics","competency":"Introductory concepts in relativity, quantum physics, and atomic/nuclear physics","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Special relativity basics"},{"type":"p","text":"Einstein's theory of special relativity established that the speed of light in a vacuum (c ≈ 3×10⁸ m/s) is constant for all inertial observers. This leads to effects like time dilation (time passes more slowly for an object moving at very high speed, relative to a stationary observer) and mass-energy equivalence, expressed by the famous equation E = mc²."},{"type":"h","text":"Quantum physics basics"},{"type":"p","text":"Quantum physics describes matter and energy at atomic and subatomic scales. Bound systems such as isolated atoms can have discrete energy levels. Particles such as electrons can show both wave-like and particle-like behavior."},{"type":"h","text":"Nuclear physics: fission and fusion"},{"type":"p","text":"Nuclear fission splits a large, heavy nucleus into smaller nuclei, releasing energy (used in nuclear power plants). Nuclear fusion combines small, light nuclei into a larger nucleus, releasing energy when the products have lower total rest mass (the process that powers the Sun and other stars)."},{"type":"example","prompt":"What is the key difference between nuclear fission and nuclear fusion?","work":"","answer":"Fission splits a heavy nucleus into smaller ones; fusion combines light nuclei into a heavier one — both release energy, but fusion releases more energy per reaction"},{"type":"tip","text":"E = mc² shows that mass and energy are two forms of the same thing — a tiny amount of mass can convert into an enormous amount of energy, because it's multiplied by the speed of light SQUARED, an enormous number."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"According to special relativity, what speed is the same for all inertial observers?","options":["Time","Mass","The speed of light in a vacuum","Distance"],"answer":2},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"What does E = mc² express?","options":["The relationship between force and acceleration","The equivalence of mass and energy","The speed of sound","The law of conservation of momentum"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"The process that combines small, light nuclei into a larger nucleus, releasing energy (and powers the Sun), is called nuclear {blank}.","accepted":["fusion"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"The process used in nuclear power plants, which SPLITS a heavy nucleus into smaller ones, is called:","options":["Fusion","Fission","Radiation","Ionization"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Are the bound-state electron energy levels of an isolated atom continuous or discrete?","accepted":["discrete","quantized"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"'Wave-particle duality' refers to the observation that:","options":["Light always behaves only as a wave","Particles like electrons can show both particle-like and wave-like behavior","Only large objects show wave behavior","Waves and particles are unrelated"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Time dilation, predicted by special relativity, means that for an object moving at very high speed (relative to a stationary observer):","options":["Time passes faster for the moving object","Time passes more slowly for the moving object","Time is unaffected by speed","Time stops completely at any speed"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"sci-earth-plate-tectonics","subject":"science","category":"Earth & Space Science","title":"Plate Tectonics & Boundary Types","competency":"The theory of plate tectonics and the three types of plate boundaries","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Plate tectonics"},{"type":"p","text":"Earth's lithosphere (crust + uppermost mantle) is broken into large, rigid plates that float and slowly move on the more fluid asthenosphere below, driven largely by convection currents in the mantle."},{"type":"h","text":"Three types of plate boundaries"},{"type":"list","items":["<b>Divergent boundary</b> — plates move APART, creating new crust (e.g., mid-ocean ridges, rift valleys)","<b>Convergent boundary</b> — plates move TOWARD each other, one may subduct beneath the other (forming volcanoes, deep ocean trenches) or they may collide (forming mountain ranges)","<b>Transform boundary</b> — plates slide PAST each other horizontally, causing frequent earthquakes (e.g., the San Andreas Fault)"]},{"type":"example","prompt":"Two oceanic plates converge, and one subducts beneath the other. What geological feature commonly forms as a result?","work":"Subduction of oceanic crust typically triggers volcanic activity and forms a deep trench","answer":"A volcanic island arc and a deep ocean trench"},{"type":"tip","text":"The Philippines sits at the boundary of several tectonic plates (including the Philippine Sea Plate), which is precisely why the country experiences frequent earthquakes and has many active volcanoes — it's part of the Pacific Ring of Fire."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"At a divergent boundary, plates:","options":["Move toward each other","Move apart from each other","Slide past each other","Do not move at all"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which type of boundary is responsible for the frequent earthquakes along California's San Andreas Fault?","options":["Divergent","Convergent","Transform","No boundary type"],"answer":2},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"At a convergent boundary between two oceanic plates, one plate often sinks beneath the other in a process called {blank}.","accepted":["subduction"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Where does new oceanic crust typically form?","options":["At convergent boundaries","At transform boundaries","At divergent boundaries (like mid-ocean ridges)","Only at the center of continents"],"answer":2},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"What layer of the Earth do tectonic plates move on top of?","accepted":["asthenosphere"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"When two continental plates converge and collide, what geological feature commonly forms?","options":["A mid-ocean ridge","A mountain range","A rift valley","A transform fault"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why does the Philippines experience frequent earthquakes and volcanic activity?","options":["It has no tectonic activity","It sits along the boundary of several tectonic plates, part of the Pacific Ring of Fire","It is far from any plate boundaries","It only has transform boundaries nearby"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"sci-earth-rock-cycle","subject":"science","category":"Earth & Space Science","title":"The Rock Cycle","competency":"The three rock types and the processes that transform one into another","estLesson":8,"estPractice":10,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Three rock types"},{"type":"list","items":["<b>Igneous rock</b> — forms when molten rock (magma or lava) cools and solidifies (e.g., granite, basalt)","<b>Sedimentary rock</b> — forms when sediment is compacted and cemented together over time (e.g., sandstone, limestone)","<b>Metamorphic rock</b> — forms when existing rock is transformed by heat and pressure, without melting completely (e.g., marble from limestone, slate from shale)"]},{"type":"h","text":"The rock cycle processes"},{"type":"p","text":"Rocks continuously transform between types: melting (any rock → magma → igneous rock upon cooling); weathering/erosion/deposition (any rock → sediment → sedimentary rock upon compaction/cementation); heat and pressure (any rock → metamorphic rock, without fully melting)."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Limestone (a sedimentary rock) is subjected to intense heat and pressure deep underground, but does not melt. What type of rock does it become?","work":"Heat and pressure without melting transforms existing rock into metamorphic rock","answer":"Marble (a metamorphic rock)"},{"type":"tip","text":"The rock cycle has no fixed starting or ending point — any rock type can eventually become any other rock type, depending on which processes it's exposed to next."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which type of rock forms when molten rock cools and solidifies?","options":["Sedimentary","Metamorphic","Igneous","Organic"],"answer":2},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Sandstone is an example of which rock type?","options":["Igneous","Sedimentary","Metamorphic","None of these"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"At low metamorphic grade, shale commonly becomes {blank}.","accepted":["slate"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"What process forms sedimentary rock from loose sediment?","options":["Melting and cooling","Compaction and cementation","Extreme heat without pressure","Radioactive decay"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"What is the metamorphic rock formed from limestone under heat and pressure?","accepted":["marble"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which of the following best describes metamorphic rock formation?","options":["Rock melts completely and re-solidifies","Rock is transformed by heat and pressure without fully melting","Rock forms only from sediment","Rock forms only in volcanoes"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"According to the rock cycle, can a metamorphic rock eventually become an igneous rock?","options":["No, rock types are permanent","Yes, if it melts and then cools/solidifies","Only sedimentary rocks can change type","Only if it turns into sediment first"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"sci-earth-layers","subject":"science","category":"Earth & Space Science","title":"Earth's Layers","competency":"The structure and characteristics of Earth's crust, mantle, and core","estLesson":8,"estPractice":10,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Earth's four main layers"},{"type":"list","items":["<b>Crust</b> — the thin, outermost solid layer (oceanic crust is thinner/denser; continental crust is thicker/less dense)","<b>Mantle</b> — a thick layer of mostly solid rock that flows slowly over long timescales, driving plate tectonics","<b>Outer core</b> — liquid layer made mostly of iron and nickel; its movement generates Earth's magnetic field","<b>Inner core</b> — solid layer made mostly of iron and nickel, solid despite extreme temperature due to immense pressure"]},{"type":"example","prompt":"Why is the inner core solid, while the outer core is liquid, even though the inner core is HOTTER?","work":"The immense pressure at Earth's center forces the iron-nickel material into a solid state, despite the higher temperature","answer":"The extreme pressure at Earth's center keeps the inner core solid, despite its higher temperature"},{"type":"tip","text":"Earth's magnetic field is generated by the movement of liquid iron and nickel in the OUTER core (not the solid inner core) — this process is called the geodynamo."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which of Earth's layers is liquid and generates Earth's magnetic field?","options":["Crust","Mantle","Outer core","Inner core"],"answer":2},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which layer is the thin, outermost solid layer of the Earth?","options":["Crust","Mantle","Outer core","Inner core"],"answer":0},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Earth's {blank} core is solid due to extreme pressure, despite being the hottest layer.","accepted":["inner"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which layer of the Earth is mostly solid rock that flows very slowly, driving plate tectonics via convection?","options":["Crust","Mantle","Outer core","Inner core"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"What two elements make up most of Earth's core (both inner and outer)?","accepted":["iron and nickel","iron, nickel"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which type of crust is thicker but less dense: oceanic or continental?","options":["Oceanic","Continental","They are equal","Neither has a defined density"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"What process, driven by the outer core's liquid metal movement, generates Earth's magnetic field?","options":["Plate tectonics","The geodynamo","The rock cycle","Radioactive decay in the crust"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"sci-earth-atmosphere-typhoons","subject":"science","category":"Earth & Space Science","title":"Atmospheric Structure & Typhoon Formation","competency":"Layers of the atmosphere; how weather forms; the conditions needed for typhoon formation","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Layers of the atmosphere"},{"type":"list","items":["<b>Troposphere</b> — lowest layer, where nearly all weather occurs, temperature decreases with altitude","<b>Stratosphere</b> — contains the ozone layer, temperature increases with altitude","<b>Mesosphere</b> — coldest layer, where most meteors burn up","<b>Thermosphere</b> — outermost layer, includes the ionosphere, where auroras occur"]},{"type":"h","text":"How typhoons form"},{"type":"p","text":"Typhoons form over warm ocean water (at least ~26.5°C) when warm, moist air rises rapidly, creating an area of low pressure. Surrounding air rushes in, is warmed and humidified by the ocean, and rises too, creating a self-sustaining cycle. The Coriolis effect (caused by Earth's rotation) makes the system spin — counterclockwise in the Northern Hemisphere (where the Philippines is located)."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Why don't typhoons typically form directly over cold ocean water?","work":"Typhoons need warm ocean water to supply the heat and moisture that fuels rising, humid air","answer":"Cold water doesn't provide enough heat/moisture to fuel the rising warm, humid air that drives typhoon formation"},{"type":"tip","text":"The Philippines lies directly in the path of the Pacific typhoon belt, making it one of the most typhoon-exposed countries in the world — typically experiencing around 20 typhoons per year, several of which make landfall."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"In which layer of the atmosphere does nearly all weather occur?","options":["Troposphere","Stratosphere","Mesosphere","Thermosphere"],"answer":0},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which layer of the atmosphere contains the ozone layer?","options":["Troposphere","Stratosphere","Mesosphere","Thermosphere"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Typhoons typically require ocean water temperatures of at least approximately {blank}°C to form.","accepted":["26.5","26","27"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"What effect, caused by Earth's rotation, makes typhoons spin?","options":["The greenhouse effect","The Coriolis effect","The albedo effect","The Doppler effect"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"In which hemisphere is the Philippines located, and which direction do typhoons spin there?","accepted":["northern hemisphere, counterclockwise","northern, counterclockwise","northern hemisphere and counterclockwise","northern hemisphere, anticlockwise","northern hemisphere and anticlockwise"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"In which layer of the atmosphere do most meteors burn up?","options":["Troposphere","Stratosphere","Mesosphere","Thermosphere"],"answer":2},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"What provides the primary energy source that fuels a typhoon?","options":["Cold air masses","Warm, moist air rising from warm ocean water","The ozone layer","Volcanic activity"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"sci-earth-water-cycle-currents","subject":"science","category":"Earth & Space Science","title":"Water Cycle & Ocean Currents","competency":"The stages of the water cycle; surface and deep ocean currents","estLesson":8,"estPractice":10,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"The water cycle"},{"type":"list","items":["<b>Evaporation</b> — liquid water turns to vapor (from oceans, lakes)","<b>Transpiration</b> — water vapor released by plants","<b>Condensation</b> — water vapor cools and forms clouds","<b>Precipitation</b> — water falls back to Earth as rain, snow, etc.","<b>Collection/runoff</b> — water gathers in bodies of water or infiltrates the ground"]},{"type":"h","text":"Ocean currents"},{"type":"p","text":"Surface currents are driven mainly by wind patterns and the Coriolis effect. Deep ocean currents are driven by differences in water density (caused by temperature and salinity) — thermohaline circulation. Colder, saltier water is denser and sinks, driving a slow, global deep-ocean 'conveyor belt.'"},{"type":"example","prompt":"Why does cold, salty water tend to sink in the ocean, driving deep currents?","work":"Both lower temperature and higher salt content increase water's density","answer":"Cold, salty water is denser than warmer, less salty water, so it sinks, driving thermohaline circulation"},{"type":"tip","text":"The water cycle has no true starting point, but for explaining it, most people start with evaporation, since it's the step that lifts water from the surface into the atmosphere to begin the visible cycle again."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the process by which liquid water turns into water vapor called?","options":["Condensation","Evaporation","Precipitation","Transpiration"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the release of water vapor by plants called?","options":["Evaporation","Condensation","Transpiration","Runoff"],"answer":2},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"Water vapor cooling and forming clouds is called {blank}.","accepted":["condensation"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"What primarily drives surface ocean currents?","options":["Water density differences alone","Wind patterns and the Coriolis effect","Volcanic activity","Tides alone"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"What two factors determine water density in thermohaline circulation (deep ocean currents)?","accepted":["temperature and salinity","temperature, salinity"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Precipitation refers to:","options":["Water vapor rising into the atmosphere","Water falling back to Earth as rain, snow, etc.","Water evaporating from the ocean","Water flowing underground only"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why does cold, salty water sink in the ocean?","options":["It is warmer than surrounding water","It is less dense than surrounding water","It is denser than surrounding water","Salt makes water float"],"answer":2}]},{"id":"sci-earth-solar-system","subject":"science","category":"Earth & Space Science","title":"Solar System Structure","competency":"The order and general characteristics of the planets; other solar system bodies","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"The planets, in order from the Sun"},{"type":"p","text":"Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune. The first four are terrestrial (rocky, dense, small); the outer four are gas/ice giants (large, low density, mostly hydrogen/helium or icy compounds)."},{"type":"h","text":"The asteroid belt and beyond"},{"type":"p","text":"The asteroid belt lies between Mars and Jupiter, containing many small rocky bodies. Beyond Neptune lies the Kuiper Belt, home to dwarf planets like Pluto, made largely of icy bodies."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Why are Mercury, Venus, Earth, and Mars called 'terrestrial' planets, while Jupiter and Saturn are not?","work":"Terrestrial planets are small, rocky, and dense, unlike the much larger, gas-dominated outer planets","answer":"Because they are small, dense, and rocky, unlike the large, low-density gas giants"},{"type":"tip","text":"A common mnemonic for planet order is 'My Very Educated Mother Just Served Us Noodles' (Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune) — this drops the old 'Pluto' ending, since Pluto was reclassified as a dwarf planet in 2006."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which planet is closest to the Sun?","options":["Venus","Earth","Mercury","Mars"],"answer":2},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which of the following is classified as a 'gas giant'?","options":["Mars","Jupiter","Mercury","Earth"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"The asteroid belt is located between the orbits of Mars and {blank}.","accepted":["Jupiter"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which planet is the largest in the solar system?","options":["Saturn","Neptune","Jupiter","Uranus"],"answer":2},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Is Pluto currently classified as a full planet or a dwarf planet?","accepted":["dwarf planet","dwarf"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"What are the terrestrial planets generally characterized by?","options":["Large size and low density","Small size, rocky composition, and high density","Rings and many moons","Being made entirely of gas"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Where is the Kuiper Belt located?","options":["Between Mars and Jupiter","Beyond Neptune","Between Earth and Mars","Inside Mercury's orbit"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"sci-earth-moon-phases-eclipses","subject":"science","category":"Earth & Space Science","title":"Moon Phases & Eclipses","competency":"Why the Moon has phases; the difference between solar and lunar eclipses","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Why the Moon has phases"},{"type":"p","text":"The Moon doesn't produce its own light — we see reflected sunlight. As the Moon orbits Earth (about every 29.5 days), the portion of its sunlit side visible from Earth changes, creating the cycle of phases: New Moon → Waxing Crescent → First Quarter → Waxing Gibbous → Full Moon → Waning Gibbous → Last Quarter → Waning Crescent → back to New Moon."},{"type":"h","text":"Solar eclipses"},{"type":"p","text":"Occurs when the Moon passes directly between the Sun and Earth, blocking the Sun's light from reaching a small area on Earth. Can only happen during a New Moon."},{"type":"h","text":"Lunar eclipses"},{"type":"p","text":"Occurs when Earth passes directly between the Sun and the Moon, and Earth's shadow falls on the Moon. Can only happen during a Full Moon."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Why don't solar and lunar eclipses happen every single month, even though the Moon completes a full New-to-Full cycle every month?","work":"The Moon's orbit is tilted relative to Earth's orbit around the Sun, so the three bodies rarely align perfectly","answer":"Because the Moon's orbital plane is tilted relative to Earth's orbit, so the Sun, Earth, and Moon don't align perfectly most months"},{"type":"tip","text":"Remember which eclipse happens at which moon phase by thinking about shadows: a solar eclipse needs the Moon to block the Sun (New Moon), while a lunar eclipse needs Earth's shadow to fall on the Moon (Full Moon)."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"A solar eclipse can only occur during which moon phase?","options":["Full Moon","New Moon","First Quarter","Last Quarter"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"A lunar eclipse can only occur during which moon phase?","options":["Full Moon","New Moon","First Quarter","Last Quarter"],"answer":0},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"The Moon's full cycle of phases takes approximately {blank} days.","accepted":["29.5","29","30"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why does the Moon appear to change shape (phases) throughout the month?","options":["The Moon's size actually changes","The Moon produces its own changing light","We see different portions of the Moon's sunlit side as it orbits Earth","Earth's shadow covers different parts of the Moon"],"answer":2},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"During a solar eclipse, which celestial body blocks the Sun's light from reaching Earth?","accepted":["the Moon","moon"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why don't eclipses happen every single month?","options":["The Moon stops orbiting sometimes","The Moon's orbital plane is tilted relative to Earth's orbit around the Sun","The Sun moves out of alignment","Eclipses actually do happen every month"],"answer":1},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"During a lunar eclipse, whose shadow falls on the Moon?","options":["The Sun's shadow","Earth's shadow","The Moon's own shadow","No shadow is involved"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"sci-earth-stellar-lifecycle","subject":"science","category":"Earth & Space Science","title":"Stellar Classification & Lifecycle","competency":"Classifying stars by spectral type/color; the general stages of a star's life cycle","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Stellar classification"},{"type":"p","text":"Stars are classified by spectral type (related to surface temperature and color), from hottest to coolest: O, B, A, F, G, K, M ('Oh Be A Fine Girl/Guy, Kiss Me'). Blue/white stars are the hottest; red stars are the coolest. Our Sun is a G-type star (yellow, medium temperature)."},{"type":"h","text":"The general stellar life cycle"},{"type":"p","text":"Stars form from collapsing clouds of gas and dust (nebulae). A star spends most of its life in the 'main sequence' stage, fusing hydrogen into helium in its core. Low/medium-mass stars (like our Sun) expand into a red giant, then shed their outer layers, leaving behind a white dwarf. High-mass stars expand into a red supergiant, then explode in a supernova, leaving behind a neutron star or, for the most massive stars, a black hole."},{"type":"example","prompt":"Why does a star's fate after the main-sequence stage depend on its mass?","work":"","answer":"Because mass determines the internal pressure and temperature reachable in the core, which determines the star's later fusion stages and how it ultimately collapses or explodes"},{"type":"tip","text":"LOW-mass stars end 'quietly' (red giant → white dwarf), while HIGH-mass stars end 'violently' (supergiant → supernova → neutron star or black hole) — bigger stars burn through fuel faster and have more dramatic, shorter lives, despite starting with more material."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which spectral type of star is the HOTTEST?","options":["M","G","O","K"],"answer":2},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"What spectral type is our Sun?","options":["O","B","G","M"],"answer":2},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"A star spends most of its life fusing hydrogen into helium during the {blank} sequence stage.","accepted":["main"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"What happens to a low/medium-mass star (like the Sun) after it leaves the main sequence?","options":["It immediately becomes a black hole","It expands into a red giant, then becomes a white dwarf","It explodes in a supernova immediately","It stays the same forever"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which compact remnants can form after the core collapse of a massive star?","accepted":["black hole","neutron star"],"options":["A neutron star or a black hole","Only a white dwarf","A rocky planet","A main-sequence star"],"answer":0},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which color of star is generally the COOLEST?","options":["Blue","White","Yellow","Red"],"answer":3},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Stars form from collapsing clouds of gas and dust called:","options":["Nebulae","Supernovae","Asteroids","Comets"],"answer":0}]},{"id":"sci-earth-philippines-ring-of-fire","subject":"science","category":"Earth & Space Science","title":"Philippine Geographic Features & the Ring of Fire","competency":"The Pacific Ring of Fire and its relevance to Philippine geography, volcanoes, and earthquakes","estLesson":9,"estPractice":11,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"The Pacific Ring of Fire"},{"type":"p","text":"A horseshoe-shaped zone around the edges of the Pacific Ocean, home to about 75% of the world's active and dormant volcanoes and roughly 90% of the world's earthquakes. It follows the boundaries of the Pacific Plate with several surrounding plates."},{"type":"h","text":"The Philippines' position"},{"type":"p","text":"The Philippines sits within the Ring of Fire, at the boundary of the Philippine Sea Plate, the Eurasian Plate, and other smaller plates/microplates. This makes the country highly prone to both volcanic activity and earthquakes."},{"type":"h","text":"Key Philippine geographic features related to this activity"},{"type":"list","items":["<b>Mayon Volcano</b> (Albay) — known for its famously symmetrical cone shape, one of the most active volcanoes in the country","<b>Taal Volcano</b> (Batangas) — a volcano-within-a-lake, one of the world's smallest active volcanoes but capable of powerful eruptions","<b>Mount Pinatubo</b> (Zambales) — dormant for centuries before its massive 1991 eruption, one of the largest volcanic eruptions of the 20th century","<b>The Philippine Trench</b> — a deep oceanic trench east of the Philippines, formed by the subduction of the Philippine Sea Plate"]},{"type":"example","prompt":"Why does the Philippines experience both frequent earthquakes AND numerous active volcanoes?","work":"Its location along multiple converging/subducting tectonic plate boundaries within the Ring of Fire causes both phenomena","answer":"Because it sits along the boundaries of several tectonic plates within the Pacific Ring of Fire, where subduction and plate movement trigger both volcanic activity and earthquakes"},{"type":"tip","text":"The Philippine Trench, formed by the subduction of the Philippine Sea Plate, is one of the deepest points in the world's oceans — a direct geographic result of the same plate activity that also produces the country's volcanoes and earthquakes."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Approximately what percentage of the world's active volcanoes are found along the Pacific Ring of Fire?","options":["25%","50%","75%","100%"],"answer":2},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which Philippine volcano erupted massively in 1991, after centuries of dormancy?","options":["Mayon","Taal","Mount Pinatubo","Mount Apo"],"answer":2},{"id":"q3","type":"fillblank","prompt":"The volcano in Batangas known for being located within a lake is {blank} Volcano.","accepted":["Taal"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"The Philippine Trench was formed primarily by which process?","options":["Volcanic eruption","Subduction of the Philippine Sea Plate","River erosion","Coral reef formation"],"answer":1},{"id":"q5","type":"short","prompt":"Which Albay volcano is known for its symmetrical, cone-shaped appearance?","accepted":["Mayon","Mount Mayon"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Roughly what percentage of the world's earthquakes occur along the Pacific Ring of Fire?","options":["50%","70%","90%","100%"],"answer":2},{"id":"q7","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why is the Philippines particularly prone to both earthquakes and volcanic eruptions?","options":["It has no tectonic plate boundaries nearby","It sits at the boundary of the Philippine Sea Plate, the Eurasian Plate, and other plates within the Ring of Fire","It is located far from the Pacific Ocean","It has unusually low seismic activity"],"answer":1}]},{"id":"sci-general-1","subject":"science","category":"General Review (mixed)","title":"General Science Review","competency":"Biology, chemistry & physics fundamentals · scientific reasoning","estLesson":12,"estPractice":15,"lesson":[{"type":"h","text":"Biology essentials"},{"type":"list","items":["<b>Nucleus</b> — stores the cell's genetic material (DNA) and controls its activities","<b>Mitochondria</b> — produce the cell's usable energy; often called its “powerhouse”","<b>Ribosome</b> — builds proteins from amino acids","<b>Cell membrane</b> — controls what enters and leaves the cell","<b>Chloroplast</b> — found only in plant cells; site of photosynthesis"]},{"type":"p","text":"Photosynthesis is the process by which plants convert light energy into chemical energy (glucose), using carbon dioxide and water and releasing oxygen. Respiration is roughly the reverse: cells break down glucose using oxygen to release energy, producing carbon dioxide and water."},{"type":"h","text":"Chemistry essentials"},{"type":"list","items":["<b>Na</b> — sodium &nbsp; <b>K</b> — potassium &nbsp; <b>Fe</b> — iron","<b>Au</b> — gold &nbsp; <b>Ag</b> — silver &nbsp; <b>Cu</b> — copper"]},{"type":"p","text":"An exothermic reaction releases heat to its surroundings (the container feels warmer); an endothermic reaction absorbs heat from its surroundings (the container feels cooler). pH is measured on a 0–14 scale: below 7 is acidic, above 7 is basic, and exactly 7 is neutral."},{"type":"h","text":"Physics essentials"},{"type":"list","items":["<b>First Law (inertia)</b> — an object at rest stays at rest, and an object in motion stays in motion, unless acted on by a net outside force","<b>Second Law</b> — force equals mass times acceleration (F = ma)","<b>Third Law</b> — for every action there is an equal and opposite reaction"]},{"type":"p","text":"For an object in free fall (ignoring air resistance), velocity increases at a constant rate — this constant rate is the acceleration due to gravity, about 9.8 m/s² near Earth's surface."},{"type":"tip","text":"General-science sections reward breadth over depth. Skim a one-page summary of each branch — biology, chemistry, physics, earth science — rather than deep-diving into just one."}],"questions":[{"id":"q1","type":"mcq","prompt":"In a typical aerobic animal cell, which organelle makes most of the ATP?","options":["Nucleus","Mitochondria","Ribosome","Golgi apparatus"],"answer":1},{"id":"q2","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the chemical symbol for sodium?","options":["So","Na","S","Sd"],"answer":1},{"id":"q3","type":"mcq","prompt":"A ball is dropped from rest near Earth’s surface. Ignoring air resistance and treating gravity as constant, what increases at a constant rate?","options":["Distance fallen","Acceleration magnitude","Speed","Mass"],"answer":2},{"id":"q4","type":"fillblank","prompt":"A water molecule is composed of two hydrogen atoms and {blank} oxygen atom.","accepted":["one","1"]},{"id":"q5","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which best describes an exothermic reaction?","options":["It absorbs heat from its surroundings","It releases heat to its surroundings","It requires a catalyst","It only occurs in living cells"],"answer":1},{"id":"q6","type":"mcq","prompt":"The process by which plants convert light energy into chemical energy is called:","options":["Respiration","Fermentation","Photosynthesis","Transpiration"],"answer":2},{"id":"q7","type":"reflect","prompt":"State Newton's First Law of Motion in your own words."},{"id":"q8","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the pH of a neutral solution at 25°C?","options":["0","7","10","14"],"answer":1}]}];
